hadiths[779]{hadithnumber,text}:
"12610","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that one who fails toward elders, children, and scholars stands outside his community. > **""He is not of my ummah who does not honor our elder, show mercy to our young, and recognize the right of our scholar.""**"
"12611","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), raised it to the Prophet ﷺ, who excluded from his people those who neglect reverence, mercy, and moral duty, with an added clause about recognizing due right. > **""He is not of us who does not revere the elder, show mercy to the young, enjoin what is right, forbid what is wrong, and recognize our due right.""**"
"12612","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Prophet ﷺ excluded from his people those who fail in reverence and mercy, with brotherhood and visiting mentioned in an addition. > **""He is not of us who does not revere our elder, show mercy to our young, establish brotherhood for our sake, and visit one another.""**"
"12613","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ denied membership to one who withholds honor from elders and mercy from children. > **""He is not of us who does not revere our elder and show mercy to our young.""**"
"12614","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed mercy to the young before honor to the elder as a mark of belonging. > **""He is not of us who does not show mercy to our young and honor our elder.""**"
"12615","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported from the Prophet ﷺ a concise pairing of honoring elders with mercy to the young. > **""He is not of us who does not honor our elder and show mercy to our young.""**"
"12616","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that the Messenger ﷺ sat with Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, and others, may Allah be pleased with them (رضي الله عنهم), when a cup of drink arrived. He offered it to Abū ʿUbaydah, who deferred to him, but he insisted, and before drinking Abū ʿUbaydah offered it back, so the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to drink and linked blessing to elders. > **""Drink, for blessing is with our elders. Whoever does not show mercy to our young nor honor our elder is not of us.""**"
"12617","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Prophet ﷺ gave precedence to the elder with a doubled command. > **""The elder, the elder.""**"
"12618","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ attached goodness and, in a variant, blessing to the elders. > **""Goodness is with your elders, and blessing is with your elders.""**"
"12619","Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bajalī, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), came to the Prophet ﷺ in a house crowded with people and stood at the door. Finding no seat, the Prophet ﷺ folded his cloak, cast it to him, and told him to sit. Jarīr embraced it, kissed it, returned it, and prayed for him, upon which the Prophet ﷺ stated the rule of honoring notables. > **""Sit upon it. When the noble man of a people comes to you, honor him.""**"
"12620","Jarīr, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that after the mission of the Prophet ﷺ he came to accept Islam at his hands. The Prophet ﷺ asked his purpose, cast his cloak toward him, then addressed his Companions with the command to honor notables. > **""O Jarīr, for what have you come to us. When the noble man of a people comes to you, honor him.""**"
"12621","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), entered a full house and found no seat. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ threw him his waist-wrapper or cloak and told him to sit on it. Jarīr kissed it, held it close, and prayed for him, and the Prophet ﷺ repeated the rule. > **""Sit upon this. When the noble man of a people comes to you, honor him.""**"
"12622","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḍamīrah reported from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ joined mercy to the young, recognition of elders, honesty, and mutual love as conditions of faith and belonging. > **""He is not of us who does not show mercy to our young and recognize the right of our elder, nor is he of us who deceives us. The believer is not a believer until he loves for the believers what he loves for himself.""**"
"12623","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded honoring a visiting notable. > **""When the noble man of a people comes to you, honor him.""**"
"12624","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), narrated that ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn visited the Prophet ﷺ while Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them (رضي الله عنهم), sat on the ground. The Prophet ﷺ called for a cushion, seated him, and stated the rule. > **""When the noble man of a people comes to you, honor him.""**"
"12625","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded honor for a visiting notable. > **""When the noble man of a people comes to you, honor him.""**"
"12626","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded honoring the elder of a visiting people. > **""When the elder of a people comes to you, honor him.""**"
"12627","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ equated honoring a Muslim commander with honoring Allah. > **""Whoever honors a Muslim commander honors Allah thereby.""**"
"12628","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), raised it to the Prophet ﷺ that honoring a brother ascends to the Lord. > **""When a man honors his brother, he honors his Lord thereby.""**"
"12629","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised forgiveness for selfless service in holding a Muslim brother's stirrup without hope or fear. > **""Whoever holds the stirrup of his Muslim brother, seeking nothing from him nor fearing him, Allah forgives him.""**"
"12630","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made courteous forbearance toward people a charity. > **""Courteous forbearance toward people is charity.""**"
"12631","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her (رضي الله عنها), narrated that a man sought permission, and the Messenger ﷺ called him an evil son of his tribe, yet received him cheerfully. Another was praised as a good son of his tribe, yet received without such warmth. When she questioned the contrast, he explained the treatment of those feared for obscenity. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, among the worst of people are those guarded against on account of their obscenity.""**"
"12632","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that a man approached the Prophet ﷺ while others spoke ill of him, yet the Prophet ﷺ welcomed him. After he departed, he described the rank of those feared for their evil. > **""The worst of people in station with Allah on the Day of Resurrection are those whom people fear on account of their evil.""**"
"12633","Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that they sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a Qurashī approached, whom he drew near. After the man rose and left, he questioned Buraydah about him. Buraydah described him as middling in lineage and foremost in wealth, deferring greater knowledge to the Prophet ﷺ, who then gave his verdict. > **""O Buraydah, do you know him. He is among those for whom Allah shall set no weight on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"12634","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed affectionate conduct toward people directly after faith as the head of intellect. > **""The head of intellect after faith in Allah is affectionate kindness toward people.""**"
"12635","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered a welcome for approaching riders even when resented. > **""Hated riders shall come to you, so when they come to you, welcome them.""**"
"12636","Al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made abandonment of idle matters part of sound Islam. > **""Part of a man's sound Islam is his leaving what does not concern him.""**"
"12637","In a variant narration, the Prophet ﷺ expressed sound Islam as restraint of speech in what does not concern. > **""Part of a man's sound Islam is fewness of speech in what does not concern him.""**"
"12638","Zayd ibn Thābit, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made leaving irrelevant matters a sign of sound Islam. > **""Part of a man's sound Islam is his leaving what does not concern him.""**"
"12639","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah as Gentle, loving gentleness and granting through it what harshness cannot obtain. > **""Allah is Gentle, loves gentleness, and grants for gentleness what He does not grant for harshness.""**"
"12640","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah as Gentle, loving gentleness and granting through it what harshness cannot obtain. > **""Allah is Gentle, loves gentleness, and grants for gentleness what He does not grant for harshness.""**"
"12641","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contrasted beautifying gentleness with disgracing harshness and affirmed divine love for gentleness. > **""Gentleness never enters anything but beautifies it, harshness never enters anything but disgraces it, and Allah is Gentle and loves gentleness.""**"
"12642","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ affirmed that Allah is Gentle, loves gentleness, and rewards it beyond harshness. > **""Allah is Gentle, loves gentleness, and grants for it what He does not grant for harshness.""**"
"12643","Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported from the Prophet ﷺ that Allah rewards gentleness beyond harshness, grants it to whom He loves, and deprives only to deprive. > **""Allah grants for gentleness what He does not grant for harshness. When Allah loves a servant, He grants him gentleness. No household are deprived of gentleness but are deprived indeed.""**"
"12644","Jarīr, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ place increase, blessing, and all good within gentleness. > **""Gentleness brings increase and blessing, and whoever is deprived of gentleness is deprived of good.""**"
"12645","Khālid ibn Maʿdān reported from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, that Allah is Gentle, loves and approves gentleness, and aids through it beyond harshness. > **""Allah is Gentle, loves gentleness and approves it, and aids through it what He does not aid through harshness.""**"
"12646","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), instructed that when the land is fertile travelers should ease their pace and give their mounts their due, for Allah is Gentle and loves gentleness."
"12647","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah as loving gentleness, approving it, and aiding through it beyond harshness. > **""Allah loves gentleness and approves it, and aids through it what He does not aid through harshness.""**"
"12648","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her (رضي الله عنها), narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counseled her to gentleness as the sign of intended household good. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, be gentle, for when Allah intends good for a household, He guides them to the door of gentleness.""**"
"12649","In another narration concerning household good, the Prophet ﷺ described divine entry of gentleness. > **""When Allah intends good for a household, He brings gentleness into them.""**"
"12650","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her (رضي الله عنها), narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave her a difficult black she-camel, black as coal and unbridled. He stroked her, invoked blessing upon her, and ordered gentle riding. In one version she began striking the animal. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, ride and be gentle.""**"
"12651","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Prophet ﷺ linked divine intent of good for a people with entry of gentleness among them. > **""When Allah intends good for a people, He brings gentleness into them.""**"
"12652","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported from the Prophet ﷺ that deliberation, acceptance of excuses, and praise belong to Allah, while haste belongs to Satan. > **""Deliberation is from Allah and haste is from Satan. None accepts excuses more than Allah, and nothing is dearer to Allah than praise.""**"
"12653","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contrasted blessed gentleness with ill-omened harshness. > **""Gentleness is blessing and harshness is misfortune.""**"
"12654","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared gentleness unfailingly beneficial to its household. > **""No household are given gentleness but it benefits them.""**"
"12655","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contrasted deliberate aim with hasty error. > **""Whoever acts deliberately hits the mark or nearly so, and whoever hastens errs or nearly so.""**"
"12656","Umm Sulaym, may Allah be pleased with her (رضي الله عنها), narrated that she accompanied the wives of the Prophet ﷺ while a driver drove their mounts. The Prophet ﷺ addressed the driver Anjashah with the famous command of restraint. > **""O Anjashah, gently with your driving over the glass vessels.""**"
"12657","ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her (رضي الله عنها), narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to supplicate for inward excellence matching outward form. > **""O Allah, as You made my form good, make my character good.""**"
"12658","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ exalted good character among creation. > **""Good character is the greatest creation of Allah.""**"
"12659","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported from the Prophet ﷺ, from Jibrīl, from Allah the Exalted, that the chosen religion is sustained only by generosity and good character and must be honored through them. > **""This is a religion I have chosen for Myself. Nothing suits it but generosity and good character, so honor it through them as long as you accompany it.""**"
"12660","ʿImrān ibn al-Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Allah chose the religion for Himself and ordered its adornment with generosity and good character. > **""Allah has chosen this religion for Himself. Nothing suits your religion but generosity and good character, so adorn it with them.""**"
"12661","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ traced good and bad character to divine apportionment of good and ill. > **""These traits are from Allah. Whomever Allah intends good for, He grants him good character, and whomever He intends ill for, He grants him bad character.""**"
"12662","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed divine revelation to Ibrāhīm commanding beautiful character even toward disbelievers, with shade, drink, and nearness as reward. > **""Allah revealed to Ibrāhīm: O My friend, make your character good even with the disbelievers; you shall enter the station of the righteous. My word has preceded for one of good character that I shall shade him under My Throne, give him drink from the enclosure of My sanctity, and bring him near to My proximity.""**"
"12663","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that combined outward and inward beauty bars the Fire forever. > **""Whose form and character Allah makes good, He shall never feed him to the Fire.""**"
"12664","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ confined guidance to best traits and removal of evil ones to Him alone. > **""None guides to the best of traits and turns away their evil but He.""**"
"12665","Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ contrasted the beloved well-mannered nearest to him with the hateful foul-mannered farthest, naming chatterers and the pompous. > **""The most beloved of you to me and nearest to me in the Hereafter are the best of you in character. The most hateful of you to me and farthest from me in the Hereafter are the worst of you in character: the chatterers, the pompous, and the grandiloquent.""**"
"12666","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), narrated that he heard the Prophet ﷺ repeat a question about the most beloved and nearest in sitting on Resurrection. When they affirmed their wish to know, he answered with character. > **""Shall I tell you of the most beloved of you to me and nearest of you to me in sitting on the Day of Resurrection. The best of you in character.""**"
"12667","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), raised it to the Prophet ﷺ that the best-mannered are most beloved on Resurrection while the grandiloquent and pompous are most hated. Asked about the pompous, a narrator explained them as the arrogant. A second wording announces the best as the well-mannered and gentle-sided. > **""The most beloved of you to me on the Day of Resurrection are the best of you in character. The most hateful of you to me on the Day of Resurrection are the grandiloquent and the pompous. Shall I tell you of the best of you. The best of you in character, the gentle-sided.""**"
"12668","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described his beloved as well-mannered, gentle-sided, loving and loved, and his hated as talebearers, dividers of loved ones, and seekers of faults in the innocent. > **""The most beloved of you to me are the best of you in character, the gentle-sided, who love and are loved. The most hateful of you to me are the talebearers, the dividers between loved ones, the seekers of faults against the innocent.""**"
"12669","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defined completeness of faith through character, gentleness, and mutual affection, excluding the unaffectionate. > **""The most complete of the believers in faith are the best of them in character, the gentle-sided, who love and are loved. He is not of us who neither loves nor is loved.""**"
"12670","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise the upright Muslim the rank of the fasting, night-praying reciter through character and noble nature. > **""The upright Muslim attains the rank of the fasting, night-praying reciter of the verses of Allah through his good character and noble nature.""**"
"12671","Rāfiʿ ibn Makīth, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), who witnessed al-Ḥudaybiyah, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ joined good character to growth, righteousness to long life, and charity to protection from evil death. > **""Good character is growth and bad character is misfortune. Righteousness increases life and charity prevents an evil death.""**"
"12672","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), and offered two traits light upon the back yet heaviest in the balance. Upon assent, he named character and silence and swore to their unrivaled adornment. > **""O Abū Dharr, shall I guide you to two traits, light upon the back and heavier in the balance than all else. Keep to good character and long silence, for by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, creatures never adorn themselves with their like.""**"
"12673","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ linked completeness of faith to character and raised character to the rank of fasting and prayer. > **""The most complete of the believers in faith are the best of them in character, and good character attains the rank of fasting and prayer.""**"
"12674","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Allah elevates a servant through character to devotional rank. > **""Allah raises the servant through his good character to the rank of fasting and prayer.""**"
"12675","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ denied that wealth can encompass people and commanded cheerfulness and good character instead. > **""You shall never encompass people with your wealth, but let your cheerful face and good character encompass them.""**"
"12676","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether to name the best, and upon assent answered with character. > **""Shall I tell you of the best of you. The best of you in character.""**"
"12677","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defined the best through longevity joined to character. > **""The best of you are the longest of you in life and the best of you in character.""**"
"12678","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported from the Prophet ﷺ that nothing outweighs good character and that it elevates its bearer to devotional rank. > **""Nothing placed in the balance is heavier than good character, and good character brings its bearer to the rank of fasting and prayer.""**"
"12679","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ guaranteed houses in the outskirts, middle, and highest Paradise for abandoning quarrel, lying, and ill character. > **""I guarantee a house in the outskirts of Paradise, a house in its middle, and a house in its highest part, for one who abandons disputation though right, abandons lying though jesting, and has good character.""**"
"12680","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), reported from the Prophet ﷺ a guarantee of houses in the gardens, highest, and lowest Paradise for abandoning dispute, lying, and ill character. > **""I guarantee a house in the gardens of Paradise, a house in its highest part, and a house in its lowest part, for one who abandons disputation though right, abandons lying though laughing, and has good character.""**"
"12681","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised chambers, courtyards, and middle Paradise for abandoning lies, disputes, and ill character. > **""A house in the chambers of Paradise, a house in its courtyard, and a house in its middle, for one who abandons lying though jesting, abandons disputation though right, and has good character.""**"
"12682","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that a man confessed love of beauty and praise while fearing for himself. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ removed the barrier to praiseworthy life and happy death and declared his mission of character. > **""What prevents you from loving to live praiseworthy and die happy. I was sent only to perfect noble character; I was sent with noble character.""**"
"12683","Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), narrated that the Prophet ﷺ sent him to a people, and when asked for counsel commanded peace, food, shame before Allah, good after evil, and beautified character. > **""Spread peace, offer food, be shy of Allah as a man of awe among your family is shy, when you do evil follow it with good, and beautify your character as much as you can.""**"
"12684","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, may Allah be pleased with them both (رضي الله عنهما), narrated that Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him (رضي الله عنه), asked the Messenger ﷺ for counsel. He commanded worship without association, then goodness after evil, then uprightness with good character, each upon request for more. > **""Worship Allah and associate nothing with Him. When you do evil, do good. Be upright and beautify your character.""**"
"12685","On the authority of Anas رضي الله عنه, he said: Umm Ḥabībah رضي الله عنها said: O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, a woman may have had two husbands, then she dies and enters Paradise, she and both her husbands, to which of them will she belong, to the first or to the last? He said: She will be given the choice of the one of them who had the best character with her in this world, he will be her husband in Paradise. O Umm Ḥabībah, good character has carried away the good of this world and the Hereafter. > **""She will be given the choice of the one of them who had the best character with her in this world, he will be her husband in Paradise. O Umm Ḥabībah, good character has carried away the good of this world and the Hereafter.""**"
"12686","On the authority of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The head of intellect after faith in Allah is showing love to people. He said, and with the same chain he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: There are three traits, whoever lacks them is neither from me nor from Allah. It was said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Forbearance by which he repels the ignorance of the ignorant, good character by which he lives among people, and scrupulous piety which restrains him from disobedience to Allah. > **""The head of intellect after faith in Allah is showing love to people.""**"
"12687","On the authority of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A man indeed attains by forbearance the rank of one who fasts and stands in prayer, and a man is indeed recorded as a tyrant though he owns nothing but his household. > **""A man indeed attains by forbearance the rank of one who fasts and stands in prayer, and a man is indeed recorded as a tyrant though he owns nothing but his household.""**"
"12688","On the authority of Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه, from the Prophet ﷺ, he said: The most excellent of you are the best of you in character, and good character is from faith. > **""The most excellent of you are the best of you in character, and good character is from faith.""**"
"12689","On his authority, from the Prophet ﷺ: And among the nearest of you to me on the Day of Resurrection are the best of you in character. > **""Among the nearest of you to me on the Day of Resurrection are the best of you in character.""**"
"12690","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Good character melts away sins just as water melts ice, and bad character corrupts deeds just as vinegar corrupts honey. > **""Good character melts away sins just as water melts ice, and bad character corrupts deeds just as vinegar corrupts honey.""**"
"12691","On the authority of Usāmah ibn Sharīk رضي الله عنه, he said: We were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ as if birds were upon our heads, none of us speaking, when some people came to him and said: Who are the most beloved of Allah's servants to Allah the Exalted? He said: The best of them in character. > **""The best of them in character.""**"
"12692","On the authority of Anas رضي الله عنه, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he said: A servant indeed reaches by his good character the great degrees of the Hereafter and its noble stations, though he be weak in worship, and he indeed reaches by his bad character the lowest degree in Hell. > **""A servant indeed reaches by his good character the great degrees of the Hereafter and its noble stations, though he be weak in worship, and he indeed reaches by his bad character the lowest degree in Hell.""**"
"12693","On the authority of Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه, he said: I was in a gathering in which were the Prophet ﷺ, and Samurah and Abū Umāmah, and he said: Obscenity and indecent speech are in no way from Islam, and the best of people in Islam are the best of them in character. > **""Obscenity and indecent speech are in no way from Islam, and the best of people in Islam are the best of them in character.""**"
"12694","On the authority of Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A man indeed attains by his good character the rank of one who stands in prayer by night and thirsts in the midday heat. > **""A man indeed attains by his good character the rank of one who stands in prayer by night and thirsts in the midday heat.""**"
"12695","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked: Which of the people are best? He said: The best of them in character. > **""The best of them in character.""**"
"12696","On the authority of Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to Yemen, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, advise me. He said: Hold to good character, for the best of people in character are the best of them in religion. > **""Hold to good character, for the best of people in character are the best of them in religion.""**"
"12697","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, from the Prophet ﷺ, he said: There is nothing except that it has repentance, except the one of bad character, for he does not repent from one sin but that he returns to one worse than it. > **""There is nothing except that it has repentance, except the one of bad character, for he does not repent from one sin but that he returns to one worse than it.""**"
"12698","On her authority, she said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Ill omen is bad character. > **""Ill omen is bad character.""**"
"12699","On the authority of Jābir رضي الله عنه, he said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is ill omen? He said: Bad character. > **""Bad character.""**"
"12700","On the authority of Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The worst of people are those who are constricting upon their families. They said: O Messenger of Allah, and how is one constricting upon his family? He said: When a man enters his house his wife cowers, and his child flees and runs away, and when he leaves his wife laughs and his household feels at ease. > **""The worst of people are those who are constricting upon their families.""**"
"12701","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whosever slave or beast or child is ill-mannered toward him, then recite in his ear: Do they seek other than the religion of Allah. > **""Whosever slave or beast or child is ill-mannered toward him, then recite in his ear: Do they seek other than the religion of Allah.""**"
"12702","On the authority of ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The best of my nation are its sharp-tempered ones, those who when they become angry return. > **""The best of my nation are its sharp-tempered ones, those who when they become angry return.""**"
"12703","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Sharpness of temper afflicts the best of my nation. > **""Sharpness of temper afflicts the best of my nation.""**"
"12704","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, she said: I used to enter my house in which were the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and my father, and I would lay aside my garment, saying: He is only my husband and my father. Then when ʿUmar was buried with them, by Allah I never entered it except with my garments drawn tightly upon me, out of modesty before ʿUmar رضي الله عنه."
"12705","On the authority of Anas رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was more modest than a virgin in her seclusion, and when he disliked something we recognized it in his face. And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Modesty is altogether good. > **""Modesty is altogether good.""**"
"12706","On the authority of ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Modesty is from faith, and faith is in Paradise, and obscenity is from harshness, and harshness is in the Fire. > **""Modesty is from faith, and faith is in Paradise, and obscenity is from harshness, and harshness is in the Fire.""**"
"12707","On the authority of Qurrah ibn Iyās رضي الله عنه, he said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ and modesty was mentioned in his presence, so they said: O Messenger of Allah, is modesty from religion? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Rather it is the whole religion. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Modesty and chastity and impediment, impediment of the tongue not impediment of the heart, and deeds are from faith, and they increase in the Hereafter and decrease in this world, and what they increase in the Hereafter is more than what they decrease in this world, and miserliness and obscenity are from hypocrisy, and they increase in this world and decrease in the Hereafter, and what they decrease in the Hereafter is more than what they increase in this world. > **""Rather it is the whole religion.""**"
"12708","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, she said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: O ʿĀʾishah, if modesty were a man it would be a righteous man, and if obscenity were a man it would be an evil man. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, if modesty were a man it would be a righteous man, and if obscenity were a man it would be an evil man.""**"
"12709","On the authority of Dāwūd ibn Muṣʿab, from his father, he said: We were with Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه, and we met people returning from Friday prayer, so he entered a house and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Whoever feels no shame before people feels no shame before Allah. > **""Whoever feels no shame before people feels no shame before Allah.""**"
"12710","On the authority of Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها, that the Prophet ﷺ said: The first of what my Lord entrusted to me and forbade me, after worship of idols and drinking of wine, was quarrelling with men. > **""The first of what my Lord entrusted to me and forbade me, after worship of idols and drinking of wine, was quarrelling with men.""**"
"12711","On the authority of Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Among what people have attained from the speech of the first prophethood is: When you feel no shame, do as you wish. > **""When you feel no shame, do as you wish.""**"
"12712","And in a narration: The last of what the people of ignorance held to from the speech of the first prophethood. > **""The last of what the people of ignorance held to from the speech of the first prophethood.""**"
"12713","On the authority of Umm al-Ṭufayl رضي الله عنها, from the Prophet ﷺ, he said: It used to be said: Among what people have attained from the speech of prophethood is: When you feel no shame, do as you wish. > **""When you feel no shame, do as you wish.""**"
"12714","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Jazʾ al-Zubaydī رضي الله عنه, that he passed by with a companion, while youths from Quraysh had loosened their waist-wrappers and made them into whips, striking one another with them while naked. ʿAbd Allāh said: When we passed by them they said: These are monks, so leave them. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out against them, and when they saw him they scattered, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned angry until he entered, and I was behind the chamber, and I heard him say: Glory be to Allah, they felt no shame before Allah, nor did they veil themselves from His Messenger. And Umm Ayman was with him, saying: Seek forgiveness for them, O Messenger of Allah, but he scarcely sought forgiveness for them. > **""Glory be to Allah, they felt no shame before Allah, nor did they veil themselves from His Messenger.""**"
"12715","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When Allah, Mighty and Majestic, created the intellect, He said to it: Stand. So it stood. Then He said to it: Turn back. So it turned back. Then He said to it: Sit. So it sat. Then He said to it: By My Might, I have not created a creation better than you, nor more noble than you, nor more excellent than you nor more beautiful. By you I seize and by you I give, and by you I am known, and by you is reward and upon you is punishment. > **""By My Might, I have not created a creation better than you, nor more noble than you, nor more excellent than you nor more beautiful. By you I seize and by you I give, and by you is reward and upon you is punishment.""**"
"12716","On the authority of Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When Allah created the intellect, He said to it: Come forward. So it came forward. Then He said to it: Turn back. So it turned back. Then He said: By My Might, I have not created a creation more pleasing to Me than you. By you I seize and by you I give, and by you is reward and upon you is punishment. > **""By My Might, I have not created a creation more pleasing to Me than you. By you I seize and by you I give, and by you is reward and upon you is punishment.""**"
"12717","On the authority of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The head of intellect after faith in Allah is showing love to people. > **""The head of intellect after faith in Allah is showing love to people.""**"
"12718","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The head of intellect after faith in Allah is showing affection to people. > **""The head of intellect after faith in Allah is showing affection to people.""**"
"12719","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A man may be among the people of prayer and charity and pilgrimage and ʿumrah and struggle, until he mentioned the shares of good, yet he will be recompensed on the Day of Resurrection only according to his intellect. > **""He will be recompensed on the Day of Resurrection only according to his intellect.""**"
"12720","On the authority of Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Two men may head to the mosque, then one of them returns with his prayer more excellent than the other's, when he is the more excellent of the two in intellect, while the other returns with his prayer not weighing even a speck. > **""Two men may head to the mosque, then one of them returns with his prayer more excellent than the other's, when he is the more excellent of the two in intellect.""**"
"12721","On the authority of Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه, he said: When it reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man that his worship was intense, he would ask about his intellect. If they said: Good, he said: I have hope for him. And if they said other than that, he said: Your companion will not reach where you suppose. > **""I have hope for him.""**"
"12722","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I am a witness on behalf of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, that no intelligent man stumbles but that He raises him, then he does not stumble but that He raises him, then he does not stumble but that He raises him, until He brings him into Paradise. > **""No intelligent man stumbles but that He raises him, until He brings him into Paradise.""**"
"12723","On the authority of Hāniʾ ibn Yazīd Abū Shurayḥ رضي الله عنه, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, guide me to a deed that will admit me to Paradise. He said: Among what necessitates forgiveness are offering peace and good speech. > **""Among what necessitates forgiveness are offering peace and good speech.""**"
"12724","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, from the Prophet ﷺ, he said: Peace is a Name among the Names of Allah the Exalted which He placed upon the earth, so spread it among you. For when a Muslim man passes by a people and greets them and they reply to him, he has a degree of excellence over them by reminding them, and if they do not reply to him, one who is better and more pleasant than them replies to him. > **""Peace is a Name among the Names of Allah the Exalted which He placed upon the earth, so spread it among you.""**"
"12725","On the authority of al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Spread peace, you will be safe, and greed is evil. > **""Spread peace, you will be safe, and greed is evil.""**"
"12726","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, from the Prophet ﷺ, he said: Peace is a Name among the Names of Allah which He placed upon the earth, as a greeting for the people of our religion and a security for the people of our covenant. > **""Peace is a Name among the Names of Allah which He placed upon the earth, as a greeting for the people of our religion and a security for the people of our covenant.""**"
"12727","On the authority of Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has made peace a greeting for our nation and a security for the people of our covenant. > **""Allah has made peace a greeting for our nation and a security for the people of our covenant.""**"
"12728","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Peace is a Name among the Names of Allah, so spread it among you. > **""Peace is a Name among the Names of Allah, so spread it among you.""**"
"12729","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Spread peace, for it is pleasing to Allah. > **""Spread peace, for it is pleasing to Allah.""**"
"12730","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: You will not enter Paradise until you believe, and you will not believe until you love one another. Shall I not guide you to a thing which, if you do it, you will love one another? Spreading peace among you. > **""You will not enter Paradise until you believe, and you will not believe until you love one another. Shall I not guide you to a thing which, if you do it, you will love one another? Spreading peace among you.""**"
"12731","On the authority of Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه, that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: You will not believe until you love one another. Shall I not guide you to what you will love one another upon? They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Spread peace among you. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you will not enter Paradise until you show mercy to one another. They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, all of us are merciful. He said: It is not the mercy of one of you to his companion, but mercy to all. > **""Spread peace among you. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you will not enter Paradise until you show mercy to one another.""**"
"12732","On the authority of al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: There has crept to you the disease of the nations before you: hatred and envy, and hatred is the shaver, not the shaver of hair, but the shaver of religion. By Him in Whose Hand is my soul, you will not enter Paradise until you believe, and you will not believe until you love one another. Shall I not inform you of what will establish that for you? Spread peace among you. > **""Hatred is the shaver, not the shaver of hair, but the shaver of religion. Spread peace among you.""**"
"12733","On the authority of Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Spread peace so that you may rise high. > **""Spread peace so that you may rise high.""**"
"12734","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, from the Prophet ﷺ, he said: Whoever greets twenty men among the Muslims in a day, together or separately, then dies on that day of his, Paradise becomes obligatory for him, and in the night likewise. > **""Whoever greets twenty men among the Muslims in a day, together or separately, then dies on that day of his, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"12735","On the authority of ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه, he said: I entered the mosque, and there was the Prophet ﷺ among a group of his Companions رضي الله عنهم, so I said: Peace be upon you. He said: And upon you be peace and the mercy of Allah, twenty for me and ten for you. He said: Then I entered a second time and said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah. He said: And upon you be peace and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, thirty for me and twenty for you. Then I entered a third time and said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. He said: And upon you be peace and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, thirty for me and thirty for you, I and you, O ʿAlī, are equal in greeting. Indeed, O ʿAlī, no man passes by a gathering and greets them but that Allah writes for him ten good deeds, and erases from him ten evil deeds, and raises him ten degrees. > **""No man passes by a gathering and greets them but that Allah writes for him ten good deeds, and erases from him ten evil deeds, and raises him ten degrees.""**"
"12736","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, he said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: Peace be upon you. He said: Ten. Then another came and said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, so he said: Twenty. Then another came and said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, so the Prophet ﷺ said: Thirty. > **""Ten. Twenty. Thirty.""**"
"12737","On the authority of Sahl ibn Ḥanīf رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever says: Peace be upon you, ten good deeds are written for him. And whoever says: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, twenty good deeds are written for him, and whoever says: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, thirty good deeds are written for him. > **""Whoever says: Peace be upon you, ten good deeds are written for him. And whoever says: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, twenty good deeds are written for him, and whoever says: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, thirty good deeds are written for him.""**"
"12738","On the authority of Mālik ibn al-Tayyihān رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever says: Peace be upon you, ten good deeds are written for him, and whoever says: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, twenty good deeds are written for him, and whoever says: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, fifty good deeds are written for him. > **""Whoever says: Peace be upon you, ten good deeds are written for him, and whoever says: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, twenty good deeds are written for him, and whoever says: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, fifty good deeds are written for him.""**"
"12739","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, from the Prophet ﷺ, he said: The most helpless of people is he who is helpless in supplication, and the most miserly of people is he who is miserly with greeting. > **""The most helpless of people is he who is helpless in supplication, and the most miserly of people is he who is miserly with greeting.""**"
"12740","On the authority of Jābir رضي الله عنه, that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: So-and-so has a date-cluster in my garden, and he has harmed me and its place has become hard upon me. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent to him and said: Sell me your cluster which is in the garden of so-and-so. He said: No. He said: Then grant it to me. He said: No. He said: Then sell it to me for a cluster in Paradise. He said: No. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I have not seen one more miserly than you, except one who is miserly with greeting. > **""I have not seen one more miserly than you, except one who is miserly with greeting.""**"
"12741","On the authority of ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: The Hour will not come until greeting is only by acquaintance, and this one knew me among you so he greeted me. > **""The Hour will not come until greeting is only by acquaintance.""**"
"12742","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever begins with asking before greeting, do not answer him. > **""Whoever begins with asking before greeting, do not answer him.""**"
"12743","On the authority of Jābir رضي الله عنه, that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: Do not give permission to one who does not begin with greeting. > **""Do not give permission to one who does not begin with greeting.""**"
"12744","On the authority of ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAṭāʾ, from Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, in doubt as to its being raised, he said: Permission is not given to one seeking permission until he begins with greeting. > **""Permission is not given to one seeking permission until he begins with greeting.""**"
"12745","On the authority of Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه, he said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we meet, so which of us begins with greeting? He said: The most obedient of you to Allah. > **""The most obedient of you to Allah.""**"
"12746","On the authority of al-Agharr, Agharr Muzaynah رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had assigned for me a share of dates with a man from the Anṣār, but he delayed me in it, so I spoke to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about him, and he said: Go with him tomorrow, O Abū Bakr, and take for him his dates. Abū Bakr promised me at the mosque after we prayed dawn, and I found him where he had promised me, so we set out, and whenever a man saw Abū Bakr from afar he greeted him, so Abū Bakr said: Do you not see what the people attain over you in excellence, no one precedes you in greeting. So whenever a man appeared we hastened to greet him before he greeted us. > **""No one precedes you in greeting.""**"
"12747","On the authority of Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه, that he used to greet everyone he met. He said: I know of no one who preceded him in greeting except a Jew once, who hid for him behind a pillar, then came out and greeted him. So Abū Umāmah said to him: Woe to you, O Jew, what carried you to do what you did? He said to him: I saw you as a man who greets often, and I knew it was excellence, so I wanted to take it. So Abū Umāmah said to him: Woe to you, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Allah has made peace a greeting for our nation and a security for the people of our covenant. > **""Allah has made peace a greeting for our nation and a security for the people of our covenant.""**"
"12748","On the authority of Salmān رضي الله عنه, he said: A man came and greeted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah. He said: And upon you be peace and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Then another came and said: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, and the mercy of Allah. He said: And upon you be peace and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Then another came and said: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: And upon you. The man said: O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so and so-and-so came to you, and you greeted them with better than what you greeted me? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: You left nothing, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: When you are greeted with a greeting, greet with better than it or return it, so I returned the greeting to you. > **""When you are greeted with a greeting, greet with better than it or return it.""**"
"12749","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما, he said: Three men came to the Prophet ﷺ, so one of them said: Peace be upon you. So the Prophet ﷺ replied: And upon you and the mercy of Allah. Then the second came and said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah, so the Prophet ﷺ replied to him: And the mercy of Allah and His blessings. And the third came and said: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. So the Prophet ﷺ replied to him: And upon you. And the father of the youth was sitting with the Prophet ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, you added for so-and-so and so-and-so, and you added nothing for my son? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: We found no addition for him, so we returned to him the like of what he said. > **""We found no addition for him, so we returned to him the like of what he said.""**"
"12750","On the authority of ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to her: O ʿĀʾishah, this is Jibrīl conveying peace to you. So I said: And upon you be peace and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, and she went to add, so the Prophet ﷺ said: To this greeting ends, then he said: The mercy of Allah and His blessings be upon you, people of the house. > **""O ʿĀʾishah, this is Jibrīl conveying peace to you.""**"
"12751","On the authority of Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When one of you meets his brother repeatedly, let him greet him. > **""When one of you meets his brother repeatedly, let him greet him.""**"
"12752","On the authority of Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه, he said: When we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and a tree separated us, then when we met again, we would greet one another."
"12753","On the authority of Thābit al-Bunānī, from Anas, or another, from the Prophet ﷺ, that he sought permission at the house of Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah رضي الله عنه, saying: Peace be upon you and the mercy of Allah. So Saʿd said: And upon you be peace and the mercy of Allah. But the Prophet ﷺ did not hear him, until he greeted three times, and Saʿd replied three times without making him hear, so the Prophet ﷺ turned back, and Saʿd followed him and said: O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransom for you, no greeting did you give but that it reached my ear, and I replied to you without making you hear, for I loved to increase in your greeting and in blessing. Then he admitted him to the house and offered him raisins, so the Prophet ﷺ ate, and when he finished he said: The righteous have eaten your food, and the angels have prayed upon you, and the fasting have broken fast with you. > **""The righteous have eaten your food, and the angels have prayed upon you, and the fasting have broken fast with you.""**"
"12754","On the authority of Umm Ṭāriq, freedwoman of Saʿd رضي الله عنها, she said: The Prophet ﷺ came to Saʿd and sought permission, so Saʿd remained silent. Then he sought permission again, so Saʿd remained silent. Then he repeated, so Saʿd remained silent. So the Prophet ﷺ turned away, and Saʿd sent me to him: Nothing prevented us from giving you permission except that we wanted you to increase us. > **""Nothing prevented us from giving you permission except that we wanted you to increase us.""**"
"12755","On the authority of Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, from the Prophet ﷺ, he said: Allah is Peace, so do not begin with anything before Him. When it is said: Peace be upon you, say: Peace be upon you. > **""Allah is Peace, so do not begin with anything before Him.""**"
"12756","And in a narration: When one of you intends, let him say: Peace be upon you, for Allah is Peace, so do not begin with anything before Allah. > **""Peace be upon you, for Allah is Peace, so do not begin with anything before Allah.""**"
"12757","On the authority of Muʿādh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said: It is a duty upon whoever comes to a gathering to greet them, and a duty upon whoever rises from a gathering to greet. Then a man stood while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was speaking, and he did not greet, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: How quickly he forgot. > **""It is a duty upon whoever comes to a gathering to greet them, and a duty upon whoever rises from a gathering to greet.""**"
"12758","On the authority of al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما, he said: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, a people come to a house, and one of them seeks permission, does it suffice for all of them? He said: Yes. It was said: And a man of the people replies, does it suffice for all? He said: Yes. It was said: And a people pass by, and one of them greets, does it suffice for all? He said: Yes. It was said: And a man of the people replies, does it suffice for all? He said: Yes. > **""Yes.""**"
"12759","On the authority of Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrah, he said: My father said: When you pass by a gathering, greet its people, for if they are in good you will be their partner, and if they are in other than that you will have reward. Thus I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say. > **""When you pass by a gathering, greet its people, for if they are in good you will be their partner, and if they are in other than that you will have reward.""**"
"12760","Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrah reports from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that his father advised him that when he sits in a gathering whose good he hopes for and a need hastens him to leave, he should say peace be upon you, for he shares with them in whatever reward they obtain in that gathering. > **""O my son, when you are in a gathering whose good you hope for and a need hastens you, say: Peace be upon you, for you are a partner with them in whatever they obtain in that gathering.""**"
"12761","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the rider greets the walker, the walker greets the seated one, and when two walkers meet, whichever of them begins first is more excellent. > **""The rider greets the walker, the walker greets the seated one, and of two walkers, whichever begins first is more excellent.""**"
"12762","Abū Sallām reports that Muʿāwiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shibl, may Allah be pleased with him, to teach the people what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he gathered them and said that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say to learn the Qurʾān, and when they learn it not to go to excess in it, nor to turn away from it, nor to eat by it, nor to seek abundance through it. He then said that merchants are the wicked, and when asked whether Allah has made trade lawful and forbidden usury, he said yes, but they swear oaths and sin thereby. He then said that the corrupt are the people of the Fire, and when asked who the corrupt are, he said women, and when asked whether they are not mothers, daughters and sisters, he said yes, but when they are given they are not grateful and when tested they are not patient. He then said that the rider greets the pedestrian, the pedestrian greets the seated, and the fewer greet the more, so whoever answers the greeting gains its reward, and whoever does not answer gains nothing. > **""Learn the Qurʾān, and when you have learned it, do not go to excess in it, do not turn away from it, do not eat by it, and do not seek abundance through it.""**"
"12763","Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met his Companions, he would not shake hands with them until he had greeted them with peace."
"12764","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said that whenever two Muslims meet and one of them takes the hand of his companion, it is binding upon Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to attend their supplication and not to separate their hands until He forgives them, and in another wording it is binding upon Allah to answer their supplication and not to turn back their hands until He forgives them. > **""There are no two Muslims who meet and one of them takes the hand of his companion except that it is binding upon Allah to attend their supplication and not to separate their hands until He forgives them.""**"
"12765","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, when they met one another, would shake hands, and when they returned from a journey, they would embrace one another."
"12766","Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that when a believer meets a believer, greets him with peace, takes his hand and shakes it, the sins of both fall away as the leaves of a tree fall away. > **""When a believer meets a believer and greets him with peace and takes his hand and shakes it, their sins fall away as the leaves of a tree fall away.""**"
"12767","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when two Muslim men meet and one of them greets his companion, the more beloved of them to Allah is the one with the better cheerfulness toward his companion, and when they shake hands, one hundred mercies descend upon them, ninety for the one who begins and ten for the one who joins him. > **""When two Muslim men meet and one of them greets his companion, the more beloved of them to Allah is the better of them in cheerfulness toward his companion, and when they shake hands, one hundred mercies descend upon them, ninety for the one who begins and ten for the one who shakes.""**"
"12768","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ met Ḥudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, and intended to shake his hand, but Ḥudhayfah withdrew and said that he was in a state of major impurity, whereupon he said that when a Muslim shakes the hand of his brother, their sins fall away as the leaves of a tree fall away. > **""When a Muslim shakes the hand of his brother, their sins fall away as the leaves of a tree fall away.""**"
"12769","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when two Muslims meet and shake hands and inquire after one another, Allah sends down one hundred mercies between them, ninety-nine of them for the more cheerful, open, dutiful and best-mannered of them toward his brother. > **""When two Muslims meet and shake hands and inquire after one another, Allah sends down one hundred mercies between them, ninety-nine for the more cheerful, open, dutiful and best-mannered of them toward his brother.""**"
"12770","Abū Dāwūd reports that al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allah be pleased with him, met him, took his hand, shook it, smiled in his face and said whether he knew why he took his hand, and he answered that he thought it was only for some good, so al-Barāʾ said that the Prophet ﷺ had met him and done the same, then asked whether he knew why he did that, and when he said no, the Prophet ﷺ said that when two Muslims meet and shake hands and each smiles in the face of his companion, doing so only for Allah, they do not separate until they are forgiven. > **""When two Muslims meet and shake hands and each smiles in the face of his companion, they do not separate until they are forgiven.""**"
"12771","Salmān al-Fārisī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that when a Muslim meets his Muslim brother and takes his hand, their sins fall from them as leaves fall from a dry tree on a day of violent wind, and they are forgiven even if their sins are like the foam of the sea. > **""When a Muslim meets his Muslim brother and takes his hand, their sins fall from them as leaves fall from a dry tree on a day of violent wind, and they are forgiven even if their sins are like the foam of the sea.""**"
"12772","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when two Muslims shake hands, their palms do not part until they are forgiven. > **""When two Muslims shake hands, their palms do not part until they are forgiven.""**"
"12773","Salmān al-Fārisī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever wishes that Satan finds with him neither food nor midday rest nor night lodging should greet with peace when he enters his house and name Allah over his food. > **""Whoever wishes that Satan finds with him neither food nor midday rest nor night lodging, let him greet with peace when he enters his house and name Allah over his food.""**"
"12774","Jarīr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by some women and greeted them with peace."
"12775","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man greeted the Prophet ﷺ while he was in prayer, so the Prophet ﷺ returned it by gesture, and when he finished the prayer he said that they used to return the greeting in prayer but were then forbidden from that. > **""We used to return the greeting, then we were forbidden from that.""**"
"12776","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying, greeted him, and he gestured to him in reply."
"12777","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, said that if he entered upon people who were praying, he would not greet them with peace."
"12778","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that greeting a man with one finger by gesturing with it is the act of the Jews. > **""Greeting with one finger by gesturing with it is the act of the Jews.""**"
"12779","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports, thinking it raised to the Prophet ﷺ, that he said that whoever imitates other than us is not of us, so do not imitate the Jews nor the Christians, for the greeting of the Jews is gesture with the fingers and the greeting of the Christians is with the palms, and do not clip only the forelocks, and trim the mustaches, and leave the beards full, and do not walk in the mosques and markets wearing only shirts without lower garments beneath them. > **""He is not of us who imitates other than us. Do not imitate the Jews nor the Christians, for the greeting of the Jews is gesture with the fingers and the greeting of the Christians is with the palms.""**"
"12780","ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamrī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ sent three men to Caesar, to Kisrā and to the ruler of Alexandria, and sent ʿAmr to the Negus, and when ʿAmr came to the Negus he found that those with him entered bending low through a small door, so when he saw the small door and their entering through it, he turned his back and entered walking backward, then straightened when inside, so the Abyssinians were alarmed and intended to kill him and asked what prevented him from entering as they entered, and he said that they do not do that even with their Prophet, who is more deserving of it, so the Negus said to leave him, for he speaks the truth."
"12781","Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, reports that when their clans oppressed them in Makkah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told the Muslims to disperse in the land, so they dispersed to the land of Abyssinia, and Quraysh sent ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Rabīʿah and ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and among what ʿAmr and ʿAbd Allāh said to the Negus was that they do not greet him with the greeting with which those from us greet him, so he asked Jaʿfar, may Allah be pleased with him, and his companions why they do not greet him as their companions greet him, and he replied that they greet him with the greeting of their Prophet ﷺ, for it is the greeting of the people of Paradise. > **""Disperse in the land.""**"
"12782","ʿAmr ibn Murrah al-Juhanī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever loves that men stand before him in attendance should take his seat in the Fire. > **""Whoever loves that men stand before him in attendance, let him take his seat in the Fire.""**"
"12783","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that those before them perished only because they magnified their kings by standing while the kings remained seated. > **""Those before you perished only because they magnified their kings by standing while they sat.""**"
"12784","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to them, and Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said to stand and seek aid with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ against this hypocrite, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that no one should stand for him, for standing is only for Allah, Blessed and Exalted. > **""Do not stand for me; standing is only for Allah, Blessed and Exalted.""**"
"12785","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that a man stands from his place for his brother, except the Banū Hāshim who do not stand for anyone. > **""A man stands from his place for his brother, except the Banū Hāshim who do not stand for anyone.""**"
"12786","Muḥammad ibn Hilāl reports from his father that when the Prophet ﷺ would come out, they would stand for him until he entered his house."
"12787","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man entered the mosque while the Prophet ﷺ was alone in it, so the Prophet ﷺ moved over for him, and the man said that the place was spacious, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said that the Muslim has a right to such consideration. > **""The Muslim has a right.""**"
"12788","Abū al-Bakhtarī reports that al-Ashʿath ibn Qays and Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bajalī, may Allah be pleased with them, came to Salmān al-Fārisī, may Allah be pleased with him, in a fortress on the outskirts of al-Madāʾin, greeted him and asked whether he was Salmān al-Fārisī, and he said yes, then they asked whether he was the companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said he did not know, so they doubted and thought he might not be the one they sought, whereupon he told them that he was indeed the companion they sought, that he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and sat with him, but the true companion is whoever enters Paradise with him, and asked their need, so they said they had come from a brother of his in Sham, and when asked who, they said Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, so he asked where was the gift he had sent with them, and they said he sent no gift, so he told them to fear Allah and fulfill the trust, for no one came to him from Abū al-Dardāʾ except with a gift, and they said not to press this upon them since they had wealth for him to decide about, but he said he did not want their wealth but the gift sent with them, so they swore by Allah that he sent nothing with them except that he told them that among them was a man with whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when alone with him, desired no one else, and that when they reached him they should convey his greeting of peace, whereupon Salmān said what gift did he want from them other than this, and what gift is better than peace, a blessed wholesome greeting from Allah. > **""What gift did I want from you other than this, and what gift is better than peace, a blessed wholesome greeting from Allah?""**"
"12789","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, said that whoever of Allah's creation greets you, return his greeting, even if he is a Magian, for Allah says that when you are greeted with a greeting, greet with better than it or return it. > **""Whoever of Allah's creation greets you, return it upon him, even if he is a Magian.""**"
"12790","Tamīm ibn Salamah reports that some people of polytheism walked with ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, and when he reached the gate of the palace, he greeted them with peace."
"12791","Abū Baṣrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they would go to the Jews in the morning, so they should not begin them with peace, but if they greeted them they should say: and upon you. He added that when they came to them and they greeted them, they said: and upon you. > **""We shall go to the Jews in the morning, so do not begin them with peace, and if they greet you, say: And upon you.""**"
"12792","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said that they were forbidden — or he said they were commanded — not to add for the People of the Book over saying: and upon you. > **""Do not add for the People of the Book over: And upon you.""**"
"12793","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man of the People of the Book came and greeted the Prophet ﷺ saying death be upon you, so ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked whether he should strike his neck, and he said no. > **""No.""**"
"12794","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that they were with the Prophet ﷺ in a gathering when a Jew passed and greeted them, and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned it, whereupon he asked whether they knew what he had said, and they said yes, he had greeted, so he said that he had said death be upon you, meaning you grow weary of your religion, and told them to return him to him, then asked him how he had spoken, and he said death be upon you, so the Prophet ﷺ said that when the People of the Book greet them they should say: upon you, meaning upon you what you have said. > **""When the People of the Book greet you, say: Upon you.""**"
"12795","Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that while he was with the Prophet ﷺ, a Jewish man called Thaʿlabah ibn al-Ḥārith came and said death be upon you, O Muḥammad, so he said: and upon you. > **""And upon you.""**"
"12796","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said not to shake hands with the Jews and Christians. > **""Do not shake hands with the Jews and Christians.""**"
"12797","Kaʿb ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the verse of his pardon was revealed, he came to the Prophet ﷺ, took his hand and kissed it."
"12798","Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥārith al-Dhimārī reports that he met Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked whether he had pledged the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with this hand of his, and he said yes, so he asked him to extend his hand so he could kiss it, and he extended it and he kissed it."
"12799","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Razīn reports from Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said he had pledged the Prophet ﷺ with this hand of his, so they kissed it and he did not disapprove of that."
"12800","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he kissed the hand of the Prophet ﷺ."
"12801","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from a journey, he would kiss his daughter Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"12802","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the door of the Prophet ﷺ would be knocked with the fingernails."
"12803","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man peered at the Prophet ﷺ while the Prophet ﷺ had a stick with him, whereupon he said that if he had known he was watching him, he would have thrust it into his eye, or words to that effect. > **""If I knew you were watching me, I would have thrust it into your eye.""**"
"12804","Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever uncovers a curtain and inserts his sight before he is given permission has committed a violation not lawful for him to commit, and if a man were to poke out his eye it would be void of compensation, but if a man passes a door with no curtain and sees something private, there is no sin upon him, for the fault is upon the people of the house. > **""Whoever uncovers a curtain and inserts his sight before he is given permission has committed a violation not lawful for him, and if a man were to poke out his eye it would be void.""**"
"12805","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ exiled al-Ḥakam ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ from Madīnah to al-Ṭāʾif because, while the Prophet ﷺ was in his chamber, a man peered at him, so the Prophet ﷺ said: a lizard, a lizard, and when they looked it was al-Ḥakam, so the Prophet ﷺ said to go out and never dwell with him in Madīnah as long as he lived, and exiled him to al-Ṭāʾif. > **""Go out; you shall never dwell with me in Madīnah as long as I live.""**"
"12806","Abū Umāmah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever testifies that he is the Messenger of Allah should not attend prayer while retaining urine or stool until he relieves himself, and whoever testifies that he is the Messenger of Allah and leads people should not single himself out in supplication, and whoever testifies that he is the Messenger of Allah should not enter a household until he seeks familiarity and greets, for if he looks into the depths of the house he has already entered. > **""Whoever testifies that I am the Messenger of Allah, let him not enter a household until he seeks familiarity and greets, for if he looks into the depths of the house he has already entered.""**"
"12807","In another wording, whoever inserts his eyes into a house without the permission of its people has brought ruin, and whoever leads people in prayer and singles himself out in supplication to their exclusion has betrayed them. > **""Whoever inserts his eyes into a house without the permission of its people has brought ruin, and whoever leads people and singles himself out in supplication to their exclusion has betrayed them.""**"
"12808","Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he sought permission while facing the door, so the Prophet ﷺ told him not to seek permission while facing the door. > **""Do not seek permission while facing the door.""**"
"12809","In another wording he said that he came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in a house, stood opposite the door and sought permission, so he gestured to him to move away, then he came again and sought permission, whereupon he said whether seeking permission is for anything but the sake of sight. > **""Is seeking permission for anything but the sake of sight?""**"
"12810","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Busr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say not to come to houses from their doors, but to come to them from their sides, then seek permission, and if permitted enter, otherwise return. > **""Do not come to houses from their doors, but come to them from their sides and seek permission; if you are permitted, enter, otherwise return.""**"
"12811","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about seeking permission in houses and said that whoever's eye enters before he seeks permission and greets has no permission left and has disobeyed his Lord. > **""Whoever's eye enters before he seeks permission and greets, there is no permission left and he has disobeyed his Lord.""**"
"12812","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in his upper chamber and said peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, peace be upon you all, may ʿUmar enter. > **""Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah; peace be upon you all; may ʿUmar enter?""**"
"12813","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Mūsā reports that Mudrik sent him to ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, to ask her about some matters, so he came to her while she was praying the forenoon prayer, and thought to sit until she finished, but they said what was the matter, so he said he wanted to announce himself and asked how to seek permission from her, and he said to say peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings, peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah, peace be upon the Mothers of the Believers, the wives of the Prophet ﷺ, peace be upon you all, then he mentioned the rest of the account."
"12814","Abū Suwayd al-ʿAbdī reports that they came to Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and sat at his door to be given permission, but permission was delayed, so he rose to a hole in the door and began to peer through it, and Ibn ʿUmar noticed him, and when they were permitted and sat, he asked which of them had peered into his house a short while before, and he said it was he, so he asked by what right he deemed it lawful to peer into his house, and he said permission had been delayed so he looked without intending it, then they asked him about matters, and he asked O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān what he said about jihād, and he said that whoever strives strives only for himself."
"12815","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in his chamber, a man peered through a crevice of the house, and he saw him with a comb-like stick in his hand and said that if he had known he was watching him, he would have risen to insert it into his eye, for permission was legislated only to restrain the gaze. > **""If I knew you were watching me, I would have risen to insert this into your eye, for permission is only to restrain the gaze.""**"
"12816","Jarīr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that ʿUyaynah ibn Ḥiṣn entered upon the Prophet ﷺ while ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was with him, and asked who was beside him, so he said ʿĀʾishah, whereupon he asked whether he should step down for him one better than her, meaning his own wife, but the Prophet ﷺ said no, then the Prophet ﷺ told him to go out and seek permission, and he replied that he had taken an oath upon himself never to seek permission from a Muḍarī, so ʿĀʾishah asked who this was, and he said this was a foolish man who is followed. > **""Go out and seek permission.""**"
"12817","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for them, so they came and sought permission to enter."
"12818","Safīnah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he was with the Prophet ﷺ when ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, came seeking permission and knocked the door lightly, so the Prophet ﷺ said O Safīnah, open for him. > **""O Safīnah, open for him.""**"
"12819","Al-Ḥasan reports that the nobles of Quraysh gathered at the door of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, among them al-Ḥārith ibn Hishām, Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb and Suhayl ibn ʿAmr, along with slaves and freedmen from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and his chamberlain came out and permitted Bilāl, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ṣuhayb, may Allah be pleased with him, and others, leaving the rest, so Abū Sufyān said he had never seen the like of this day, that he permitted these slaves and left them sitting at his door without permission, whereupon Suhayl ibn ʿAmr, who was a man of reason, said O people, by Allah he saw what was in their faces, and if they were angry they should be angry at themselves, for the people were called and they were called, but they hastened and they delayed, then he said by Allah the virtue they were preceded to is a more severe loss upon them than this door over which they compete. Al-Ḥasan said by Allah, Allah does not make a servant hastening to Him like a servant delaying from Him."
"12820","Jundub ibn Sufyān, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that when one of them seeks permission three times and is not permitted, he should return. > **""When one of you seeks permission three times and is not permitted, let him return.""**"
"12821","Aʿyan al-Jarāmīzī reports that he came to Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was in a corridor, greeted him and asked whether he should enter, and he said this was a place in which no permission is sought."
"12822","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said that when you invite a man, you have already given him permission."
"12823","A man reports that they sought permission from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, after the dawn prayer, so he permitted them, cast a cloak over his wife and said that he disliked detaining them."
"12824","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said not to enter the houses of the protected non-Muslims except with permission. > **""Do not enter the houses of the protected non-Muslims except with permission.""**"
"12825","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ asked a man how he was, O so-and-so, and he said he praised Allah to him, O Messenger of Allah, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said this is what he wanted from him. > **""How are you, O so-and-so?""**"
"12826","Abū Ṣāliḥ reports that ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission from Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she permitted him, so he asked whether ʿAlī was there, and they said no, so he returned, then sought permission from her another time and asked whether ʿAlī was there, and they said yes, so he entered, whereupon ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, asked what had prevented him from entering when he had not found him there, and he said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden them from entering upon women whose husbands were absent."
"12827","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ would take good omens and would not take evil omens, and a good name pleased him."
"12828","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked about a man's name and it was good, that would be recognized in his face, and if it was otherwise he disliked it, and when he stopped at a village he would ask its name, and if its name was good he rejoiced at that, and if it was otherwise that would be seen in his face."
"12829","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that part of the right of a child upon his father is that he make his name good and make his upbringing good. > **""Part of the right of a child upon his father is that he make his name good and make his upbringing good.""**"
"12830","Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when they send a messenger to him, they should send one of handsome face and handsome name. > **""When you send a messenger to me, send one of handsome face and handsome name.""**"
"12831","Yaʿīsh al-Ghifārī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for a she-camel one day and asked who would milk it, so a man said he would, and asked his name, and he said Murrah, so he told him to sit, then another stood and asked his name, and he said Murrah, so he told him to sit, then another stood and asked his name, and he said Jamrah, so he told him to sit, then Yaʿīsh stood and asked his name, and he said Yaʿīsh, so he told him to milk it. > **""What is your name? Milk it.""**"
"12832","Abū Ḥadrad, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ asked who would drive these camels of theirs, or who would convey them, so a man stood and asked his name, and he said so-and-so, so he told him to sit, then another stood and said he would, and asked his name, and he said Nājiyah, so he said he was for it and told him to drive them. > **""What is your name? You are for it, so drive them.""**"
"12833","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAmrah reports from his uncle that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said not to combine his name and his kunya. > **""Do not combine my name and my kunya.""**"
"12834","Abū Ḥumayd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever is named with his name should not use his kunya. > **""Whoever is named with my name, let him not use my kunya.""**"
"12835","On the authority of Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave instruction concerning his blessed name and kunya. > **""Name yourselves with my name, but do not take my kunya.""**"
"12836","Muḥammad ibn Faḍāla al-Ẓafarī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived when he was two weeks old, he was brought to him, he wiped his head, commanded his naming, and he was later taken for Ḥajjat al-Wadāʿ at ten years of age. He lived long until his head grew white, except the spot touched by the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never grew white. > **""Name him with my name, and do not give him my kunya.""**"
"12837","Abū Ghaziyya al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade joining his name and his kunya together. > **""Do not combine my name and my kunya.""**"
"12838","ʿUbayd ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade joining his name with his kunya. > **""Do not combine my name and my kunya.""**"
"12839","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ rebuked those who give the name Muḥammad and then curse its bearer. > **""You name them Muḥammad and then you curse them!""**"
"12840","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command honour toward a child given the name Muḥammad. > **""When you name him Muḥammad, then do not beat him and do not deprive him.""**"
"12841","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā reported that ʿUmar رضي الله عنه saw the son of ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd, whose name was Muḥammad, while a man was saying to him, May Allah do such-and-such to you, O Muḥammad. So he named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, then sent for Banū Ṭalḥa, who were seven, their chief and eldest being Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥa, and changed their names. Muḥammad said, I remind you by Allah, O Commander of the Believers, for Muḥammad ﷺ himself named me. He then said, Rise, for there is no way against anything the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has named. > **""Rise, for there is no way against anything the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has named.""**"
"12842","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described ignorance in one who has three children yet names none of them Muḥammad. > **""Whoever has three born to him and does not name one of them Muḥammad has indeed acted ignorantly.""**"
"12843","Wāthila رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described ignorance in one granted three sons yet naming none Muḥammad. > **""Whoever has three sons born to him and does not name one of them Muḥammad has indeed acted ignorantly.""**"
"12844","ʿĪsā ibn Ṭalḥa reported from the wet-nurse of Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥa that when Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥa was born she brought him to the Prophet ﷺ. He asked what they had named him, they said Muḥammad, and he affirmed his name and kunya. Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥa ibn ʿUbayd Allāh رضي الله عنه was born in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who named him Muḥammad and gave him the kunya Abū al-Qāsim. > **""What have you named him? This is my name, and his kunya is Abū al-Qāsim.""**"
"12845","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named the most beloved names to Allah. > **""The most beloved names to Allah are ʿAbd Allāh, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and al-Ḥārith.""**"
"12846","Khaythama ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sabra reported that his father ʿAbd al-Raḥmān went with his grandfather to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ asked the name of his son, he said ʿAzīz, so the Prophet ﷺ ordered him not to call him ʿAzīz but to name him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, then stated the best of names. > **""Do not name him ʿAzīz, but name him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. Indeed the best of names are ʿAbd Allāh, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and al-Ḥārith.""**"
"12847","Khaythama reported in a variant that a boy was born to his grandfather, he named him ʿAzīz, then came to the Prophet ﷺ and said a boy was born to me. When asked his chosen name and answering ʿAzīz, the Prophet ﷺ corrected it. > **""What did you name him? No, rather he is ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.""**"
"12848","Khaythama reported from his father that his father's name in Jāhiliyya was ʿAzīz, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. > **""In Jāhiliyya my father's name was ʿAzīz, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.""**"
"12849","Khaythama ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reported from his father that he came with his father to the Prophet ﷺ as a boy. The Prophet ﷺ asked his father the boy's name, he said ʿAzīz, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered him to change it and stated the most beloved names to Allah. > **""Do not name him ʿAzīz, but name him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, for indeed the most beloved names to Allah are ʿAbd Allāh and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.""**"
"12850","Khaythama ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reported from his father, from his father, that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked his name. He answered ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, so he named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. In a similar variant, when he answered ʿAzīz, the Prophet ﷺ affirmed that might belongs to Allah al-ʿAzīz. > **""What is your name? Rather, you are ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.""**"
"12851","Sabra ibn Abī Sabra reported from his father that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked about his children. He mentioned so-and-so, so-and-so, and ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ renamed him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and described it among the most rightful and best names when naming. > **""What are your children? He is ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. He is among the most rightful of your names, or among the best of your names, if you name.""**"
"12852","Sabra ibn Abī Sabra reported that his father came to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked about his children. He said ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, Sabra, and al-Ḥārith, so the Prophet ﷺ forbade the name ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, commanded ʿAbd Allāh, stated the best names, and supplicated for his children, who remained in honour to this day. > **""Do not name him ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā, and name him ʿAbd Allāh, for indeed the best of names are ʿAbd Allāh, ʿUbayd Allāh, al-Ḥārith, and Hammām.""**"
"12853","Khaythama ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reported from his father that he entered upon the Prophet ﷺ, who asked his father whether this was his son, what his name was, and upon hearing al-Ḥubāb forbade it and named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. > **""What is his name? Do not name him al-Ḥubāb, for indeed al-Ḥubāb is a devil, but rather he is ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.""**"
"12854","Abū Zuhayr al-Thaqafī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded names expressing servitude to Allah when naming. > **""When you name, then give names of servitude.""**"
"12855","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned of Allah's severe anger against one who claims to be king of kings. > **""Allah's anger is severe against whoever claims that he is the king of kings.""**"
"12856","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man to name his servant or his son Ḥārith, Murra, Walīd, Ḥakam, Abū al-Ḥakam, Aflaḥ, Najīḥ, or Yasār, and stated which names are most beloved to Allah and which are most truthful. > **""The most beloved names to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, are those expressing servitude to Him, and the most truthful of names is Hammām.""**"
"12857","Burayda رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade naming with Kalb or Kulayb. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade that Kalb or Kulayb be used as a name.""**"
"12858","Muslim ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Azdī reported that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qurṭ al-Azdī رضي الله عنه came to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked his name. He said Shayṭān ibn Qurṭ, so the Prophet ﷺ renamed him. > **""What is your name? You are ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qurṭ.""**"
"12859","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qurṭ رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked his name. He said Shayṭān ibn Qurṭ, so the Prophet ﷺ renamed him. > **""What is your name? You are ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qurṭ.""**"
"12860","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ heard a man asking another man his name, he said Shihāb, so the Prophet ﷺ renamed him Hishām. > **""What is your name? You are Hishām.""**"
"12861","A man from Juhayna reported that the Prophet ﷺ heard him calling out, O Ḥarām, so he changed his address to its wholesome opposite. > **""O Ḥarām! O Ḥalāl.""**"
"12862","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a land called Ghudra and renamed it Khuḍra. > **""He passed by a land called Ghudra, so he named it Khuḍra.""**"
"12863","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ would change an ugly name when he heard it, and when he passed a village called ʿUfra he named it Khuḍra. > **""When the Prophet ﷺ heard an ugly name he changed it, so he passed a village called ʿUfra and named it Khuḍra.""**"
"12864","Bashīr ibn al-Khaṣāṣiyya رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ while his name was Zaḥm, and the Prophet ﷺ named him Bashīr. > **""His name was Zaḥm, so the Prophet ﷺ named him Bashīr.""**"
"12865","Al-Jahdama, the wife of Bashīr ibn al-Khaṣāṣiyya رضي الله عنها, reported that Bashīr's name had been Zaḥm, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him Bashīr. > **""Bashīr's name was Zaḥm, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him Bashīr.""**"
"12866","Hishām ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked his name. He said Shihāb, so the Prophet ﷺ renamed him Hishām. > **""What is your name? Rather, you are Hishām.""**"
"12867","ʿUtba ibn ʿAbd al-Sulamī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ would change a man's name when one came to him with a name he disliked. They came to him as seven men from Banū Sulaym, the eldest being al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriya, and pledged allegiance to him all together. > **""When a man with a name he disliked came to him, the Prophet ﷺ would change it.""**"
"12868","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported that when al-Ḥasan was born he named him Ḥamza, and when al-Ḥusayn was born he named him after his uncle Jaʿfar. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then summoned him and said he was commanded to change the names of these two sons. ʿAlī said Allah and His Messenger know best, so he named them Ḥasan and Ḥusayn. > **""I have been commanded to change the names of these two sons of mine.""**"
"12869","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported that when al-Ḥasan was born he named him Ḥarb, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and asked to see his son and what they had named him, and renamed him Ḥasan. The same occurred with al-Ḥusayn, then with the third child, whom he named Muḥsin, and he stated he named them by the names of the sons of Hārūn. In another wording the names were Jabr, Jubayr, and Mujabbar. > **""Show me my son. What have you named him? Rather, he is Ḥasan. Rather, he is Ḥusayn. Rather, he is Muḥsin. I have named them by the names of the sons of Hārūn: Bishr, Bashīr, and Mubashshir.""**"
"12870","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported that when al-Ḥasan was born he named him Ḥarb, loving the kunya Abū Ḥarb. The Prophet ﷺ came, performed taḥnīk, asked what they had named his son, and named him al-Ḥasan. Then al-Ḥusayn was born, he again named him Ḥarb, and the Prophet ﷺ likewise renamed him al-Ḥusayn. > **""What have you named my son? He is al-Ḥasan. He is al-Ḥusayn.""**"
"12871","Salmān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said he named al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn by the names of the two sons of Hārūn. > **""I named them both, meaning al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, by the names of the two sons of Hārūn: Shibr and Shubayr.""**"
"12872","Rāʾiṭa bint Muslim reported from her father رضي الله عنهما that he was present with the Prophet ﷺ at Ḥunayn. He asked his name, he said Ghurāb, so he named him Muslim. > **""What is your name? You are Muslim.""**"
"12873","Saʿīd ibn Yarbūʿ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked which of them was greater. He replied, You are greater and better than me, while I am older in age. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him Saʿīd and declared that al-Ṣirm had departed, meaning that had been his former name. > **""Which of us is greater? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him Saʿīd and said: al-Ṣirm has departed.""**"
"12874","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reported that his name had been ʿAbd ʿAmr, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. > **""My name was ʿAbd ʿAmr, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named me ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.""**"
"12875","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Jazʾ رضي الله عنه reported that a man who had come to the Prophet ﷺ and accepted Islam died as a stranger. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood at the grave and asked his name, he said al-ʿĀṣī, then asked Ibn ʿUmar his name and he said al-ʿĀṣī, then asked al-ʿĀṣī his name and he said al-ʿĀṣī. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then declared them servants of Allah, ordered them to lower the deceased, and when they left the grave their names had been changed. > **""What is your name? You are servants of Allah, descend.""**"
"12876","ʿUtba ibn ʿAbd رضي الله عنه reported that people came intending to change their names. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him, he called him while he was a young boy, asked his name, he said ʿAtala ibn ʿAbd, so the Prophet ﷺ named him ʿUtba ibn ʿAbd, asked to see his sword, drew it, saw it thin and weak, and advised him not to strike with it but to thrust. > **""What is your name? Rather, you are ʿUtba ibn ʿAbd. Show me your sword. Do not strike with this, but thrust with it a thrusting.""**"
"12877","ʿUtba رضي الله عنه reported that when he pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, he asked his name. He said Shayba, so the Prophet ﷺ renamed him. > **""What is your name? You are ʿUtba ibn ʿAbd.""**"
"12878","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ asked a man his name. He said Nuʿm, so the Prophet ﷺ renamed him ʿAbd Allāh. > **""What is your name? Rather, you are ʿAbd Allāh.""**"
"12879","ʿAlī ibn Jahm al-Balawī reported from his father that they came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on Friday. He asked who they were, they said Banū ʿAbd Manāf, so he renamed them. > **""Who are you? You are Banū ʿAbd Allāh.""**"
"12880","Al-Ḥakam ibn Saʿīd رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ to pledge allegiance. He asked his name, he said al-Ḥakam, so the Prophet ﷺ renamed him ʿAbd Allāh. > **""What is your name? Rather, you are ʿAbd Allāh.""**"
"12881","Al-Ḥakam ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ, greeted him, and was asked his name. He said al-Ḥakam, so the Prophet ﷺ said he was ʿAbd Allāh, and he accepted it. > **""What is your name? You are ʿAbd Allāh. I am ʿAbd Allāh, O Messenger of Allah.""**"
"12882","Qayyūm, also known as Abū ʿUbayd, reported that he was with Abū Rāshid al-Azdī before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he came as a delegate. The Prophet ﷺ asked Abū Rāshid his name, he said ʿAbd al-ʿUzzā Abū Muʿāwiya, so he renamed him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Abū Rāshid. He then asked who was with him, he said his freedman, asked his name, he said Qayyūm, so he renamed him ʿAbd al-Qayyūm Abū ʿUbayda. > **""What is your name? No, rather you are ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Abū Rāshid. Who is this with you? What is his name? No, rather he is ʿAbd al-Qayyūm Abū ʿUbayda.""**"
"12883","Abū Qirṣāfa رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he had any dependants, he said a brother, and was told to bring him. He brought his small young brother, who fled when near the Prophet ﷺ, so he seized him, clasped his hands and feet, brought him to the Prophet ﷺ, and he accepted Islam and pledged allegiance. His name had been Maysam, so the Prophet ﷺ renamed him Muslim, and Abū Qirṣāfa affirmed it. > **""Do you have any offspring? Bring him. What is his name, O Abū Qirṣāfa? Rather, his name is Muslim. Muslim is with you, O Messenger of Allah.""**"
"12884","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām رضي الله عنه reported that his name in Jāhiliyya had been Ghaylān, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him ʿAbd Allāh. > **""My name in Jāhiliyya was Ghaylān, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named me ʿAbd Allāh.""**"
"12885","Aṣram رضي الله عنه reported that he said, O Messenger of Allah, I have purchased a slave, so supplicate for blessing for him and name him. He asked his own name, he said Aṣram, so he named him Zurʿa, asked what he wanted for the slave, he said a cultivator, so he named him ʿĀṣim. > **""What is your name? Rather, you are Zurʿa. What do you want for him? So he is ʿĀṣim.""**"
"12886","Usāma ibn Akhdarī رضي الله عنه reported that a man from Banū Shuqra called Aṣram was among the group who came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He brought an Abyssinian slave he had purchased in that land and asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to name him and bless him. He asked his own name, he said Aṣram, so he named him Zurʿa, asked what he wanted for him, he said a herdsman, so he named him ʿĀṣim, and the Prophet ﷺ closed his fist. > **""What is your name? You are Zurʿa. What do you want for him? He is ʿĀṣim.""**"
"12887","Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ named him Muṭāʿ, told him he would be obeyed among his people, told him to go to his companions, carried him upon a piebald horse, gave him the banner, and declared safety for whoever entered beneath it. > **""I named him Muṭāʿ. You are obeyed among your people. Proceed to your companions. Whoever enters beneath this banner of yours is safe from punishment.""**"
"12888","Abū Juḥayfa رضي الله عنه reported that he saw the Prophet ﷺ when a garment was brought from the fuller with the word Shayṭān written upon it. He ordered it effaced and sought refuge in Allah from Satan. > **""I seek refuge in Allah from Satan.""**"
"12889","Abū Bakr ibn Abī Maryam reported from his father, from his grandfather, that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said a daughter was born to him that night. The Prophet ﷺ said Sūrat Maryam had been revealed to him that night and commanded her naming, so she was called Maryam. > **""This night Sūrat Maryam was revealed to me. Name her Maryam.""**"
"12890","Suhayl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that a man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was named Aswad, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him Abyaḍ. > **""A man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was named Aswad, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him Abyaḍ.""**"
"12891","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samura رضي الله عنه reported that his name in Jāhiliyya had been ʿAbd Kulāl, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. > **""My name in Jāhiliyya was ʿAbd Kulāl, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named me ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.""**"
"12892","ʿIkrima reported that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samura's name had been ʿAbd Kallūb, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named him ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. He passed by him while performing ablution and called him, then the Prophet of Allah ﷺ warned him never to seek leadership, for one who seeks it and is given preference is entrusted to it, while one who does not seek it is aided in it. > **""Come, O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. Do not seek leadership, for if you seek it and are given it, you will be entrusted to it, and if you do not seek it, you will be aided in it.""**"
"12893","Samura رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say to them that the name of the believing man in the Books is al-Karm, because of Allah's honouring him over creation, while what they call the grapes in the garden al-Karm is properly al-Ḥafr, for the man himself is al-Karm. > **""Indeed the name of the believing man in the Books is al-Karm, because of what Allah has honoured him with over creation. You call what is in the wall of grapes al-Karm; rather, its name is al-Ḥafr, and the man is al-Karm.""**"
"12894","Ḥanẓala ibn Ḥidhyam رضي الله عنه reported that it pleased the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to call a man by the name most beloved to him and by his most beloved kunya. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ liked to call a man by his most beloved name to him and his most beloved kunya.""**"
"12895","Yazīd ibn Jāriya al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reported that he was with the Prophet ﷺ, and when he did not remember a man's name he would address him as son of ʿAbd Allāh. > **""When he did not remember a man's name, he said: O son of ʿAbd Allāh.""**"
"12896","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him the kunya Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, though no child had been born to him. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him the kunya Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, though no child had been born to him.""**"
"12897","Ḥamza ibn ʿUmar al-Aslamī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him the kunya Abū Ṣāliḥ. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him the kunya Abū Ṣāliḥ.""**"
"12898","Abū al-Ward رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him as a ruddy man and gave him his kunya. > **""You are Abū al-Ward.""**"
"12899","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sneezed he would cover his face and lower his voice. > **""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sneezed, he covered his face and lowered his voice.""**"
"12900","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar Dhū al-Janāḥayn رضي الله عنهما reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sneezed he would praise Allah, it would be said, May Allah have mercy on you, and he would reply with guidance and rectification. > **""May Allah have mercy on you. May Allah guide you and set right your state.""**"
"12901","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that they were sitting with the Prophet ﷺ, he sneezed and praised Allah, they said, May Allah have mercy on you, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied with guidance and rectification. > **""May Allah have mercy on you. May Allah guide you and set right your state.""**"
"12902","ʿAbd Allāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach them, when one of them sneezed, to answer his sneeze. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us, when one of us sneezed, to answer his sneeze.""**"
"12903","ʿĀʾisha رضي الله عنها reported that a man sneezed before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and asked what to say, those present asked what to say to him, and he then asked what to reply to them. The Prophet ﷺ taught each of the three responses in turn. > **""Say: Praise be to Allah. Say: May Allah have mercy on you. Say to them: May Allah guide you and set right your state.""**"
"12904","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught them that when one of them sneezes he should say praise to Allah, Lord of the worlds, those with him should invoke mercy upon him, and he should then invoke forgiveness for himself and them. > **""When one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds. When he says that, let those with him say: May Allah have mercy on you. When he says that, let him say: May Allah forgive me and you.""**"
"12905","Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught the sneezer to praise Allah in every condition, those around him to invoke mercy, and him to reply with guidance and rectification for those around him. > **""When a man sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah in every condition, and let those around him say: May Allah have mercy on you, and let him say to those around him: May Allah guide you and set right your state.""**"
"12906","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that when one of them sneezes and says praise be to Allah, the angels say Lord of the worlds, and when he says Lord of the worlds, the angels invoke mercy upon him. > **""When one of you sneezes and says: Praise be to Allah, the angels say: Lord of the worlds, and when he says: Lord of the worlds, the angels say: May Allah have mercy on you.""**"
"12907","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ taught that when one of them sneezes he should say praise be to Allah in every condition, it should be said to him, May Allah have mercy on you, and he should reply with forgiveness for them and him. > **""When one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah in every condition, and let it be said to him: May Allah have mercy on you, and let him say: May Allah forgive us and you.""**"
"12908","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that when one of them sneezes he should praise Allah, those with him should invoke mercy, and he should reply with guidance and rectification. > **""When one of you sneezes, let him say: Praise be to Allah, and let those with him say: May Allah have mercy on you, and let him say: May Allah guide you and set right your state.""**"
"12909","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised protection from flank pain and never complaining of a molar to one who hastens the sneezer with praise. > **""Whoever hastens the sneezer with praise is protected from pain of the flank and will never complain of his molar.""**"
"12910","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that two men sneezed in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ, one of them nobler in rank than the other. The nobler man sneezed and did not praise Allah, so the Prophet ﷺ did not respond to him with tashmīt, while the other man sneezed and praised Allah, so the Prophet ﷺ responded to him. The nobler man then said that he had sneezed before him and received no response, while this other man had received a response, whereupon he explained the difference between remembrance and forgetfulness. > **""Indeed this one remembered Allah, so I remembered him, while you forgot Allah, so I forgot you.""**"
"12911","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that ʿĀmir ibn al-Ṭufayl came to the Prophet ﷺ in Madīnah and disputed with him in a raised voice, while Thābit ibn Qays رضي الله عنه stood over the Prophet ﷺ with his sword and ordered ʿĀmir to lower his voice. Harsh words passed between ʿĀmir and Thābit, each threatening the other. Then a nephew of ʿĀmir sneezed and praised Allah, so the Prophet ﷺ responded to him, while ʿĀmir sneezed without praising Allah and received no response. When ʿĀmir objected that a boy had been answered and he had been left, the Prophet ﷺ gave the reason. > **""Indeed this one praised Allah.""**"
"12912","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when a sneezer sneezes, he should be answered with tashmīt even from beyond seven seas, and that whoever answers a sneezer is freed from pleurisy and from pain of the molars and the ears. > **""When the sneezer sneezes, respond to him with tashmīt, even from beyond seven seas, and whoever responds to a sneezer, pleurisy and pain of the molars and ears depart from him.""**"
"12913","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever is narrating a report and sneezes at that moment, then it is true. > **""Whoever narrates a report and then sneezes in the course of it, it is true.""**"
"12914","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the truest speech is that at which sneezing occurs. > **""The truest speech is that at which sneezing occurs.""**"
"12915","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that everything has a chief, and that the chief of sittings is that which faces the qiblah. > **""Everything has a chief, and the chief of sittings is that which faces the qiblah.""**"
"12916","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the most honourable of sittings are those faced toward the qiblah. > **""The most honourable of sittings are those faced toward the qiblah.""**"
"12917","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that everything has an honour, and that the most honourable of sittings are those faced toward the qiblah. > **""Everything has an honour, and the most honourable of sittings are those faced toward the qiblah.""**"
"12918","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the best of sittings are the most spacious of them. > **""The best of sittings are the most spacious of them.""**"
"12919","Muṣʿab ibn Shaybah, reporting from his father رضي الله عنه, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when one of you reaches the end of a gathering, if room is made for him he should sit, and otherwise he should look for the widest place he sees and sit there. > **""When one of you reaches the gathering, if room is made for him then let him sit, and otherwise let him look for the widest place he sees and sit there.""**"
"12920","Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd رضي الله عنه said that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that part of humility is contentment with the lower portion of the gathering rather than its honoured front. > **""Part of humility is contentment with the lower portion of the honoured gathering.""**"
"12921","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said there is no man who comes to a people and they make room for him until he is pleased, except that it becomes binding upon Allah to please them. > **""There is no man who comes to a people and they make room for him until he is pleased, except that it becomes binding upon Allah to please them.""**"
"12922","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said to beware of these madhābiḥ, meaning the maḥārīb, which are the foremost seats of gatherings. > **""Beware of these slaughter-places, meaning the prayer-niches, that is the foremost seats of gatherings.""**"
"12923","Abū Umāmah ibn Thaʿlabah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to sit in the posture of qurayfiṣāʾ, crouched upon his feet. The Prophet ﷺ used to sit crouched in the posture known as qurayfiṣāʾ."
"12924","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat by the Kaʿbah with his legs drawn together and set upright, and he wrapped them with his hands in the posture of iḥtibāʾ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat by the Kaʿbah with his legs drawn together and raised, enclosing them with his hands."
"12925","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat, he would set his knees upright and wrap them with his hands in iḥtibāʾ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he sat, would raise his knees and enclose them with his hands."
"12926","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that a man should not be made to rise from his seat for another man, but rather room should be made, and Allah will make room for you. > **""Let no man rise for another man from his seat, but make room, and Allah will make room for you.""**"
"12927","Abū ʿIyāḍ, reporting from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ رضي الله عنهم, reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade sitting between the bright sun and the shade, and said that it is the sitting-place of Satan. > **""Do not sit between the sun and the shade, for it is the sitting-place of Satan.""**"
"12928","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade a man to sit down between the shade and the sun. The Prophet ﷺ forbade a man to sit between the shade and the sun."
"12929","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not sit in a dark house unless a lamp was lit therein. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not remain seated in a dark house without a lamp being lit in it."
"12930","Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said not to wipe away the earth in contempt, for it is dutiful toward you. > **""Do not wipe yourselves with the earth, for it is dutiful toward you.""**"
"12931","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the righteous companion is like the perfume-seller: even if he gives you none of his perfume, its fragrance clings to you; and the evil companion is like the blacksmith: even if he does not burn your clothes, his smoke clings to you. > **""The likeness of the righteous companion is that of the perfume-seller: if he does not give you of his perfume, its fragrance still clings to you; and the likeness of the evil companion is that of the blacksmith: if he does not burn your clothes, his smoke still clings to you.""**"
"12932","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that a man should not sit between a man and his son in a gathering. > **""Let no man sit between a man and his son in the gathering.""**"
"12933","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said a man has the greatest right to the front of his mount and to his seat when he returns to it. > **""A man has the greatest right to the front of his mount and to his seat when he returns to it.""**"
"12934","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade one man to take the place of another man in his seat, and said that when he returns, he has the greatest right to it. > **""When he returns, he has the greatest right to it.""**"
"12935","Abū Shurayḥ ibn ʿAmr al-Khuzāʿī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to beware of sitting upon the roadways, and whoever sits upon a roadway must give it its due. When asked what its due is, he mentioned lowering the gaze, returning the greeting, enjoining good, and forbidding evil. > **""Beware of sitting upon the roadways, and whoever sits upon a roadway must give it its due: lowering the gaze, returning the greeting, enjoining good, and forbidding evil.""**"
"12936","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ came to a gathering of the Anṣār in which a group of them were present and greeted them, and they returned the greeting, but the Prophet ﷺ disliked that sitting for them. They said it was a sitting their forefathers had held in ignorance and they wished to maintain it and sit therein. He replied that if they insisted upon doing so, they must return the greeting, lower their gazes, and guide the path. > **""If you refuse but to do so, then return the greeting, lower your gazes, and guide the path.""**"
"12937","ʿUmar رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to beware of sitting in the roadways, and if you must do so, then give the road its due. When asked what its due is, he said lowering the gaze and returning the greeting, and the narrator thought he also said guiding the stray. > **""Beware of sitting in the roadways, and if you must do so, then give the road its due: lowering the gaze, returning the greeting, and guiding the stray.""**"
"12938","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said not to sit in the roadside gatherings, and if you must do so, then return the greeting, lower the gazes, guide the path, and help with heavy loads. > **""Do not sit in the gatherings, and if you must do so, then return the greeting, lower the gazes, guide the path, and help with loads.""**"
"12939","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه reported that the people of al-ʿĀliyah said that they could not do without sittings, whereupon he said to fulfil the due of sittings. When asked what the due of sittings is, he said much remembrance of Allah, guiding the path, and lowering the gazes. > **""Fulfil the due of sittings: much remembrance of Allah, guiding the path, and lowering the gazes.""**"
"12940","Waḥshī ibn Ḥarb رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that they might after him conquer great cities and take sittings in their markets, and when that occurs they must return the greeting, lower their gazes, guide the blind, and aid the wronged. > **""Perhaps after me you will conquer great cities and take sittings in their markets; when that occurs, return the greeting, lower your gazes, guide the blind, and aid the wronged.""**"
"12941","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Sodom and the villages around it were not destroyed until they began to use the siwāk and chew gum openly in gatherings. > **""Sodom and the villages around it were not destroyed until they used the siwāk and chewed gum in gatherings.""**"
"12942","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said whoever steps over the circle of a people without their permission is disobedient. > **""Whoever steps over the circle of a people without their permission is disobedient.""**"
"12943","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said there is no Muslim who looks at the charms of a woman and then lowers his gaze, except that Allah brings forth for him worship in which he finds sweetness. > **""There is no Muslim who looks at the charms of a woman and then lowers his gaze, except that Allah brings forth for him worship in which he finds its sweetness.""**"
"12944","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said to him that he has a treasure in Paradise and is its two-horned possessor in eminence, so he should not follow one glance with another, for only the first is permitted to him and the latter is not. > **""O ʿAlī, you have a treasure in Paradise, and you are its two-horned one, so do not follow one glance with another, for only the first is yours and the latter is not yours.""**"
"12945","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said they must lower their gazes, guard their private parts, and keep their faces upright, or else uncover their faces in disgrace. > **""You shall lower your gazes, guard your private parts, and keep your faces upright, or else your faces will be uncovered.""**"
"12946","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, conveying from Allah, that the glance is a poisoned arrow among the arrows of Iblīs, and whoever abandons it out of fear of Him, He will replace it with faith in whose heart he finds sweetness. > **""The glance is a poisoned arrow among the arrows of Iblīs; whoever abandons it out of fear of Me, I shall replace it for him with faith in which he finds sweetness in his heart.""**"
"12947","Saʿīd al-Maqburī reported that he sat beside Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما while a man was speaking to him, and he entered between them. Ibn ʿUmar struck his hand upon his chest and said that he should have known the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when two men are in private counsel, one should not sit with them until they permit him. > **""When two men are in private counsel, do not sit with them until they permit you.""**"
"12948","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما was once seen in private counsel with a man, and another man entered between them, whereupon a report similar to the prohibition of sitting with two in counsel without their permission was mentioned. Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما was seen conversing privately with a man when another man intruded between them, and the same prohibition against joining two in private talk without permission was recalled."
"12949","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that it is not lawful to marry a woman upon the divorce of another, nor lawful for a man to outbid his companion until he abandons it, nor lawful for three men in a desert land that two of them whisper to the exclusion of their companion. > **""It is not lawful to marry a woman upon the divorce of another, nor lawful for a man to sell over the sale of his companion until he leaves it, nor lawful for three men in a barren land that two whisper to the exclusion of their companion.""**"
"12950","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when there are three, two should not whisper to the exclusion of their companion. > **""When there are three, let not two whisper to the exclusion of their companion.""**"
"12951","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to forbid that when there are three men, two of them should whisper to the exclusion of the other. The Prophet ﷺ used to forbid that when three men are together, two of them should whisper to the exclusion of the third."
"12952","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that two should not whisper to the exclusion of the third, for that harms the believer, and Allah dislikes harm to the believer. > **""Let not two whisper to the exclusion of the third, for that harms the believer, and Allah dislikes harm to the believer.""**"
"12953","ʿUmayr ibn Ḥabīb ibn Khumāshah رضي الله عنه, who had reached the Prophet ﷺ near the time of his maturity, counselled his son saying that they must beware of sitting with fools, for their company is a disease: whoever forbears toward the fool will rejoice, whoever answers him will regret, and whoever is not content with the little harm that comes from the fool will have to be content with much. ʿUmayr ibn Ḥabīb ibn Khumāshah رضي الله عنه advised his children to avoid the company of fools, for it is a disease, and explained that forbearance toward the fool brings joy while engaging him brings regret."
"12954","Sulaym, the freedman of Banī Layth, who was early and had met Usāmah ibn Zayd and Marwān, reported that Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam passed by Usāmah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنهما while he was praying. Marwān came to him, whereupon Usāmah said that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that Allah does not love every foul and obscene-mouthed person. > **""Indeed Allah does not love every foul and obscene-mouthed person.""**"
"12955","ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUtbah reported that he saw Usāmah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنهما supplicating by the chamber of ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, when Marwān came and spoke harshly to him. Usāmah replied that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that Allah the Exalted detests the foul and obscene-tongued person. > **""Indeed Allah the Exalted detests the foul and obscene-tongued person.""**"
"12956","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that the meanest of the morals of the believer is obscenity. The vilest of the character of the believer is foul speech."
"12957","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when the night of mid-Shaʿbān comes, Allah the Blessed and Exalted descends to the lowest heaven and forgives His servants, except the idolater and the one who harbours enmity against his brother. > **""When the night of mid-Shaʿbān comes, Allah the Blessed and Exalted descends to the lowest heaven and forgives His servants, except the idolater and the one who harbours hatred against his brother.""**"
"12958","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when the night of mid-Shaʿbān comes, Allah forgives His servants except the idolater and the harbourer of enmity. > **""When the night of mid-Shaʿbān comes, Allah forgives His servants, except the idolater and the harbourer of enmity.""**"
"12959","ʿAwf ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah the Blessed and Exalted looks upon His creation on the night of mid-Shaʿbān and forgives them all, except the idolater and the harbourer of enmity. > **""Allah the Blessed and Exalted looks upon His creation on the night of mid-Shaʿbān and forgives them all, except the idolater and the harbourer of enmity.""**"
"12960","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said Allah looks upon all His creation on the night of mid-Shaʿbān and forgives all His creation, except the idolater and the harbourer of enmity. > **""Allah looks upon all His creation on the night of mid-Shaʿbān and forgives all His creation, except the idolater and the harbourer of enmity.""**"
"12961","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah looks upon His creation on the night of mid-Shaʿbān and forgives His servants except for two: the harbourer of enmity and the killer of a soul. > **""Allah looks upon His creation on the night of mid-Shaʿbān and forgives His servants, except for two: the harbourer of enmity and the killer of a soul.""**"
"12962","Abū Thaʿlabah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said Allah looks upon His servants on the night of mid-Shaʿbān and forgives the believers, grants respite to the disbelievers, and leaves the people of spite in their spite until they abandon it. > **""Allah looks upon His servants on the night of mid-Shaʿbān and forgives the believers, grants respite to the disbelievers, and leaves the people of spite in their spite until they abandon it.""**"
"12963","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said the deeds of the children of Ādam are presented every Monday and every Thursday, so He shows mercy to the merciful and forgives the seekers of forgiveness, then leaves the people of spite in their spite. > **""The deeds of the children of Ādam are presented every Monday and every Thursday, so He shows mercy to the merciful and forgives the seekers of forgiveness, then leaves the people of spite in their spite.""**"
"12964","Usāmah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said deeds are presented to Allah on Monday and Thursday, and Allah forgives except for two quarrelling persons and the severer of kinship. > **""Deeds are presented to Allah on Monday and Thursday, and Allah forgives except for two who harbour enmity and the severer of kinship.""**"
"12965","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the records of the people of the earth are copied into the records of the people of heaven every Monday and Thursday, and every Muslim who associates nothing with Allah is forgiven, except a man between whom and his brother there is enmity. > **""The records of the people of the earth are copied into the records of the people of heaven every Monday and Thursday, and every Muslim who associates nothing with Allah is forgiven, except a man between whom and his brother there is enmity.""**"
"12966","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there are no two Muslims except that between them is a veil from Allah, and when one of them speaks a word of abandonment to his companion, he rends the veil of Allah. > **""There are no two Muslims except that between them is a veil from Allah, and when one of them speaks a word of abandonment to his companion, he rends the veil of Allah.""**"
"12967","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said deeds are presented on Monday and Thursday: whoever seeks forgiveness is forgiven, whoever repents is accepted, while the people of grudges are left in their grudges until they repent. > **""Deeds are presented on Monday and Thursday; whoever seeks forgiveness is forgiven, whoever repents is accepted, while the people of grudges are left in their grudges until they repent.""**"
"12968","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it is not lawful for a Muslim to abandon his brother beyond three days. > **""It is not lawful for a Muslim to abandon his brother beyond three days.""**"
"12969","Hishām ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it is not lawful for a Muslim to abandon a Muslim beyond three nights, for the two remain turned away from truth while upon their severance. The first of them to greet precedes by two thousand expiations, and if he greets and the other does not accept, the angels return it upon him while Satan returns it upon the other. If both die upon their severance, they will never enter Paradise together ever. > **""It is not lawful for a Muslim to abandon a Muslim beyond three nights, for they remain turned away from truth while upon their severance; and the first of them to greet precedes by two thousand expiations. If he greets and the other does not accept his greeting, the angels return it upon him and Satan returns it upon the other, and if they die upon their severance they will never enter Paradise together ever.""**"
"12970","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that if two men entered Islam and then abandoned each other, one of them would be outside Islam until he returns, meaning the wrongdoer among them. If two men who had entered Islam then abandoned one another, the wrongdoer among them would stand outside its protection until he returns."
"12971","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it is not lawful for a Muslim to abandon his brother beyond three days. > **""It is not lawful for a Muslim to abandon his brother beyond three days.""**"
"12972","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that two men who have entered Islam do not abandon one another except that one of them exits from it until he returns to what he left, and his return is that he comes to him and greets him. If two Muslims who had entered Islam abandon one another, one of them leaves its fold until he returns, and his return is to come to the other and greet him."
"12973","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it is not lawful for a believer to abandon his brother beyond three days. > **""It is not lawful for a believer to abandon his brother beyond three days.""**"
"12974","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said abandonment beyond three days is not lawful. If the two meet and one greets the other and the other returns the greeting, both share in the reward. If the other refuses to return the greeting, this one is freed of the sin and the other bears it. He feared that if they die while mutually abandoning, they will not gather together in Paradise. > **""Abandonment beyond three days is not lawful; if they meet and one greets the other and the other returns the greeting, both share in the reward. If the other refuses to return the greeting, this one is freed of the sin and the other bears it, and I fear that if they die while mutually abandoning, they will not gather together in Paradise.""**"
"12975","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said not to envy one another nor turn backs upon one another, but to be servants of Allah as brothers. It is not lawful for a Muslim to abandon his brother beyond three days, with the two meeting and each turning away, and the one who begins with greeting hastens toward Paradise. > **""Do not envy one another, do not turn your backs upon one another, and be servants of Allah as brothers. It is not lawful for a Muslim to abandon his brother beyond three days, with the two meeting and each turning away, and the one who begins with greeting hastens toward Paradise.""**"
"12976","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said not to turn backs nor sever ties, but to be servants of Allah as brothers. The abandonment of two believers is three days; if they speak, then well, otherwise Allah the Exalted turns away from them until they speak. > **""Do not turn your backs upon one another, do not sever ties, and be servants of Allah as brothers. The abandonment of two believers is three days; if they speak then well, otherwise Allah the Exalted turns away from them until they speak.""**"
"12977","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said there is no Monday nor Thursday except that deeds are raised therein, save for the two who abandon one another. > **""There is no Monday nor Thursday except that deeds are raised therein, save for the two who abandon one another.""**"
"12978","Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever abandons his brother beyond three days is in the Fire, unless Allah overtakes him with His mercy. > **""Whoever abandons his brother beyond three days is in the Fire, unless Allah overtakes him with His mercy.""**"
"12979","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said he would tell of the affairs of men and their differences. One man is swift to anger and swift to return, so it is even, neither for him nor against him. One man is slow to anger and swift to return, so it is for him and not against him. One man demands what is his and pays what is upon him, so it is neither for him nor against him. One man demands what is his yet delays the people in what is upon him, so it is against him and not for him. > **""A man is swift to anger and swift to return, so it is even, neither for him nor against him. A man is slow to anger and swift to return, so it is for him and not against him. A man demands what is his and pays what is upon him, so it is neither for him nor against him. A man demands what is his and delays the people in what is upon him, so it is against him and not for him.""**"
"12980","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the best of his nation are its sharp-tempered ones who, when they grow angry, return. > **""The best of my nation are its sharp-tempered ones who, when they grow angry, return.""**"
"12981","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a people lifting a stone, and asked what they were doing. They said they were lifting a stone seeking strength. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether he should not guide them to one stronger than them, namely the one who masters himself at anger. > **""Shall I not guide you to one stronger than them? The one who masters himself at the time of anger.""**"
"12982","Anas رضي الله عنه reported in another narration that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a people wrestling and asked what this was. They said that such-and-such a champion throws everyone he wrestles. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he should not guide them to one stronger than him: a man wronged by another who swallows his rage, thereby overcoming him and his own Satan and the Satan of his companion. > **""Shall I not guide you to one stronger than him? A man wronged by another who swallows his rage, thereby overcoming him and his own Satan and the Satan of his companion.""**"
"12983","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever repels his anger, Allah will repel His punishment from him, and whoever guards his tongue, Allah will veil his faults. > **""Whoever repels his anger, Allah will repel His punishment from him, and whoever guards his tongue, Allah will veil his faults.""**"
"12984","A man who witnessed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preaching reported that he asked whether they knew who the raqūb is. They said it is one without children. He said the complete raqūb is one who has children yet death takes them without sending any of them ahead in reward. He then asked who the destitute is. They said one without wealth. He said the complete destitute is one who has wealth yet dies without sending any of it ahead. Then the Prophet ﷺ asked what wrestling strength is. They said it is the champion. He said complete strength is that of a man who grows angry with intensified rage, reddened face, and bristling hair, yet overcomes his anger. > **""Do you know who the raqūb is? The complete raqūb is one who has children and then dies without sending any of them ahead. Do you know who the destitute is? The complete destitute is one who has wealth and then dies without sending any of it ahead. What is wrestling strength? Complete strength is that of a man who grows angry with intensified anger, reddened face, and bristling hair, yet overcomes his anger.""**"
"12985","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what keeps him far from the anger of Allah, mighty and exalted, and he replied that he should not become angry. > **""Do not become angry.""**"
"12986","Jāriya ibn Qudāma, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that a man said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to tell him a brief saying that he might retain, and he replied that he should not become angry. The man repeated his request several times, and each time he said to him that he should not become angry. > **""Do not become angry.""**"
"12987","Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reports from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that a man said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to counsel him, and he said to him not to become angry. The narrator said that he reflected upon what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said, and behold, anger gathers all evil together. > **""Do not become angry.""**"
"12988","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to tell him a brief saying that he might comprehend, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him not to become angry. He repeated his request twice, and each time the Prophet ﷺ answered him that he should not become angry. > **""Do not become angry.""**"
"12989","Abū Ṣāliḥ reports from one of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that he said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to teach him a deed that would admit him to Paradise without laying much upon him, and he said to him not to become angry. > **""Do not become angry.""**"
"12990","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, said that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to guide him to a deed that would admit him to Paradise, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him not to become angry, and Paradise would be his. > **""Do not become angry, and Paradise is yours.""**"
"12991","Sufyān ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Thaqafī, may Allah be pleased with him, said that he asked the Prophet ﷺ to tell him a brief and beneficial saying that he might retain, and the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said to him not to become angry. He kept asking him about it repeatedly, and the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said to him each time not to become angry. > **""Do not become angry.""**"
"12992","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever repels his anger, Allah will repel His punishment from him, and whoever guards his tongue, Allah will conceal his faults. > **""Whoever repels his anger, Allah will repel His punishment from him, and whoever guards his tongue, Allah will conceal his faults.""**"
"12993","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that they should teach, make matters easy and not make them difficult, and that when one of them becomes angry he should remain silent, repeating that when one of them becomes angry he should remain silent. > **""Teach, and make things easy and do not make them difficult, and when one of you becomes angry, let him remain silent.""**"
"12994","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that if one of them, when he becomes angry, were to say that he seeks refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan, his anger would depart from him. > **""If one of you becomes angry and says: I seek refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan, his anger will depart from him.""**"
"12995","Abū al-Aswad reports from Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, that he was giving water at a cistern when some people came, and he asked which of them would come to Abū Dharr and earn reward as great as counting the hairs of his head. A man said that he would, then he came to him at the cistern and jostled him. Abū Dharr was standing, so he sat down and then reclined. When he was asked why he had sat and then reclined, he said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had told them that when one of them becomes angry while standing he should sit, and if his anger departs, well and good, otherwise he should recline. > **""When one of you becomes angry while he is standing, let him sit; if his anger leaves him, well and good, otherwise let him recline.""**"
"12996","Muḥammad ibn ʿAṭiyya reported from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when the ruler blazes with rage, Satan gains mastery over him. > **""When the ruler blazes with rage, Satan gains mastery over him.""**"
"12997","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that there is a gate of Hell which none shall enter except one who relieves his rage at the cost of Allah's displeasure. He then mentioned the remainder of the ḥadīth, which belongs in the chapter describing Hell. > **""There is a gate of Hell which none shall enter except one who relieves his rage at the cost of Allah's displeasure.""**"
"12998","Abū Qatāda, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should not revile time, for Allah is the disposer of time. > **""Do not revile time, for Allah is time.""**"
"12999","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should not revile time, for Allah, mighty and exalted, has said that He is time, that the days and nights belong to Him, that He renews them and wears them out, and that He brings kings after kings. > **""Do not revile time, for Allah, mighty and exalted, has said: I am time; the days and nights belong to Me; I renew them and wear them out, and I bring kings after kings.""**"
"13000","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should not revile time, for Allah is the disposer of time. > **""Do not revile time, for Allah is time.""**"
"13001","Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should not revile night and day, nor the sun, the moon, or the wind, for they are a mercy to some people and a punishment to others. > **""Do not revile night and day, nor the sun, the moon, or the wind, for they are a mercy to some people and a punishment to others.""**"
"13002","Jarmūz al-Hujaymī, may Allah be pleased with him, said that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to counsel him, and he said that he counseled him never to be one who curses frequently. > **""I counsel you never to be one who curses.""**"
"13003","Abū Tamīma al-Hujaymī, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from a man of his tribe that the man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, or that he was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a man came to him and asked whether he was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, or whether he was Muḥammad. He replied that he was. The man asked to what he called, and he said that he called to Allah alone, who removes harm when one calls upon Him in distress, who causes growth for one when famine strikes and one calls upon Him, and who restores one's lost mount when one is in a barren land and calls upon Him. The man then embraced Islam and asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to counsel him, and he said to him never to revile anything, or any person, the narrator being uncertain of the exact word. The man said that since the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave him this counsel, he never reviled even a camel or a sheep. > **""I call to Allah alone, who removes harm from you when you call upon Him in distress, who causes growth for you when famine strikes and you call upon Him, and who restores to you your lost mount when you are in a barren land and call upon Him. Never revile anything.""**"
"13004","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that it is not fitting for those given to cursing to be among the most truthful saints. > **""Those given to cursing should not be among the most truthful.""**"
"13005","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the believer is neither one who stabs at honour, nor one who curses, nor one who speaks obscenely, nor one who is foul-mouthed. > **""The believer is neither one who stabs at honour, nor one who curses, nor one who speaks obscenely, nor one who is foul-mouthed.""**"
"13006","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that the believer is neither given to stabbing at honour nor to cursing. The narrator said that he never heard Ibn ʿUmar curse anyone at all, except for one man."
"13007","Karīz ibn Usāma, may Allah be pleased with him, who had come in a delegation to the Prophet ﷺ, said that it was said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to supplicate against the tribe of Banū ʿĀmir, and he replied that he had not been sent as one who curses. > **""I was not sent as one who curses.""**"
"13008","Abū al-Dardāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, once heard a man reviling another man in a raised voice, and he said that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that obscenity is baseness, and that ill treatment of those under one's authority is baseness. > **""Obscenity is baseness, and ill treatment of those under one's authority is baseness.""**"
"13009","Salama ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, said that whenever they saw a man cursing his brother, they considered that he had approached one of the doors of the major sins."
"13010","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that cursing the believer is like killing him. > **""Cursing the believer is like killing him.""**"
"13011","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported in a raised chain that reviling the Muslim is like one who overlooks destruction. > **""Reviling the Muslim is like one who overlooks destruction.""**"
"13012","ʿAmr ibn al-Nuʿmān ibn Muqarrin, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Prophet ﷺ came to a gathering of the Anṣār in which there was a man known for obscenity, and the Prophet ﷺ said that reviling the Muslim is wickedness and fighting him is disbelief. > **""Reviling the Muslim is wickedness, and fighting him is disbelief.""**"
"13013","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mughaffal, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that reviling the Muslim is wickedness and fighting him is disbelief. > **""Reviling the Muslim is wickedness, and fighting him is disbelief.""**"
"13014","Ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that there is no pair of Muslims but that a veil from Allah lies between them, and that when one of them speaks foully to his companion he tears his veil, and that when he says to him that he is a disbeliever, then one of the two has fallen into disbelief. > **""There is no pair of Muslims but that a veil from Allah lies between them; when one of them speaks foully to his companion he tears his veil, and when he says: O disbeliever, then one of the two has fallen into disbelief.""**"
"13015","Abū Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, said that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that no man accuses another man of wickedness or accuses him of disbelief but that it returns upon him, if his companion is not so. > **""No man accuses another man of wickedness, nor accuses him of disbelief, but that it returns upon him, if his companion is not so.""**"
"13016","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when a man says to his brother that he is a disbeliever, it is like killing him. > **""When a man says to his brother: O disbeliever, it is like killing him.""**"
"13017","Qays ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the worst form of unlawful excess is for a man to indulge at length in reviling his brother, and that among the greatest of major sins is for a man to revile his parents. They asked how he could revile them, O Messenger of Allah, and he said that he reviles another man's father, whereupon the other reviles his parents in return. > **""The worst form of unlawful excess is for a man to indulge at length in reviling his brother, and among the greatest of major sins is for a man to revile his parents. He reviles another man's father, whereupon he reviles his parents in return.""**"
"13018","Samura ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade them to revile, and said that if one of them must inevitably speak against his companion, he should not invent falsehood, nor revile his parents, nor revile his tribe, but if he knows it to be true he may say that he is miserly, or that he is cowardly, or that he is a liar, or that he is base. > **""If one of you must inevitably speak against his companion, let him not invent falsehood, nor revile his parents, nor revile his tribe; but if he knows it to be true, let him say: You are miserly, or you are cowardly, or you are a liar, or you are base.""**"
"13019","Al-ʿAyzār ibn Jarwal reports from a man among them called Abū ʿUmayr, who was a friend of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, that Ibn Masʿūd visited him among his household but did not find him. He sought permission to enter upon his household, greeted them, and asked for a drink. The mistress of the house sent a slave girl to fetch a drink from the neighbours, but she was delayed, so he cursed her. Then ʿAbd Allāh went out, and Abū ʿUmayr arrived and said to him that one like him should not be resented, asking why he had not greeted his brother's household, sat down, and taken some drink. He replied that he had done so, but the slave girl they had sent was delayed, either because the neighbours had no drink or because they withheld what they had, and when the servant was delayed he had cursed her. He said that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that when a curse is directed toward its target, if it finds a way to him it proceeds, but if not, it says that it was directed to such-and-such a person and found no path to him, whereupon it is told to return whence it came. He said that he feared the servant might have been excused, so that the curse would return and he would be its cause. > **""When a curse is directed toward its target, if it finds a way to him it proceeds; otherwise it says: My Lord, I was directed to such-and-such a person but found no path to him. It is then told: Return whence you came.""**"
"13020","Abū Mūsā, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that if he is able never to curse anything, he should do so, for when a curse leaves its utterer, if the one cursed deserves it, it strikes him, and if he does not deserve it, then if the curser deserves it, it returns upon him, and if neither deserves it, it strikes a Jew, a Christian, or a Magian. So if he is able never to curse anything at all, he should do so. > **""If you are able never to curse anything, then do so, for when a curse leaves its utterer, if the one cursed deserves it, it strikes him; if he does not deserve it, then if the curser deserves it, it returns upon him; and if neither deserves it, it strikes a Jew, a Christian, or a Magian.""**"
"13021","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Muqarrin al-Muzanī, may Allah be pleased with him, said that a man reviled another man in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the man who was reviled began to reply with words of peace. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that an angel was defending him, and that each time the other reviled him, the angel said to him that rather he himself was so and was more deserving of it, but that when he replied with words of peace, the angel said to him that rather he himself was more deserving of peace. > **""An angel defends you; each time he reviles you, he says to him: Rather you are so, and you are more deserving of it. Then when you said: Peace be upon you, he said: Rather you are more deserving of it.""**"
"13022","ʿIyāḍ ibn Ḥimār, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the sin of two men who revile one another for what they say lies upon the one who began, so long as the wronged party does not transgress, and that two men who revile one another are two devils who lie against one another and utter falsehood against one another. > **""The sin of two men who revile one another for what they say lies upon the one who began, so long as the wronged party does not transgress; and two men who revile one another are two devils who lie against one another and utter falsehood against one another.""**"
"13023","ʿIyāḍ, may Allah be pleased with him, said in another narration that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether there would be any harm upon him if he retaliated against a man of his tribe who reviled him although he was beneath him, and he mentioned the like of the preceding report. > **""The sin of two men who revile one another lies upon the one who began, so long as the wronged party does not transgress.""**"
"13024","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whatever two men who revile one another say, the blame lies upon the one who began, until the wronged party transgresses. > **""Whatever two men who revile one another say, the blame lies upon the one who began, until the wronged party transgresses.""**"
"13025","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to beware of quarrelling with people, for it buries honour and exposes faults. > **""Beware of quarrelling with people, for it buries honour and exposes faults.""**"
"13026","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, said that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that Allah does not love the ignorant elder, nor the unjust rich man, nor the arrogant poor man. > **""Allah does not love the ignorant elder, nor the unjust rich man, nor the arrogant poor man.""**"
"13027","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that no servant truly believes until his neighbour is safe from his harms, that whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should honour his guest, that whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should speak good or remain silent, that Allah, blessed and exalted, loves the rich, forbearing, and chaste man, and hates the obscene, wicked, persistently begging man. > **""No servant truly believes until his neighbour is safe from his harms; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should honour his guest; whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should speak good or remain silent; Allah loves the rich, forbearing, and chaste man, and hates the obscene, wicked, persistently begging man.""**"
"13028","Ziyād ibn ʿIlāqa said that al-Mughīra ibn Shuʿba, may Allah be pleased with him, spoke against ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, whereupon Zayd ibn Arqam, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him that he knew the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid them from reviling the dead, so why did he revile ʿAlī, may Allah have mercy upon him, when he had died. > **""You know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid us from reviling the dead, so why do you revile ʿAlī when he has died.""**"
"13029","Sahl ibn Saʿd, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should not revile Tubbaʿ, for he had embraced Islam. > **""Do not revile Tubbaʿ, for he had embraced Islam.""**"
"13030","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should not revile Tubbaʿ, for he had embraced Islam. > **""Do not revile Tubbaʿ, for he had embraced Islam.""**"
"13031","Ziyād ibn ʿIlāqa said that he heard a man in the presence of al-Mughīra ibn Shuʿba, may Allah be pleased with him, report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should not revile the dead, lest they harm the living thereby. > **""Do not revile the dead, lest you harm the living.""**"
"13032","Ṣakhr, may Allah be pleased with him, who had met the Prophet ﷺ, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should not revile the dead lest they harm the living, meaning unbelievers whose children had embraced Islam. > **""Do not revile the dead, lest you harm the living.""**"
"13033","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, reported in a raised chain that reviling the dead, and on one occasion he said the dead in the plural, is like one who overlooks destruction. > **""Reviling the dead is like one who overlooks destruction.""**"
"13034","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should not harm the living through the dead, meaning through reviling the dead. He added the report concerning the prohibition of reviling Abū Lahab, when his daughter complained to him ﷺ after she had embraced Islam that people said to her that she was the daughter of the enemy of Allah, whereupon he ﷺ said not to revile their dead lest they harm their living. > **""Do not harm the living through the dead. Do not revile our dead, lest you harm our living.""**"
"13035","ʿĀʾisha, may Allah be pleased with her, said that she was travelling with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when she cursed a camel belonging to her, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ ordered that it be sent back, saying that nothing accursed would accompany him. > **""Nothing accursed shall accompany me.""**"
"13036","ʿĀʾisha, may Allah be pleased with her, once mounted a camel and cursed it, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said to her not to ride it. > **""Do not ride it.""**"
"13037","Abū Hurayra, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey when a man cursed a she-camel, so he asked where the owner of the she-camel was. The man said that it was he, and he said to him to put her behind, for his supplication against her had been answered. > **""Where is the owner of the she-camel. Put her behind, for your supplication against her has been answered.""**"
"13038","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, said that a man travelled with the Prophet ﷺ and cursed his camel, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said to him that he should not travel with them upon an accursed camel. > **""O servant of Allah, do not travel with us upon an accursed camel.""**"
"13039","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that they were with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey when a man cursed a camel belonging to him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ ordered that it be set aside."
"13040","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, may Allah be pleased with him, said that a rooster crowed in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and a man reviled it, whereupon he forbade reviling the rooster, saying in another wording not to curse it nor revile it, for it calls to prayer. > **""Do not curse it nor revile it, for it calls to prayer.""**"
"13041","Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that a rooster crowed near the Prophet ﷺ and a man said that Allah should curse it, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him to stop, for it calls to prayer. > **""Stop; nay, it calls to prayer.""**"
"13042","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, said that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a flea bit a man and he cursed it, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ told him not to curse it, for it had awakened one of the Prophets for prayer. In another wording he said not to revile it, for it awakened one of the Prophets for the dawn prayer, and in another wording, when fleas were mentioned before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he said that they awaken one for prayer. > **""Do not curse it, for it awakened one of the Prophets for prayer.""**"
"13043","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, said that they halted at a resting place where fleas harmed them, so they reviled them, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told them not to revile them, for they were an excellent creature, since they had awakened them for the remembrance of Allah. > **""Do not revile them, for they are an excellent creature, since they awakened you for the remembrance of Allah.""**"
"13044","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that envy almost outruns the decree, and that need almost becomes disbelief. > **""Envy almost outruns the decree, and need almost becomes disbelief.""**"
"13045","Ḍamra ibn Thaʿlaba, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that people will remain well so long as they do not envy one another. > **""People will remain well so long as they do not envy one another.""**"
"13046","Ḥāritha ibn al-Nuʿmān, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that three things cleave to his nation: taking omens, envy, and evil suspicion. A man asked what removes them from one in whom they occur, O Messenger of Allah, and he said that when one envies he should seek Allah's forgiveness, that when one suspects he should not pursue verification, and that when one takes omens he should proceed. > **""When you envy, seek Allah's forgiveness; when you suspect, do not pursue verification; and when you take omens, proceed.""**"
"13047","Abū Ḥāzim said that they bought a house from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he sat at the door while dust grew thick. They said to him that they would take only their right and would not betray him, and he replied that he feared suspicion. > **""I fear suspicion.""**"
"13048","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, said that they were sitting with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he said that a man of Paradise would now appear before them. Then a man of the Anṣār appeared, his beard dripping from his ablution, holding his sandals in his left hand. On the next day the Prophet ﷺ said the like, and the same man appeared as on the first occasion. On the third day the Prophet ﷺ said the like again, and the same man appeared in his first state. When the Prophet ﷺ rose, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, followed the man and said that he had quarrelled with his father and sworn not to enter upon him for three nights, asking him to shelter him until they passed. The man agreed. Anas said that ʿAbd Allāh reported that he spent those three nights with him but never saw him rise for night prayer, except that when he stirred and turned upon his bed he remembered Allah, mighty and exalted, and uttered the takbīr until he rose for the dawn prayer. ʿAbd Allāh said that he never heard him say anything but good. When the three nights had passed and he had almost deemed his deeds insignificant, he said to him that there had been no anger or estrangement between him and his father, but that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say three times that a man of Paradise would appear before them, and he had appeared all three times, so he had wished to stay with him and observe his deeds in order to follow him, yet he had not seen him perform many deeds, and he asked what had raised him to what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had announced. He replied that it was nothing but what he had seen. When ʿAbd Allāh turned away, the man called him back and said that it was nothing but what he had seen, except that he found no deceit in himself toward any Muslim, nor did he envy anyone for any good Allah had granted him. ʿAbd Allāh said to him that this was what had raised him, and that it was what they could not bear. > **""A man of Paradise will now appear before you. I harbour no deceit in myself toward any Muslim, nor do I envy anyone for any good Allah has granted him.""**"
"13049","Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that a man of Paradise would enter upon them, whereupon Saʿd entered. He said this on three days, and each time Saʿd entered. > **""A man of Paradise will enter upon you.""**"
"13050","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that most of the people of Paradise are the simple-hearted, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ also said that many a weak and despised man, if he were to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil his oath. > **""Most of the people of Paradise are the simple-hearted. Many a weak and despised man, if he were to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil his oath.""**"
"13051","Abū Ayyūb, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him that he should guide him to a charity loved by Allah and His Messenger, namely to reconcile between people when they hate one another and their relations are corrupt. > **""O Abū Ayyūb, shall I not guide you to a charity loved by Allah and His Messenger. Reconcile between people when they hate one another and their relations are corrupt.""**"
"13052","Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said to Abū Ayyūb, may Allah be pleased with him, asking whether he should guide him to a trade. He replied that he should, and he said to him to join people together when their relations are corrupt, and to bring them near when they have grown distant. > **""Shall I not guide you to a trade. Join people together when their relations are corrupt, and bring them near when they have grown distant.""**"
"13053","Abū Umāma, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Abū Ayyūb ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, asking whether he should guide him to a deed pleasing to Allah and His Messenger. He replied that he should, and he said to him to reconcile between people when their relations are corrupt, and to bring them near when they have grown distant. > **""O Abū Ayyūb, shall I not guide you to a deed pleasing to Allah and His Messenger. Reconcile between people when their relations are corrupt, and bring them near when they have grown distant.""**"
"13054","Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, said that Aws and Khazraj were two tribes of the Anṣār between whom there had been enmity in the pre-Islamic age, but that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them it vanished and Allah united their hearts. While they were sitting in one of their gatherings, a man of Aws recited a verse containing lampoon of Khazraj, and a man of Khazraj recited a verse containing lampoon of Aws. Each kept reciting verses in turn until some of them rose against others, seized their weapons, and set out to fight. When this reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he came hurrying with his shanks uncovered. When he saw them he called to them and recited that O you who believe, fear Allah as He should be feared and do not die except as Muslims, continuing through the verses. Thereupon they lowered their weapons, cast them down, embraced one another, and wept. > **""O you who believe, fear Allah as He should be feared, and do not die except as Muslims.""**"
"13055","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with them both, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the best charity is the reconciling of relations. > **""The best charity is the reconciling of relations.""**"
"13056","Abū Kāhil, may Allah be pleased with him, said that harsh words passed between two men among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until they severed relations. He met one of them and asked what was the matter with him and the other, saying that he had heard him praise him highly and supplicate for him often. He met the other and said the like to him. He kept walking between them until they were reconciled. Then he thought about what he had done, fearing that he had ruined himself while reconciling them. He came to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him of the matter, saying that by the One who sent him with the truth, he had heard nothing from either of them. He said to him that he should reconcile between people even with such-and-such words he did not fully grasp, and when he asked what was meant by it, he was told that lying was meant. > **""O Abū Kāhil, reconcile between people even with such words.""**"
"13057","Shaddād ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that one who speaks good or spreads good is not a liar. > **""One who speaks good or spreads good is not a liar.""**"
"13058","Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, may Allah be pleased with him, reportedly raised the report that lying is recorded against one, except for what benefits a Muslim thereby or wards off harm from him thereby. > **""Lying is recorded, except for what benefits a Muslim thereby or wards off harm from him thereby.""**"
"13059","Al-Nawwās ibn Samʿān, may Allah be pleased with him, said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that every lie is recorded against the son of Adam except for three: a man who lies in war, for war is deceit; a man who lies to his wife and thereby pleases her; and a man who lies between two men and thereby reconciles them. > **""Every lie is recorded against the son of Adam except for three: a man who lies in war, for war is deceit; a man who lies to his wife and thereby pleases her; and a man who lies between two men and thereby reconciles them.""**"
"13060","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had two freedmen, one Abyssinian and one Copt. One day they reviled one another, one saying O Abyssinian and the other saying O Copt, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said do not speak thus, for you are two men of the household of Muhammad ﷺ. > **""Do not speak thus; you are two men of the household of Muhammad.""**"
"13061","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said whoever offers an excuse to his brother and he does not excuse him, or does not accept his excuse, upon him shall be the like of the sin of the collector of unjust dues, and Abu al-Zubayr explained that al-maks means the tithe-taker. > **""Whoever offers an excuse to his brother and he does not excuse him, or does not accept his excuse, upon him shall be the like of the sin of the collector of unjust dues.""**"
"13062","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever is offered exoneration and does not accept it shall not come to me at the Basin. > **""Whoever is offered exoneration and does not accept it shall not come to me at the Basin.""**"
"13063","A'isha رضي الله عنها reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said be chaste and your women will be chaste, be dutiful to your fathers and your sons will be dutiful to you, and whoever offers an excuse to his Muslim brother concerning something reported about him, and he does not accept his excuse, he shall not come to the Basin. > **""Be chaste and your women will be chaste; be dutiful to your fathers and your sons will be dutiful to you; and whoever offers an excuse to his Muslim brother and it is not accepted, he shall not come to the Basin.""**"
"13064","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said pardon one another and the hatreds between you shall fall away. > **""Pardon one another; the hatreds between you shall fall away.""**"
"13065","Shayba al-Hajabi reports from his uncle that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said three things purify for you the love of your brother: that you greet him when you meet him, that you make room for him in the gathering, and that you call him by the names most beloved to him. > **""Three things purify for you the love of your brother: that you greet him when you meet him, that you make room for him in the gathering, and that you call him by the names most beloved to him.""**"
"13066","In another wording of the preceding report there is the addition: and that you visit him when he is ill. > **""And that you visit him when he is ill.""**"
"13067","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه, marfu in wording, reports that Allah the Blessed and Exalted says whoever humbles himself to Me like this, lowering the palm of his hand toward the earth, I shall raise him like this, raising the palms toward the heaven. In the wording of his address upon the pulpit he said O people be humble, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say whoever humbles himself for Allah, Allah raises him and says rise, may Allah raise you, so he is great in the eyes of men though small in himself, and whoever is proud, Allah breaks him and says be debased, so he is small in the eyes of men though great in himself. > **""Whoever humbles himself for Allah, Allah raises him and says: Rise, may Allah raise you; and whoever is proud, Allah breaks him and says: Be debased.""**"
"13068","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports, raised to the Prophet ﷺ, that he said whoever humbles himself to Me like this, gesturing with the palm toward the earth, I shall raise him like this, gesturing with the palm toward the heaven. > **""Whoever humbles himself to Me like this, I shall raise him like this.""**"
"13069","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said there is no human being except that in his head is a bridle in the hand of an angel; when he is humble it is said to the angel raise his bridle, and when he is proud it is said to the angel lower his bridle. > **""There is no human being except that in his head is a bridle in the hand of an angel; when he is humble it is said to the angel: Raise his bridle, and when he is proud it is said to the angel: Lower his bridle.""**"
"13070","Abu Umama رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said hold fast to humility, for humility is in the heart, and no Muslim shall harm another Muslim; many a man wrapped in worn garments, if he were to swear by Allah, Allah would fulfil it for him. > **""Hold fast to humility, for humility is in the heart, and no Muslim shall harm another Muslim.""**"
"13071","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever humbles himself for his Muslim brother, Allah raises him, and whoever exalts himself over him, Allah abases him. > **""Whoever humbles himself for his Muslim brother, Allah raises him, and whoever exalts himself over him, Allah abases him.""**"
"13072","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said there is no man except that in his head is a bridle, and the bridle is in the hand of an angel; if he is humble it is said to the angel raise the bridle, and if he seeks to exalt himself it is said to the angel lower the bridle. > **""There is no man except that in his head is a bridle in the hand of an angel; if he is humble it is said to the angel: Raise the bridle, and if he seeks to exalt himself it is said to the angel: Lower the bridle.""**"
"13073","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there is no human being except that in his head are two chains, a chain toward the heaven and a chain toward the earth; if he is humble Allah the Mighty and Majestic raises him by the chain in the heaven, and if he is tyrannical Allah lowers him by the chain in the earth. > **""There is no human being except that in his head are two chains, a chain toward the heaven and a chain toward the earth; if he is humble Allah raises him by the heavenly chain, and if he is tyrannical Allah lowers him by the earthly chain.""**"
"13074","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that he walked behind the Prophet ﷺ testing whether he disliked it, so the Prophet ﷺ reached back with his hand and drew him alongside, then he fell behind again testing whether he disliked it, and he again drew him alongside, then he fell behind a third time and he again drew him alongside, so he knew that he disliked walking behind him."
"13075","Wathila ibn al-Asqa رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the Muslim is inviolable to the Muslim in his blood, his honour, and his wealth; the Muslim is the brother of the Muslim, he does not wrong him nor forsake him; piety is here, gesturing with his hand to his heart; and sufficient evil it is for a man to despise his Muslim brother. > **""The Muslim is inviolable to the Muslim in his blood, his honour, and his wealth; the Muslim is the brother of the Muslim, he does not wrong him nor forsake him; piety is here; and sufficient evil it is for a man to despise his Muslim brother.""**"
"13076","Uqba ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said these lineages of yours are no grounds for reviling anyone; you are all the children of Adam, like an unfilled measure; no one has merit over another except by religion or righteous deed; sufficient it is for a man to be foul-mouthed, obscene, miserly, and cowardly. > **""These lineages of yours are no grounds for reviling anyone; you are all the children of Adam; no one has merit over another except by religion or righteous deed.""**"
"13077","In another wording of the preceding report: these lineages of yours are no cause for disparaging anyone. > **""These lineages of yours are no cause for disparaging anyone.""**"
"13078","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said to him consider, for you are no better than a red man nor a black man, except that you excel him by piety. > **""You are no better than a red man nor a black man, except that you excel him by piety.""**"
"13079","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said your Lord is One and your father is one, so there is no merit for an Arab over a non-Arab, nor for a red man over a black man, except by piety. In the wording of al-Bazzar: your father is one and your religion is one, your father is Adam, and Adam was created from dust. > **""Your Lord is One and your father is one, so there is no merit for an Arab over a non-Arab, nor for a red man over a black man, except by piety.""**"
"13080","Habib ibn Khirash al-Asari رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the Muslims are brothers; there is no merit of one over another except by piety. > **""The Muslims are brothers; there is no merit of one over another except by piety.""**"
"13081","A'isha رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ was not impressed by anything, nor did anything of the world please him, except that piety be in it."
"13082","Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf reports that Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه heard a man say I am the closest of men to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he said another besides you is closer to him than you, though kinship to him belongs to you. > **""Another besides you is closer to him than you, though kinship to him belongs to you.""**"
"13083","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said when the Day of Resurrection comes Allah shall command a caller to proclaim: Behold, I made a lineage and you made a lineage; I made the noblest of you the most God-fearing, but you refused except to say So-and-so son of So-and-so is better than So-and-so son of So-and-so; today I raise My lineage and lower yours; where are the God-fearing? > **""I made the noblest of you the most God-fearing, but you refused except to say So-and-so is better than So-and-so; today I raise My lineage and lower yours; where are the God-fearing?""**"
"13084","Qanbar, the chamberlain of Mu'awiya رضي الله عنه, reports that Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه spoke harshly to Mu'awiya, so Mu'awiya complained to Ubada ibn al-Samit, Abu al-Darda, Amr ibn al-As, and Umm Haram رضي الله عنهم, saying you companied as he companied and you hold a view as he holds; if you see fit, speak to him. They sent for Abu Dharr and he came, and they spoke to him, whereupon he said: As for you O Abu al-Walid, you embraced Islam before me, and age and merit belong to you over me, yet I would have spared you such a gathering; as for you O Abu al-Darda, the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ nearly passed you by, then you embraced Islam and became of the righteous Muslims; as for you O Amr, you strove alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; as for you O Umm Haram, you are but a woman, and your mind is the mind of a woman, so what have you to do with this? Ubada then said: Decidedly, I shall never sit in such a gathering again. > **""As for you O Abu al-Walid, you embraced Islam before me and merit belongs to you; as for you O Abu al-Darda, you became of the righteous Muslims; as for you O Amr, you strove alongside the Messenger of Allah; as for you O Umm Haram, you are but a woman, so what have you to do with this?""**"
"13085","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ said do not boast of your fathers who died in ignorance, for by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, what the dung-beetle rolls with its nostrils is better than your fathers who died in ignorance. In another wording: what the beetle rolls with its nose is better than them. > **""Do not boast of your fathers who died in ignorance, for by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, what the dung-beetle rolls with its nostrils is better than them.""**"
"13086","Abu Rayhana رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever ascribes himself to nine unbelieving forefathers, seeking thereby honour and nobility, he shall be their tenth in the Fire. > **""Whoever ascribes himself to nine unbelieving forefathers, seeking thereby honour, he shall be their tenth in the Fire.""**"
"13087","Ubayy ibn Ka'b رضي الله عنه reports that two men boasted lineage in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; one said I am So-and-so son of So-and-so, so who are you, may you be bereft of your mother? The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said two men boasted lineage in the time of Musa عليه السلام; one said I am So-and-so son of So-and-so until he counted nine; who are you, may you be bereft of your mother? The other said I am So-and-so son of So-and-so, son of Islam. Allah revealed to Musa that as for these two boasters: as for you who ascribe yourself to nine in the Fire, you are their tenth, and as for you who ascribe yourself to two in Paradise, you are their third in Paradise. > **""As for you who ascribe yourself to nine in the Fire, you are their tenth; and as for you who ascribe yourself to two in Paradise, you are their third in Paradise.""**"
"13088","Mu'adh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that two men of the Children of Israel boasted lineage in the time of Musa عليه السلام, one a Muslim and the other a polytheist; the polytheist ascribed himself saying I am So-and-so son of So-and-so until he counted nine forefathers, then said to his companion ascribe yourself, may you be bereft of your mother; he said I am So-and-so son of So-and-so, and I disavow what lies beyond that. Musa gathered the people and said judgment has been passed between you: as for you who ascribed yourself to forefathers, you complete them as the tenth in the Fire, and as for you who ascribed yourself to your two parents, you are a man of the people of Islam. > **""As for you who ascribed yourself to nine forefathers, you complete them as the tenth in the Fire; and as for you who ascribed yourself to your two parents, you are a man of the people of Islam.""**"
"13089","Hudhayfa رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said all of you are the children of Adam, and Adam is from dust; a people boasting of their fathers shall either cease, or they shall be more contemptible to Allah than the dung-beetle. > **""All of you are the children of Adam, and Adam is from dust; a people boasting of their fathers shall either cease, or they shall be more contemptible to Allah than the dung-beetle.""**"
"13090","Hisham ibn Urwa reports that he heard Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما, when the nobility of ignorance was mentioned before him, say leave this aside, for Islam gave life to houses that were obscure and obscured houses that were flourishing; and if you refuse, then when the man of Banu Taym ibn Jud'an died, men divided glory after him."
"13091","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that he reviled a Muslim man by his mother from the days of ignorance, so the man complained against him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said in you is a branch of disbelief. When disbelief was mentioned his feet trembled, and he said O Messenger of Allah, by Him Who sent you with the truth, I shall never revile a Muslim after this. > **""In you is a branch of disbelief.""**"
"13092","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ had two freedmen, an Abyssinian and a Copt, and they reviled one another while the Prophet ﷺ listened; one said to his companion O Abyssinian, and the other said O Copt, so the Prophet ﷺ said do not say this, you are but two men of the companions of Muhammad ﷺ, and in another wording: of the household of Muhammad ﷺ. > **""Do not say this; you are but two men of the companions of Muhammad.""**"
"13093","Thawban رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said do not harm the servants of Allah, nor taunt them, nor seek out their faults, for whoever seeks out the fault of his Muslim brother, Allah shall seek out his fault until He disgraces him in his own house. > **""Do not harm the servants of Allah, nor taunt them, nor seek out their faults; for whoever seeks out the fault of his Muslim brother, Allah shall seek out his fault until He disgraces him in his own house.""**"
"13094","Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said the believer, among the people of faith, is like the head to the body; the believer aches for the people of faith as the body aches for what is in the head. > **""The believer, among the people of faith, is like the head to the body; the believer aches for the people of faith as the body aches for what is in the head.""**"
"13095","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه and Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه report from the Prophet ﷺ that he said the believer to the believer is like a building, one part strengthening another. > **""The believer to the believer is like a building, one part strengthening another.""**"
"13096","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said the believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended. > **""The believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended.""**"
"13097","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended. > **""The believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended.""**"
"13098","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه, said the believer befriends, and there is no good in one who neither keeps affection nor is befriended."
"13099","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended. > **""The believer befriends and is befriended, and there is no good in one who neither befriends nor is befriended.""**"
"13100","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said souls are mobilized troops; those of them that recognized one another harmonized, and those that were strangers to one another differed. > **""Souls are mobilized troops; those of them that recognized one another harmonized, and those that were strangers to one another differed.""**"
"13101","al-Harith ibn Umayra reports that he set out until he reached al-Mada'in, where he saw a man in worn garments rubbing a red hide; the man glanced at him and signalled with his hand stay where you are O servant of Allah. He asked those with him who this man was, and they said this is Salman رضي الله عنه. He entered his house, dressed in white garments, then came forward, took his hand, shook it, and asked after him. al-Harith said O Abu Abdillah, you never saw me before nor did I see you, you did not know me nor did I know you. He said indeed, by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, my soul recognized your soul when it saw you; are you not al-Harith ibn Umayra? He said yes. He then said I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say souls are mobilized troops; those of them that recognized one another for the sake of Allah harmonized, and those that were strangers to one another for the sake of Allah differed. > **""Souls are mobilized troops; those of them that recognized one another for the sake of Allah harmonized, and those that were strangers to one another for the sake of Allah differed.""**"
"13102","Amra bint Abd al-Rahman reports that there was a jesting woman in Makkah who lodged with a woman like her, and when this reached A'isha رضي الله عنها she said my beloved spoke the truth; I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say souls are mobilized troops; those of them that recognized one another harmonized, and those that were strangers to one another differed. The narrator added, as I know, that the first woman had not known the other before. > **""Souls are mobilized troops; those of them that recognized one another harmonized, and those that were strangers to one another differed.""**"
"13103","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said love your beloved in measure, for perhaps he will become hateful to you one day, and hate your enemy in measure, for perhaps he will become your beloved one day. > **""Love your beloved in measure, for perhaps he will become hateful to you one day; and hate your enemy in measure, for perhaps he will become your beloved one day.""**"
"13104","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said love your beloved in measure, for perhaps he will become your enemy one day, and hate your enemy in measure, for perhaps he will become your beloved one day. > **""Love your beloved in measure, for perhaps he will become your enemy one day; and hate your enemy in measure, for perhaps he will become your beloved one day.""**"
"13105","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said a man speaks a word intending thereby no evil except to make the people laugh by it, yet by it he falls further than the heaven is distant. > **""A man speaks a word intending thereby no evil except to make the people laugh by it, yet by it he falls further than the heaven is distant.""**"
"13106","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said I jest, but I speak only the truth. > **""I jest, but I speak only the truth.""**"
"13107","Ubayd ibn Umayr reports that he heard a man say to Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما did you not hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say I jest but I speak only the truth, and he replied yes. > **""I jest, but I speak only the truth.""**"
"13108","Qurra reports that he asked Ibn Sirin whether they used to jest, and he said they were but like other men; Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما used to jest and recite: he loves wine at the expense of his boon-companions, yet hates that his coins should part from him. > **""He loves wine at the expense of his boon-companions, yet hates that his coins should part from him.""**"
"13109","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ said to a man: choose well and beware. Tabarani explained, and Allah knows best, that in his view it means choose a friend carefully and beware of him, while it reached him from some people of knowledge that it means guard against sins and beware their punishment. > **""Choose well and beware.""**"
"13110","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said guard yourselves against men through misgiving. > **""Guard yourselves against men through misgiving.""**"
"13111","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there shall come upon men a time when they are wolves; whoever is not a wolf, the wolves devour him. > **""There shall come upon men a time when they are wolves; whoever is not a wolf, the wolves devour him.""**"
"13112","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said shall I not inform you of the worst of you? They said indeed. He said the worst of you are those whose evil is feared and whose good is not hoped for, and the best of you are those whose good is hoped for and whose evil is not feared. > **""The worst of you are those whose evil is feared and whose good is not hoped for; and the best of you are those whose good is hoped for and whose evil is not feared.""**"
"13113","Amr ibn Awf al-Muzani رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the believer is not stung from one burrow twice. > **""The believer is not stung from one burrow twice.""**"
"13114","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said test and you will dislike. > **""Test and you will dislike.""**"
"13115","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه used to say trust men slowly, and he also said test and you will dislike."
"13116","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه, said judge men by their brethren."
"13117","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said right guidance, good comportment, and moderation are one part of forty-seven parts of prophethood, while in the narration with Abu Dawud the figure is twenty-five parts. > **""Right guidance, good comportment, and moderation are one part of forty-seven parts of prophethood.""**"
"13118","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said right guidance and good comportment are one part of seventy parts of prophethood. > **""Right guidance and good comportment are one part of seventy parts of prophethood.""**"
"13119","Uqba ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said when a man is pleased with the conduct and deed of another man, he is like him. > **""When a man is pleased with the conduct and deed of another man, he is like him.""**"
"13120","Abu Umama رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said Allah the Mighty and Majestic indeed blames incapacity, so exert yourself to the utmost; then if you are overcome say I have placed my trust in Allah, or Allah suffices me and He is the best Disposer. > **""Allah indeed blames incapacity, so exert yourself to the utmost; then if you are overcome say: I have placed my trust in Allah.""**"
"13121","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما reports that they were with the Prophet ﷺ when a foul wind rose, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said do you know what this wind is? This is the wind of those who backbite the believers. > **""Do you know what this wind is? This is the wind of those who backbite the believers.""**"
"13122","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade backbiting and listening to backbiting."
"13123","By the same chain Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade tale-bearing and listening to tale-bearing."
"13124","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say tale-bearing, reviling, and zealous partisanship are in the Fire, and they do not gather together in the breast of a believer. > **""Tale-bearing, reviling, and zealous partisanship are in the Fire, and they do not gather together in the breast of a believer.""**"
"13125","Ali رضي الله عنه used to say the utterer of obscenity and the one who listens to it are equal in sin."
"13126","Abdullah ibn Busr رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said the envious, the tale-bearer, and the soothsayer are not of me, nor am I of them. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the verse: Those who harm the believing men and believing women undeservedly bear the burden of slander and manifest sin. > **""The envious, the tale-bearer, and the soothsayer are not of me, nor am I of them.""**"
"13127","Abu Barza رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say behold, lying blackens the face, and tale-bearing is of the punishment of the grave. > **""Behold, lying blackens the face, and tale-bearing is of the punishment of the grave.""**"
"13128","Jabir ibn Abdillah رضي الله عنهما and Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said backbiting is more severe than adultery. It was asked how so? He said a man commits adultery then repents, and Allah pardons him, but the backbiter is not forgiven until his victim forgives him. > **""Backbiting is more severe than adultery; a man commits adultery then repents and Allah pardons him, but the backbiter is not forgiven until his victim forgives him.""**"
"13129","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever eats the flesh of his brother in this world shall have it brought near to him on the Day of Resurrection and it shall be said to him eat it alive as you ate it dead, so he shall eat it, grimacing and screaming. > **""Whoever eats the flesh of his brother in this world, it shall be said to him: Eat it alive as you ate it dead.""**"
"13130","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the man who used to backbite men in this world shall be brought and told eat the flesh of your brother dead as you ate it living, and the like was mentioned. > **""Eat the flesh of your brother dead as you ate it living.""**"
"13131","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that on the night the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was taken by night he looked into the Fire and saw a people eating carrion; he said who are these O Jibril? He said these are those who ate the flesh of men. And he saw a red, blue-eyed man with curly dishevelled hair; he said who is this O Jibril? He said this is the hamstringer of the she-camel. > **""Who are these O Jibril? — These are those who ate the flesh of men.""**"
"13132","A'isha رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his Companions do you know what the gravest adultery is with Allah? They said Allah and His Messenger know best. He said the gravest adultery with Allah is the declaring lawful the honour of a Muslim man, then he recited: Those who harm the believing men and believing women undeservedly. > **""Do you know what the gravest adultery is with Allah? The gravest adultery with Allah is the declaring lawful the honour of a Muslim man.""**"
"13133","Abu Hurayra رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said among the gravest of adulteries is a man's assault upon the honour of his brother. > **""Among the gravest of adulteries is a man's assault upon the honour of his brother.""**"
"13134","Yusuf ibn Abdillah ibn Salam رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said among the gravest of adulteries is one of you assaulting the honour of his Muslim brother. > **""Among the gravest of adulteries is one of you assaulting the honour of his Muslim brother.""**"
"13135","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه said: While I was walking with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, with him holding my hand, and another man to his left, we came upon two graves ahead of us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they were being punished, and they were not being punished for something great, yet indeed one of them did not guard himself against his urine, and the other used to carry tales. He then asked which of us would bring him a palm stalk. We raced and I outran him, so I brought him a stalk. He broke it in two, placed a piece upon this grave and a piece upon that grave, and said that it would lighten their burden while the two remained moist, and they were punished only for backbiting and urine. > **""They are being punished, and they are not being punished for something great. One of them did not guard himself against his urine, and the other used to carry tales. Which of you will bring me a palm stalk? It will lighten them while the two remain moist.""**"
"13136","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a man being punished in his grave for talebearing."
"13137","Yaʿlā ibn Siyābah رضي الله عنه knew of the Prophet ﷺ that he came upon a grave whose occupant was being punished, and said that this man used to devour the flesh of people. He then called for a moist palm stalk, placed it upon his grave, and said that perhaps it would be lightened for him while it remained moist. > **""This man used to devour the flesh of people. Perhaps it will be lightened for him while it remains moist.""**"
"13138","Asmaʾ bint Yazīd رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Shall I not inform you of the best of you. They said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Those when they are seen Allah is remembered. Then he said: Shall I not inform you of the worst of you. They are those who carry tales, corrupt between loved ones, and seek hardship for the innocent. > **""Shall I not inform you of the best of you? Those when they are seen Allah is remembered. Shall I not inform you of the worst of you? Those who carry tales, corrupt between loved ones, and seek hardship for the innocent.""**"
"13139","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ghanm رضي الله عنه conveys from the Prophet ﷺ that the best of the servants of Allah are those when they are seen Allah is remembered, and the worst of the servants of Allah are those who carry tales, separate between loved ones, and seek hardship for the innocent. > **""The best of the servants of Allah are those when they are seen Allah is remembered, and the worst of the servants of Allah are those who carry tales, separate between loved ones, and seek hardship for the innocent.""**"
"13140","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The best of my Ummah are those when they are seen Allah is remembered, and the worst of my Ummah are those who carry tales, separate between loved ones, and seek hardship for the innocent. > **""The best of my Ummah are those when they are seen Allah is remembered, and the worst of my Ummah are those who carry tales, separate between loved ones, and seek hardship for the innocent.""**"
"13141","Al-Barāʾ رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us until he made the secluded maidens hear in their houses, or he said in their chambers, and said: O company of those who believed with their tongues while faith did not enter their hearts, do not backbite the Muslims and do not pursue their faults, for whoever pursues the fault of his brother, Allah will pursue his fault, and whomever Allah pursues He will expose in the midst of his house. > **""O company of those who believed with their tongues while faith did not enter their hearts, do not backbite the Muslims and do not pursue their faults, for whoever pursues the fault of his brother, Allah will pursue his fault, and whomever Allah pursues He will expose in the midst of his house.""**"
"13142","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: We prayed Ẓuhr behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he finished his prayer he turned to us in anger and called with a voice that made the secluded maidens hear in the depths of their chambers, saying: O company of those who submitted while faith did not enter their hearts, do not disparage the Muslims and do not pursue their faults, for whoever seeks the fault of his Muslim brother, Allah will tear his veil and reveal his fault, even if he were within the cover of his house. > **""O company of those who submitted while faith did not enter their hearts, do not disparage the Muslims and do not pursue their faults, for whoever seeks the fault of his Muslim brother, Allah will tear his veil and reveal his fault, even if he were within the cover of his house.""**"
"13143","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a sermon until he made the secluded maidens hear in their chambers, saying: O company of those who believed with their tongues while faith did not enter their hearts, do not harm the believers and do not pursue their faults, for whoever pursues the fault of his Muslim brother, Allah will tear his veil and set upon him one who pursues his fault and exposes him, even in the midst of his house. > **""O company of those who believed with their tongues while faith did not enter their hearts, do not harm the believers and do not pursue their faults, for whoever pursues the fault of his Muslim brother, Allah will tear his veil and expose him, even in the midst of his house.""**"
"13144","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ when a man stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, how helpless so-and-so is, or he said how difficult so-and-so is. The Prophet ﷺ said: You have backbitten your companion and eaten his flesh. In the wording of al-Ṭabarānī: A man stood from the presence of the Prophet ﷺ, and they saw incapacity in his standing, so they said: How helpless so-and-so is. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: You have eaten your brother and backbitten him. > **""You have backbitten your companion and eaten his flesh. You have eaten your brother and backbitten him.""**"
"13145","ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ when a man stood and left, then another man fell upon him after him. The Prophet ﷺ said: Pick your teeth. He said: For what should I pick my teeth, O Messenger of Allah, did I eat meat. He said: You have eaten the flesh of your brother. > **""Pick your teeth. You have eaten the flesh of your brother.""**"
"13146","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said: I was with the Prophet ﷺ when they mentioned a man before him and said: How helpless he is. The Prophet ﷺ said: You have backbitten your brother. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we said what is in him. He said: If you say what is not in him, then you have slandered him. > **""You have backbitten your brother. If you say what is not in him, then you have slandered him.""**"
"13147","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever mentions a man with what is not in him in order to disgrace him with what is not in him, Allah will confine him in the Fire of Hell until he fulfills what he said about him. > **""Whoever mentions a man with what is not in him in order to disgrace him with what is not in him, Allah will confine him in the Fire of Hell until he fulfills what he said about him.""**"
"13148","Al-ʿĀṣī ibn ʿAmr al-Ṭufāwī reports that Abū al-ʿĀdiyah, Ḥabīb ibn al-Ḥārith, and Umm al-ʿAlāʾ رضي الله عنهم emigrated to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and accepted Islam. The woman said: Advise me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Beware what offends the ear. In the narration of al-Ṭabarānī from his aunt: I entered with some people upon the Prophet ﷺ and said: Relate to me a ḥadīth by which Allah will benefit me. He said: Beware what offends the ear. > **""Beware what offends the ear.""**"
"13149","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه reports in marfūʿ form that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: A man may utter a word intending no harm by it except to make the people laugh, yet he falls because of it farther than the sky. > **""A man may utter a word intending no harm by it except to make the people laugh, yet he falls because of it farther than the sky.""**"
"13150","Asmaʾ bint Yazīd رضي الله عنها said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever defends the honour of his brother against backbiting, it is binding upon Allah to free him from the Fire. > **""Whoever defends the honour of his brother against backbiting, it is binding upon Allah to free him from the Fire.""**"
"13151","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: The two-faced one in this world will come on the Day of Resurrection with two faces of Fire. > **""The two-faced one in this world will come on the Day of Resurrection with two faces of Fire.""**"
"13152","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever has two tongues, Allah will make for him on the Day of Resurrection two tongues of Fire. > **""Whoever has two tongues, Allah will make for him on the Day of Resurrection two tongues of Fire.""**"
"13153","Jundub ibn ʿAbdillah al-Bajalī رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: Whoever seeks repute, Allah will expose him, and whoever shows off, Allah will expose him, and whoever has two tongues in this world, Allah will make for him two tongues of the Fire of Hell on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever seeks repute, Allah will expose him, and whoever shows off, Allah will expose him, and whoever has two tongues in this world, Allah will make for him two tongues of the Fire of Hell on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"13154","ʿAbdullah, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, said that the two-tongued one in this world will have two tongues of Fire on the Day of Resurrection."
"13155","Abū Hind al-Dārī رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Whoever stands against his brother in a station of show and repute, Allah will station him on the Day of Resurrection and expose him. > **""Whoever stands against his brother in a station of show and repute, Allah will station him on the Day of Resurrection and expose him.""**"
"13156","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: There is no servant who stands in this world in a station of show and repute except that Allah will expose him before all creation on the Day of Resurrection. > **""There is no servant who stands in this world in a station of show and repute except that Allah will expose him before all creation on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"13157","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever seeks the good never fails, whoever consults never regrets, and whoever economizes never becomes poor. > **""Whoever seeks the good never fails, whoever consults never regrets, and whoever economizes never becomes poor.""**"
"13158","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever intends a matter and consults a Muslim man about it, Allah will guide him to the most right of his affairs. > **""Whoever intends a matter and consults a Muslim man about it, Allah will guide him to the most right of his affairs.""**"
"13159","ʿAlī, meaning Ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: The consulted one is entrusted, so when he is consulted let him advise as he would do for himself. > **""The consulted one is entrusted, so when he is consulted let him advise as he would do for himself.""**"
"13160","Umm Maslamah رضي الله عنها reports that Abū al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihān al-Anṣārī came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking a servant, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised him that if captives arrived he would give him. He then met ʿUmar رضي الله عنه, who said: O Abū al-Haytham, the Prophet ﷺ has received captives, so go to him and claim your promise. Abū al-Haytham and ʿUmar went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abū al-Haytham has come to claim his promise. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: We have received two black lads, choose whichever you wish. He said: I seek your counsel. He said: The consulted one is entrusted. Take this one, for he has prayed with us, and do not beat him, for we have been forbidden from beating those who pray. > **""We have received two black lads, choose whichever you wish. The consulted one is entrusted. Take this one, for he has prayed with us, and do not beat him, for we have been forbidden from beating those who pray.""**"
"13161","Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه and others said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The consulted one is entrusted. > **""The consulted one is entrusted.""**"
"13162","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The consulted one is entrusted; if he wishes he advises, and if he wishes he does not advise. > **""The consulted one is entrusted; if he wishes he advises, and if he wishes he does not advise.""**"
"13163","ʿAbdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The consulted one is entrusted. > **""The consulted one is entrusted.""**"
"13164","Abū al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihān رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: The consulted one is entrusted. > **""The consulted one is entrusted.""**"
"13165","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: The consulted one is entrusted. > **""The consulted one is entrusted.""**"
"13166","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever hears a report from a man who dislikes that it be mentioned from him, it is a trust, even if he did not ask him to conceal it. In the narration of al-Ṭabarānī from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr: ʿAbdullah ibn Salmān was sitting and spoke some words, and a man heard them whom he disliked to hear them. He turned to Abū al-Dardāʾ and said: Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Whoever relates a report disliking that it be spread from him, it is a trust, even if his companion did not ask him to conceal it. He said: Yes, I knew what you intended. Then he turned to the man and said: Do not mention this report. > **""Whoever hears a report from a man who dislikes that it be mentioned from him, it is a trust, even if he did not ask him to conceal it. Whoever relates a report disliking that it be spread from him, it is a trust, even if his companion did not ask him to conceal it.""**"
"13167","ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr said: ʿAbdullah ibn Salām رضي الله عنه was sitting and spoke a word, and a man heard it whom he disliked to hear it. He turned to Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه and said: Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Whoever relates a report disliking that it be spread from him, it is a trust, even if his companion did not ask him to conceal it. > **""Whoever relates a report disliking that it be spread from him, it is a trust, even if his companion did not ask him to conceal it.""**"
"13168","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When a man relates a report and then turns, it is a trust. > **""When a man relates a report and then turns, it is a trust.""**"
"13169","Sufyān ibn ʿAbdillah al-Thaqafī reports from his father that the Prophet ﷺ said: The one who satiates himself with what he was not given is like the wearer of two garments of falsehood. > **""The one who satiates himself with what he was not given is like the wearer of two garments of falsehood.""**"
"13170","Al-Nawwās ibn Samʿān رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: It is a great treachery to tell your brother a report while he deems you truthful and you are lying to him about it. > **""It is a great treachery to tell your brother a report while he deems you truthful and you are lying to him about it.""**"
"13171","Salmān, meaning al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه, said: None was greater in sanctity than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, yet when his Companions wrote to him they wrote: From so-and-so to Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"13172","Ḥanẓalah al-Kātib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and Khālid ibn al-Walīd رضي الله عنهما to Yemen and said: When you are together then ʿAlī is the commander, and when you separate then each of you is over his own task. Khālid ibn al-Walīd wrote to the Prophet ﷺ beginning with himself, and he did not object to that. ʿAlī wrote to the Prophet ﷺ beginning with the Prophet ﷺ. > **""When you are together then ʿAlī is the commander, and when you separate then each of you is over his own task.""**"
"13173","Al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥaḍramī رضي الله عنه wrote to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ beginning with himself."
"13174","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When one of you writes to a man, let him begin with himself, and when he writes let him soil his letter with dust, for it is more successful. > **""When one of you writes to a man, let him begin with himself, and when he writes let him soil his letter with dust, for it is more successful.""**"
"13175","Muslim ibn al-Ḥārith al-Tamīmī رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ wrote for him a letter enjoining those after him among the rulers concerning him, and sealed it."
"13176","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: The honour of a letter is its seal. > **""The honour of a letter is its seal.""**"
"13177","Ibn ʿImrān al-Jawnī said: One of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrated to me, while we campaigned toward Persia, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever sleeps atop a house with no parapet and falls and dies, liability is absolved from him, and whoever sails the sea when it rages, liability is absolved from him. > **""Whoever sleeps atop a house with no parapet and falls and dies, liability is absolved from him, and whoever sails the sea when it rages, liability is absolved from him.""**"
"13178","Ibn ʿImrān al-Jawnī said: We were in Persia under a commander called Zuhayr ibn ʿAbdillah, who said: A man narrated to me that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: Whoever spends the night atop a parapet, or atop a house, with nothing around him to restrain his foot, liability is absolved from him, and whoever sails the sea after it rages, liability is absolved from him. > **""Whoever spends the night atop a parapet with nothing around him to restrain his foot, liability is absolved from him, and whoever sails the sea after it rages, liability is absolved from him.""**"
"13179","ʿAbdullah ibn Jaʿfar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ said: Whoever shoots us by night is not from us, and whoever sleeps upon a roof with no wall and falls and dies, his blood is wasted. > **""Whoever shoots us by night is not from us, and whoever sleeps upon a roof with no wall and falls and dies, his blood is wasted.""**"
"13180","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he went out in summer, went out on Friday night, and when winter entered, he entered on Friday night."
"13181","Abū al-Naḍr reports that Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه was complaining of his leg. His brother entered upon him while he lay with one leg placed over the other. He struck his painful leg with his hand and hurt him. He said: You hurt me, did you not know my leg aches. He said: Yes. He said: What prompted you to do that. He said: Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade this."
"13182","ʿUbayd ibn Ḥunayn said: While I was sitting, Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān رضي الله عنه came to me and said: Come with us, O Ibn Jubayr, to Abū Saʿīd. We went until we entered upon Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه and found him reclining with his right leg raised over his left. We greeted and sat. Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān raised his hand to the leg of Abū Saʿīd and pinched it hard. Abū Saʿīd said: Glory be to Allah, O son of my mother, you hurt me. He said: That is what I intended, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When Allah completed His creation He reclined, placed one leg over the other, and said: None of My creation should do this. Abū Saʿīd said: By Allah, I will never do it again. > **""When Allah completed His creation He reclined, placed one leg over the other, and said: None of My creation should do this.""**"
"13183","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade a man to place one leg over the other while reclining."
"13184","Abū Qirṣāfah رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reclining upon his back with one leg placed over the other."
"13185","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: When one of you reclines, let him not place one leg over the other. > **""When one of you reclines, let him not place one leg over the other.""**"
"13186","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillah رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man to sleep between people, and to sleep on the roadway."
"13187","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ passed by a man lying upon his belly and said: This is a lying that Allah does not love. > **""This is a lying that Allah does not love.""**"
"13188","ʿAmr ibn al-Sharīd reports from his father that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ found a man lying upon his face with nothing upon his backside, he prodded him with his foot and said: This is the most hateful lying to Allah. > **""This is the most hateful lying to Allah.""**"
"13189","Al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: While I sat with Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, a man of Banū Ghifār, son of ʿAbdullah ibn Ṭihfah رضي الله عنه, came upon us. Abū Salamah said: Will you not inform us of your father. He said that ʿAbdullah ibn Ṭihfah said: When guests grew numerous with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ he would say: Let each man return with his guest. One night many guests gathered, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Let each man return with his companion. I was among those who returned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he entered he said: O ʿĀʾishah, is there anything. She said: Yes, a little ḥays I prepared for your breaking fast. She brought it in her small bowl, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took a little and ate, then said: Eat in the name of Allah. We ate from it until we no longer looked at it. Then he said: Have you any drink. She said: Yes, a little milk I kept for you. He said: Bring it. She brought it, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it, raised it to his mouth, drank a little, then said: Drink in the name of Allah. We drank until, by Allah, we no longer looked at it. Then we went out, I came to the mosque and lay upon my face. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out unaware, waking the people for prayer, as he would wake them when he went out. He passed by me while I lay upon my face and said: Who is this. I said: ʿAbdullah ibn Ṭihfah. He said: This is a lying that Allah dislikes. > **""Let each man return with his guest. Let each man return with his companion. O ʿĀʾishah, is there anything? Eat in the name of Allah. Have you any drink? Bring it. Drink in the name of Allah. Who is this? This is a lying that Allah dislikes.""**"
"13190","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: No man shall touch another man, nor a woman another woman. > **""No man shall touch another man, nor a woman another woman.""**"
"13191","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillah رضي الله عنهما said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: No man shall touch another man in one garment, nor a woman another woman in one garment. The narrator said: I said to Jābir: Did you count sins as idolatry. He said: Allah forbid. > **""No man shall touch another man in one garment, nor a woman another woman in one garment.""**"
"13192","Abū al-Zubayr said: I asked Jābir رضي الله عنه about a man touching another man, and Jābir said: The Prophet ﷺ rebuked that. I asked Jābir about a woman touching another woman, and he said: The Prophet ﷺ forbade that."
"13193","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: No man shall touch another man, nor a woman another woman. > **""No man shall touch another man, nor a woman another woman.""**"
"13194","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: No woman touches another woman except that both are adulteresses, and no man touches another man except that both are adulterers. > **""No woman touches another woman except that both are adulteresses, and no man touches another man except that both are adulterers.""**"
"13195","Samurah, meaning Ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid women to lie together with one another except with garments between them, and a man to lie with his companion except with a garment between them."
"13196","A man of Hudhayl said: I saw ʿAbdullah ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنهما, his dwelling in the Ḥill and his mosque in the Ḥaram. While I was with him, he saw Umm Saʿīd, daughter of Abū Jahl, wearing a bow and walking with the gait of men. ʿAbdullah asked: Who is this. I said: This is Umm Saʿīd bint Abī Jahl. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: Not from us are those women who imitate men, nor those men who imitate women. > **""Not from us are those women who imitate men, nor those men who imitate women.""**"
"13197","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that a woman passed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wearing a bow, so the Prophet ﷺ said: May Allah curse the women who imitate men and the men who imitate women. > **""May Allah curse the women who imitate men and the men who imitate women.""**"
"13198","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed the effeminate among men and the masculine among women."
"13199","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed the effeminate men who imitate women, the masculine women who imitate men, and the lone rider in the desert."
"13200","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed the men who imitate women and the women who imitate men."
"13201","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ cursed the effeminate among men and the masculine among women."
"13202","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: Four are cursed in this world and the Hereafter, and the angels say amen: a man whom Allah made male yet feminizes himself and imitates women, a woman whom Allah made female yet masculinizes herself and imitates men, one who misleads the blind, and a celibate man, though Allah made none celibate except Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā. > **""Four are cursed in this world and the Hereafter, and the angels say amen: a man whom Allah made male yet feminizes himself and imitates women, a woman whom Allah made female yet masculinizes herself and imitates men, one who misleads the blind, and a celibate man, though Allah made none celibate except Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā.""**"
"13203","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed the men who imitate women and the women who imitate men."
"13204","Wāthilah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed the effeminate among men and the masculine among women, and said: Expel them from your houses. So the Prophet ﷺ expelled Anjashah, and ʿUmar رضي الله عنه expelled so-and-so. > **""Expel them from your houses.""**"
"13205","ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the house of Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها and saw with them an effeminate man saying: O ʿAbdullah ibn Abī Umayyah, if Allah conquers al-Ṭāʾif I shall show you Bādiyah bint Ghaylān, approaching with four and retreating with eight. The Prophet ﷺ said: These shall not enter upon you. > **""These shall not enter upon you.""**"
"13206","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that a man went out, two men followed him, and a third man trailed them saying: Return, return. They returned. He said to him: These two are devils, and I kept with them until I turned them back. When you come to the Prophet ﷺ, convey greetings to him and inform him that we have gathered our charities, and if it were suitable for him we would send them to him. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade solitude at that time."
"13207","In another narration the wording is Return you two instead of Return. Aḥmad and Abū Yaʿlā report it, except that he said: A man went out from Khaybar. > **""Return you two.""**"
"13208","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade solitude, that a man spend the night alone or travel alone."
"13209","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: If people knew what is in solitude, no rider would ever travel by night, and no man would sleep in a house alone. > **""If people knew what is in solitude, no rider would ever travel by night, and no man would sleep in a house alone.""**"
"13210","Al-Bara, meaning Ibn Azib, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave a concise warning concerning Bedouin life and its hardening effect, as quoted below. > **""Whoever takes to the desert life becomes harsh.""**"
"13211","Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warn of ruin through the Book and through milk. When asked what was meant, the Prophet ﷺ explained that people would learn the Quran and interpret it contrary to what Allah revealed, and would love milk, abandon the congregations, and settle in the wilderness. > **""The ruin of my community is in the Book and in milk. They learn the Quran and interpret it contrary to what Allah revealed, and they love milk and abandon the congregations and settle in the desert.""**"
"13212","Abdullah ibn Amr, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expressed fear for his community only concerning milk, explaining that the devil stands between its foam and its pure portion, as quoted below. > **""I fear for my community nothing except milk, for indeed the devil is between the foam and the pure milk.""**"
"13213","Abu Said al-Khudri, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade settling in remote hamlets, likening them to graves. The narrator explained that the term al-kufur means villages. > **""Do not settle in the remote villages, for they are like graves.""**"
"13214","Abu Said al-Khudri, radiya Allahu anhu, reported a series of injunctions from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning Bedouin tents, the congregation, mounting, striking faces of beasts, and forbidden names, as quoted below. > **""Do not stretch tent ropes for Bedouin life, for Bedouin life is harshness. The Hand of Allah is upon the congregation, and Allah does not care for the deviation of whoever deviates. Let no more than two ride upon a mount. Do not strike the faces of beasts, for everything glorifies its Lord with praise. Do not name your sons or your brothers al-Hakam nor Abu al-Hakam, for Allah is al-Hakam.""**"
"13215","Abdur Rahman ibn Ghanm reported that Umar ibn al-Khattab, radiya Allahu anhu, appointed Muadh ibn Jabal, radiya Allahu anhu, over Syria and wrote to him to distribute stipends and lead the people in expedition. While Muadh was distributing stipends, a man from the countryside asked for his stipend early so he could return before night, but Muadh refused to prefer him over the townsfolk and cited the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ quoted below concerning precedence in Paradise. > **""All the Prophets will enter Paradise forty years before Sulayman son of Dawud, peace be upon him. The poor believers will enter Paradise forty years before the rich. The righteous slaves will enter Paradise forty years before others. The people of the cities will enter Paradise forty years before the people of the countryside, by virtue of the cities, the congregations, the Friday prayers, and the circles of remembrance, and if tribulation comes it singles them out apart from the others.""**"
"13216","Abdullah ibn Umar, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the best bequest of a father to his child, as quoted below. > **""No father bequeaths to his child anything better than good upbringing.""**"
"13217","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded hanging the whip in view of the household as a means of discipline, as quoted below. In another wording the word household is replaced by servant. > **""Hang the whip where the members of the household can see it, for it disciplines them.""**"
"13218","Ibn Umar, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed that the staff not be removed from the family and that they be made to fear Allah, as quoted below. > **""Do not withdraw the staff from your family, and instill fear of Allah in them.""**"
"13219","Asad ibn Wadaa reported that a man called Jarir came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked how to punish his disobedient family. The Prophet ﷺ thrice told him to pardon, then permitted proportionate punishment while forbidding striking the face, as quoted below. > **""Pardon. If you must punish, then punish according to the offence, and avoid the face.""**"
"13220","Ibn Umar, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade reviling the face, giving as reason the creation of the son of Adam in the image of the Most Merciful, as quoted below. > **""Do not revile the face, for the son of Adam was created in the image of the Most Merciful.""**"
"13221","Abu Said al-Khudri, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded avoidance of the face in combat with a brother, as quoted below. > **""When one of you fights his brother, let him avoid the face.""**"
"13222","In a variant narration from Abu Said al-Khudri, radiya Allahu anhu, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ extended the prohibition to shooting and striking generally, as quoted below. > **""When one of you shoots or strikes, let him avoid the face.""**"
"13223","Al-Miqdam ibn Madikarib, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbid slapping the cheeks of beasts and declare that Allah had provided sticks and whips instead, as quoted below. > **""Allah has made sticks and whips for you.""**"
"13224","Abdullah ibn Ziyad reported that he visited the two sons of Busr al-Sulami and asked whether they had heard anything from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about a man who beats his mount with a whip and jerks its bridle. From inside the house their elder sister, who had met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, called out the verse that there is no beast on earth nor bird flying with its wings except communities like you, and that nothing was neglected in the Book. > **""There is no beast on earth nor bird flying with its two wings except communities like you. We have neglected nothing in the Book.""**"
"13225","Muadh ibn Anas, radiya Allahu anhu, reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he passed by people halted upon their mounts and riding beasts and instructed them to mount them soundly and leave them soundly, and not to make them chairs for talk in roads and markets, for often a mount is better than its rider and more frequent in remembrance of Allah than he. > **""Ride them soundly and leave them soundly, and do not make them chairs for your conversations in the roads and markets, for often a mount is better than its rider and more frequent in remembrance of Allah than he.""**"
"13226","Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Umayya reported that Habib ibn Maslama came to Qays ibn Saad ibn Ubada, radiya Allahu anhu, during the first civil strife while Qays was on a horse. Habib moved back from the saddle and invited him to mount, but Qays refused and cited the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ quoted below. Habib replied that he knew it but feared for Qays. > **""The owner of the mount has most right to its front.""**"
"13227","Qays ibn Saad, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Prophet ﷺ visited them, they provided water for washing and he bathed, they brought a Wars-dyed cloak which he wrapped about himself so that the trace of Wars could be seen on his side, then they brought a donkey for him to ride. He declared the ruling quoted below, and when they said the donkey was his, the narrator notes that Ibn Maja narrated only up to the mention of his side. > **""The owner of the donkey has most right to the front of his donkey.""**"
"13228","Umar ibn al-Khattab, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruled that the owner of the mount is most rightful to its front, as quoted below. > **""The owner of the mount is most rightful to its front.""**"
"13229","Urwa ibn Mutib al-Ansari, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ruled that the owner of the mount is most deserving of its front, as quoted below. > **""The owner of the mount is most deserving of its front.""**"
"13230","Isma ibn Malik al-Khatmi, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited them at Quba, and when he wished to return they brought a short-stepping donkey and he mounted. They proposed sending a boy with him to return the beast, but he stated the ruling quoted below. They then asked him to ride it and return it to them, so he departed upon it and returned it to them moving with a brisk ambling pace beyond comparison. > **""The owner of the mount is most deserving of its front.""**"
"13231","Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn reported that al-Husayn, radiya Allahu anhu, set out toward his land beyond al-Harra on foot with others when al-Numan ibn Bashir, radiya Allahu anhu, overtook them on a mule, dismounted, and insisted that Abu Abdillah mount. After repeated oaths al-Husayn accepted reluctantly and asked al-Numan to sit in front while he rode behind, citing what he heard from Fatima daughter of Muhammad, radiya Allahu anha, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as quoted below, with the exception of permission. Al-Numan affirmed her truthfulness and cited his father Bashir to the same effect. > **""A man has most right to the front of his mount, to the front of his bedding, and to leading prayer in his house, except for what people gather him to do. Except with permission.""**"
"13232","Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Husayn reported that he set out with his grandfather Husayn ibn Ali, radiya Allahu anhuma, toward his land at al-Zaraniq beyond al-Bayda when the son of al-Numan ibn Bashir overtook them on a mule, dismounted, and urged Husayn to mount. After persistent oaths Husayn cited what Fatima, radiya Allahu anha, narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as quoted below. The son of al-Numan affirmed her and added his father's narration with the increase concerning permission. > **""A man has most right to the front of his mount, to the front of his bedding, and to prayer in his house, except with his permission.""**"
"13233","Abu Tamima al-Hujaymi, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that while he was upon his donkey he met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he moved to the rear of the donkey and invited the Prophet ﷺ to mount at the front. The Prophet ﷺ declared the ruling quoted below, and when Abu Tamima gifted the donkey to him, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mounted in front while Abu Tamima rode behind. > **""You are most rightful to the front of your donkey.""**"
"13234","Al-Muhajir, freedman of the family of Ziyad, reported that while he was upon a small donkey with his feet nearly touching the ground, Amir al-Muminin Ali ibn Abi Talib, radiya Allahu anhu, appeared in the moonlight. Asked where he was going, Ali mentioned a need, accepted the offer to ride, and when the narrator moved to the rear and invited him forward, Ali refused and cited the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ quoted below. > **""The owner of the mount is most deserving of the front of the mount, and the owner of the bedding is most deserving of the front of the bedding.""**"
"13235","Abu Hurayra, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed that loads be placed to the rear when loading, for the foot is bound and the hand is tied, as quoted below. > **""When you load, place the load to the rear, for the foot is bound and the hand is tied.""**"
"13236","Jabir, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade three persons riding upon one mount."
"13237","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave precedence to the barefoot over the shod concerning the middle of the road, as quoted below. > **""The barefoot is more deserving of the middle of the road than the shod.""**"
"13238","Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade branding upon the face, whereupon al-Abbas declared that he would brand only upon the hindquarters near the tail."
"13239","Talha ibn Ubaydillah, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that a camel branded upon its face was brought past the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who remarked that its people should have spared the beast from fire. Talha then resolved to brand only at the farthest point from the face and branded at the root of the tail. In another wording the Prophet ﷺ explicitly forbade branding upon the face. > **""If only the people of this camel had spared this beast from fire.""**"
"13240","Anas, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a donkey branded upon its face and invoked a curse upon the doer, as quoted below. > **""May Allah curse whoever did this.""**"
"13241","Abu Hurayra, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that al-Abbas branded a camel of his upon its face, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked why he did not brand elsewhere than the face. Al-Abbas swore by Him Who sent the Prophet ﷺ with truth that he would brand only upon its hindmost bone, and thereafter branded upon the hindquarters. > **""Why not elsewhere than the face?""**"
"13242","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed whoever brands the face, as quoted below. > **""May Allah curse whoever brands the face.""**"
"13243","Junada ibn Jarada of Banu Ghaylan ibn Junada, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that he brought camels to the Prophet ﷺ branded upon their noses. The Prophet ﷺ reproached him for finding no limb but the face and warned of retribution ahead. Junada entrusted the matter to him, so the Prophet ﷺ asked for an unbranded animal. When Junada brought a young camel and a Hiqqa and placed the iron upon the neck, the Prophet ﷺ kept saying move back until it reached the thigh, then permitted branding and Junada branded the thighs. Their alms amounted to two Hiqqas among ninety. > **""O Junada, did you find no limb to brand except the face? Ahead of you lies retribution. Bring me something unbranded. Move back, move back. Brand with the blessing of Allah.""**"
"13244","Nuqada, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that he said he brands, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he had not seen him branding upon the face and forbade burning flesh. When asked where to brand, the Prophet ﷺ directed him to the place of the halter upon the neck, as quoted below. > **""Have I not seen you branding upon the face? Do not burn the flesh. Upon the place of the halter on the neck.""**"
"13245","Khalid ibn Ubaydillah ibn Harmala al-Mudliji, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted at Usfan when a man asked about the women and camels of Banu Mudlij, while a Mudliji man stood among the people with displeasure visible upon his face. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then stated the principle quoted below. > **""The best of the people is he who defends his people so long as he does not sin.""**"
"13246","Abu Umama, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded closing doors, overturning vessels, tying waterskins, and extinguishing lamps, for the intruders were not permitted to climb over them, as quoted below. > **""Close your doors, overturn your vessels, tie your waterskins, and extinguish your lamps, for they have not been permitted to climb over you.""**"
"13247","Abdullah ibn Sarjis, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade urinating into burrows and commanded extinguishing lamps at sleep lest a mouse seize the wick and burn the household, tying waterskins, covering drink, and locking doors at night. Asked why urination in burrows was disliked, Qatada explained that they are said to be dwellings of the Jinn. > **""Let none of you urinate into a burrow. When you sleep, extinguish the lamp, for the mouse seizes the wick and burns the household. Tie the waterskins, cover the drink, and lock the doors at night.""**"
"13248","Abu Hurayra, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that a man called Abu Humayd brought the Prophet ﷺ a vessel of milk from al-Baqi by day, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked why he had not covered it even by laying a stick across it, as quoted below. > **""Why did you not cover it, even by laying a stick across it?""**"
"13249","Jabir ibn Abdillah, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned against the onset of the evening meal time, which the narrator understood as fear of sudden seizure by unseen beings, as quoted below. > **""Beware the onset of the night meal.""**"
"13250","Abu Hurayra, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described a creation of Allah spread beneath the night as He wills, and commanded tying waterskins, closing doors, and covering vessels, for none of them can open a door, uncover a covering, or loosen a tie, as quoted below. > **""Allah has a creation which He spreads beneath the night as He wills. Tie the waterskin, close the doors, and cover the vessel, for none opens a door, nor uncovers a covering, nor loosens a tie.""**"
"13251","Ali, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded them to bolt the door, cover vessels, tie the waterskin, and extinguish the lamp."
"13252","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, conveyed with doubt whether raised to the Prophet ﷺ, that when the sun has set children should be restrained, for it is an hour in which devils spread, as quoted below. > **""When the sun has set, restrain your children, for it is an hour in which the devils spread.""**"
"13253","Wahshi ibn Harb, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Prophet ﷺ went out at night for a need and left the door open, then returned to find Iblis standing in the middle of the house. The Prophet ﷺ ordered him away from his house as an evil one, then instructed that doors be locked when leaving houses at night, as quoted below. > **""Away from my house, O evil one. When you leave your houses at night, lock their doors.""**"
"13254","Abu Said al-Khudri, radiya Allahu anhu, in the course of a narration concerning what a pilgrim in Ihram may kill, was asked about the mouse and explained that the Prophet ﷺ once awoke to find it had seized the wick and carried it up to the ceiling."
"13255","Jabir, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked the lamp at the time of dawn."
"13256","Anas ibn Malik, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined the midday nap because the devil does not nap, as quoted below. > **""Take the midday nap, for the devil does not nap.""**"
"13257","Wahb, meaning Ibn Umayya, radiya Allahu anhu, used to say that everything has two ends and a middle: if held by one end the other inclines, but if held by the middle both ends remain balanced. He then gave a counsel concerning how to hold things. > **""Hold to the middle of things.""**"
"13258","Muhammad, meaning Ibn Sirin, reported that they were with Abu Qatada, radiya Allahu anhu, upon the roof of their house when a star shot across and people looked toward it. Abu Qatada then said they had been forbidden to follow it with their gaze. > **""We have been forbidden to follow it with our eyes.""**"
"13259","Ibn Abbas, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade looking at one's shadow in water, as quoted below. > **""Let none of you look at his shadow in water.""**"
"13260","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned against the two marked playing pieces which urge one another on, for they are the gambling of the non-Arabs, as quoted below. > **""Beware these two marked playing pieces which urge one another on, for they are the gambling of the non-Arabs.""**"
"13261","Abdur Rahman, meaning Ibn Abi Said, reported that he heard his father say he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ liken one who plays Nard then rises to pray to one who performs ablution with pus and swine blood then rises to pray, with the addition in another wording that his prayer is not accepted, as quoted below. > **""The likeness of one who plays Nard then rises to pray is as one who performs ablution with pus and swine blood then rises to pray. His prayer is not accepted.""**"
"13262","Ibn Umar, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the player of Nard to one who dips his hand in swine flesh, and its onlooker to one who dips his hand in swine blood, as quoted below. > **""The player of Nard is as one who dips his hand in swine flesh, and the one who looks at it is as one who dips his hand in swine blood.""**"
"13263","Ibn Umar, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that a Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and said he had seen in a dream the previous night that every servant who testifies that none is worthy of worship but Allah and that Muhammad is His Messenger is raised a degree in Paradise, except the companions of al-Shah, meaning chess."
"13264","Umar ibn al-Khattab, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Prophet ﷺ heard a man say to another, come and gamble with me, so he commanded him to give charity."
"13265","Abu Musa al-Ashari, radiya Allahu anhu, reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he used to say to avoid the marked knucklebones which are urged on, for they are from gambling, as quoted below. > **""Avoid these marked knucklebones which are urged on, for they are from gambling.""**"
"13266","Jubayr ibn Mutim, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade saying my soul has become wicked and instructed saying instead that it is sluggish, the narrator noting that laqs means nausea as explained by the author of al-Nihaya, as quoted below. > **""Let none of you say, my soul has become wicked, but let him say, it has become sluggish.""**"
"13267","Abu Umama, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked seeing a man loud-voiced and harsh, and loved to see him soft-voiced."
"13268","Abu Ayyub, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about whistling and explained it as twisting of the cheek, as quoted below. > **""Twisting of the cheek.""**"
"13269","Saad ibn Abi Waqqas, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instruct that spittle be concealed so it does not touch the skin or clothing of a believer, as quoted below. > **""When one of you spits, let him conceal his spittle, lest it touch the skin or clothing of a believer.""**"
"13270","Tariq ibn Abdillah al-Muharibi, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told him not to spit to his right when wishing to spit, but to his left if empty, and if not empty then beneath his foot, as quoted below. > **""When you wish to spit, do not spit to your right, but to your left if it is empty, and if it is not empty then beneath your foot.""**"
"13271","Al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that he left Madina with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who turned toward it and declared that Allah had cleared this peninsula of polytheism but feared the stars would misguide them. Asked how, he explained that rain would fall and they would say they were rained upon by such and such a star, as quoted below. > **""Allah has cleared this peninsula of polytheism, but I fear the stars will misguide them. Rain will fall and they will say, we were rained upon by such and such a star.""**"
"13272","Ibn Masud, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Hudaybiyya when heavy rain fell that night. In the morning the Prophet ﷺ thrice asked whether they knew what their Lord had said, they replied Allah and His Messenger know best, and he conveyed that whoever says they were rained upon by such a star disbelieves in Allah and believes in the star, while whoever says Allah gave them drink believes in Allah and disbelieves in the star, as quoted below. > **""Do you know what your Lord has said? Your Lord has said: whoever says, we were rained upon by such and such a star, has disbelieved in Me and believed in that star, and whoever says, Allah has given us drink, has believed in Me and disbelieved in that star.""**"
"13273","Ali ibn Abi Talib, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that women have no share in the middle of the road, should seek its edges, and should not draw attention while walking, as quoted below. > **""Women have no share in the middle of the road. Let them seek its edges and not draw attention.""**"
"13274","Umar ibn Himas, radiya Allahu anhu, who had companionship, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that women have no right to the middle of the road, as quoted below. > **""Women have no right to the middle of the road.""**"
"13275","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud, radiya Allahu anhu, said he would rather be jostled by a tar-smeared camel than be jostled by a perfumed woman."
"13276","Aisha, radiya Allahu anha, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the cutting down of swings."
"13277","Ali, meaning Ibn Abi Talib, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him to go out and announce among the people the curse upon the cutter of the lote tree, as quoted below. > **""Go out and announce among the people: may Allah curse the cutter of the lote tree.""**"
"13278","Aisha, radiya Allahu anha, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that those who cut the lote tree will be poured into the Fire upon their faces, as quoted below. > **""Those who cut the lote tree will be poured into the Fire upon their faces.""**"
"13279","Abdullah ibn Hubshi, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that whoever cuts a lote tree of the Sanctuary will have his head cast down by Allah into the Fire, as quoted below, noting that Abu Dawud narrated it without the words of the Sanctuary. > **""Whoever cuts a lote tree of the Sanctuary, Allah will cast his head down into the Fire.""**"
"13280","Muawiya, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed those who split speech as poetry is split, as quoted below. > **""May Allah curse those who split speech as poetry is split.""**"
"13281","Umar ibn Saad, meaning Ibn Abi Waqqas, reported that he had a need from his father Saad, and Mujammi reported that Umar prefaced his request with ornate fashionable speech unfamiliar to Saad. When he finished, Saad asked if he had done, then said he had never been further from fulfilling his need nor more disinclined toward him since hearing it, and cited the statement of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ quoted below. In the best chain through Zayd ibn Aslam the wording mentions the Hour not coming until such a people appear. > **""There will be a people who eat with their tongues as cows eat from the earth with their tongues. The Hour will not come until a people appear who eat with their tongues as cows eat with their tongues.""**"
"13282","Abdullah ibn Umar, radiya Allahu anhuma, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Allah hates the eloquent man who curls his tongue as the cow curls its tongue, as quoted below. > **""Allah hates the eloquent among men who curls with his tongue as the cow curls with its tongue.""**"
"13283","Amr ibn al-As, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that eloquence, all of it, is a branch of the devil, as quoted below. > **""Eloquence, all of it, is a branch of the devil.""**"
"13284","Abu Umama, radiya Allahu anhu, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Allah has disliked for them eloquence, all of it, as quoted below. > **""Allah has disliked for you eloquence, all of it.""**"
"13285","With the same chain, it is reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he declared those who speak with twisted mouths and affected grandiloquence to be among the people of the Fire. > **""Indeed those who speak with affected grandiloquence are in the Fire.""**"
"13286","Abdullah bin Masud رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that he said a portion of eloquent speech has the effect of magic. > **""Indeed, part of eloquent speech is magic.""**"
"13287","Abu Bakrah رضي الله عنه reports that they were with the Prophet ﷺ when a delegation of Banu Tamim arrived under Qays bin Asim, Amr bin al-Ahtam, and al-Zibriqan bin Badr رضي الله عنهم. The Prophet ﷺ asked Amr bin al-Ahtam for his opinion of al-Zibriqan bin Badr, and Amr replied that he was obeyed in his councils, strong in resistance, and protective of what lay behind him. Al-Zibriqan then said that Amr knew more good of him than he had described but had envied him. Amr then swore by Allah that al-Zibriqan was lacking in manliness, narrow in hospitality, ignoble through his maternal uncles, and foolish in his offspring, adding that he had not lied in the first description nor in the second, but had spoken the best he knew when pleased and the worst he knew when angry. Upon this the Messenger of Allah ﷺ observed on the power of speech and poetry. > **""Indeed, part of eloquent speech is surely magic, and indeed, part of poetry is surely wisdom.""**"
"13288","Man bin Yazid, or Abu Man رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed the people to gather in their mosques and to notify him when each group had assembled. The narrator says they gathered earliest and went to him, and he walked with them until he sat with them. A speaker among them then spoke with contrived praise of Allah, saying all praise belongs to Allah with none before Him restricting praise and no outlet beyond Him, and words to that effect. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ became angry and rose, so they blamed one another and said Allah had singled them out by his coming to them first among the people, yet such and such had happened. They then came to him and found him in the mosque of such-and-such tribe, spoke with him, and he returned walking with them until he sat in the sitting place where he had been or near it. He then praised Allah in plain words, placing what He willed before Him and what He willed behind Him, and noted the magical effect of eloquence. He then turned to them, commanded them, conversed with them, and taught them. > **""All praise belongs to Allah. Whatever He wills He places before Him, and whatever He wills He places behind Him, and indeed, part of eloquent speech is magic.""**"
"13289","Ata bin Abi Rabah reports that a man began to praise Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما, so Ibn Umar threw dust toward his face, saying he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command such treatment for habitual flatterers. > **""When you see those who habitually praise people, throw dust in their faces.""**"
"13290","Anas bin Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that dust be thrown in the faces of habitual flatterers when they are seen. > **""When you see those who habitually praise people, throw dust in their faces.""**"
"13291","Abdullah bin Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command that dust be thrown in the faces of habitual flatterers when they are seen. > **""When you see those who habitually praise people, throw dust in their faces.""**"
"13292","Tariq bin Shihab reports that Abdullah رضي الله عنه said a man may leave his house with his religion intact and return with nothing of it left, when he goes to a man who controls neither harm nor benefit for him or for himself, yet swears by Allah to him in exaggerated praise and returns having secured some of his need while having earned the anger of Allah against himself. > **""You are such and such, you are such and such.""**"
"13293","Aswad bin Sari رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said he had praised his Lord, Blessed and Exalted, with praises and had also composed praise for him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied that his Lord loves praise and asked him to present what he had used to praise his Lord. The narrator began to recite to him, when a man sought permission to enter, wheat-colored, tall, bald in front, left-handed and strong-bodied. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ signaled him to be silent for the newcomer, gesturing as one does to a cat, as Abu Salamah described. The man entered, spoke for a while, then left. The narrator resumed his recitation, then the man returned and the Prophet ﷺ again signaled him to silence. He asked who the man was for whom he had been silenced, and the Prophet ﷺ identified him as one who does not love falsehood. > **""As for your Lord, Blessed and Exalted, He loves praise. Bring forth that with which you praised your Lord, Blessed and Exalted.""**"
"13294","Abdullah bin Masud رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that none is more jealous than Allah, for which reason He forbade shameful deeds, and none loves praise more than Allah, for which reason He praised Himself, and none loves acceptance of excuses more than Allah, for which reason He accepts excuses from His creation, and none loves glorification more than Allah, for which reason He glorified Himself. > **""There is none more jealous than Allah, and that is why He forbade shameful deeds, and there is none to whom praise is more beloved than Allah, and that is why He praised Himself.""**"
"13295","Ubadah bin al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports that a man of Banu Layth came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked three times whether he might recite to him, then on the fourth occasion recited his praise of him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ responded that if any poet spoke well, then he had spoken well. > **""If any of the poets has spoken well, then you have spoken well.""**"
"13296","Khallad bin al-Saib reports that he visited Usamah bin Zayd رضي الله عنهما, who praised him to his face and explained that he was moved to do so because he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that faith increases in the heart of a believer when he is praised to his face. > **""When the believer is praised to his face, faith grows in his heart.""**"
"13297","Abu Nawfal bin Abi Aqrab reports that Aishah رضي الله عنها was asked whether poetry was listened to in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and she replied that it was the most hateful discourse to him. > **""It was the most hateful discourse to him.""**"
"13298","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that when Iblis was sent down to the earth he asked his Lord, having been sent down and made accursed, to appoint for him a house, a council, food, drink, a caller, a recitation, a book, discourse, and snares. His Lord appointed the bathhouse as his house, the marketplaces and gatherings on roads as his council, whatever Allah's name is not mentioned over as his food, every intoxicant as his drink, wind instruments as his caller, poetry as his recitation, branding as his book, lying as his discourse, and women as his snares. > **""Your house is the bathhouse, your council is the marketplaces and gatherings on roads, your food is that over which the name of Allah is not mentioned, your drink is every intoxicant, your caller is wind instruments, your recitation is poetry, your book is branding, your discourse is lying, and your snares are women.""**"
"13299","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said Imru al-Qays will be the bearer of the banner of the poets toward the Fire. > **""Imru al-Qays is the bearer of the banner of the poets toward the Fire.""**"
"13300","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whenever a man raises his voice in prolonged singing, Allah sends two devils who sit upon his shoulders and strike his chest with their heels until he falls silent, whenever he falls silent. > **""No man raises his voice in prolonged singing except that Allah sends two devils who sit upon his shoulders and strike his chest with their heels until he falls silent.""**"
"13301","Kaysan, the freedman of Muawiyah, reports that Muawiyah رضي الله عنه addressed them and said the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had forbidden seven things and that he too forbade them, among them wailing, singing, images, poetry, gold, khazz, saddles, and silk. > **""The Messenger of Allah forbade seven things, and I forbid you from them: wailing, singing, images, poetry, gold, khazz, saddles, and silk.""**"
"13302","Umar bin al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that it is better for a man that his belly be filled with pus than that it be filled with poetry. > **""That the belly of one of you be filled with pus is better for him than that it be filled with poetry.""**"
"13303","Malik bin Umayr رضي الله عنه reports that he was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the Conquest, Hunayn, and Taif, being a poet, and he asked for a ruling concerning poetry. The Prophet ﷺ replied that it would be better for the space between his throat and his navel to be filled with pus than with poetry. He then asked the Prophet ﷺ to wipe his head, so he placed his hand upon his head, after which he never uttered a line of poetry. Malik lived long until his head and beard turned white, except for the place where the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had rested. > **""That what is between your throat and your navel be filled with pus is better for you than that it be filled with poetry.""**"
"13304","Salman رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it is better for a man that his belly be filled with pus until it satiates him than that it be filled with poetry. > **""That the belly of one of you be filled with pus until it fills him is better for him than that it be filled with poetry.""**"
"13305","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it is better for a man that his belly be filled with pus or blood than that it be filled with poetry by which he was satirized. > **""That the belly of one of you be filled with pus or blood is better for him than that it be filled with poetry by which I was satirized.""**"
"13306","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that it is better for a man that his belly be filled with pus until it is full than that it be filled with poetry. > **""That the belly of one of you be filled with pus until he sees it full is better than that it be filled with poetry.""**"
"13307","Abu al-Darda and Utbah bin Abd رضي الله عنهما both report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said it is better for a man that his belly be filled with pus than that it be filled with poetry. > **""That the belly of one of you be filled with pus is better than that it be filled with poetry.""**"
"13308","Awf bin Malik رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say it is better for a man that his belly be filled with agitated pus from his navel to his throat than that it be filled with poetry. > **""That the belly of one of you be filled from his navel to his throat with agitated pus is better for him than that it be filled with poetry.""**"
"13309","Abu al-Zara reports that Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, said it is better for a man that his belly be filled with pus than that it be filled with poetry. > **""That the belly of a man be filled with pus is better for him than that it be filled with poetry.""**"
"13310","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ said whoever composes affected poetry will have no share with Allah. > **""Whoever fashions speech into poetry will have no share with Allah.""**"
"13311","Abu Barzah رضي الله عنه reports that they were with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey when he heard two men singing, one answering the other with the verse that his companion remained with his bones exposed after war had withdrawn from him only to be hidden and buried. The Prophet ﷺ asked who they were, and when told they were so-and-so, he supplicated against them to be overturned and thrust into the Fire. > **""O Allah, overturn them both with an overturning, and thrust them into the Fire with a thrusting.""**"
"13312","Muttalib bin Rabiah رضي الله عنه reports that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on one of his journeys, he heard the sound of singing and asked what it was. They looked and found one man challenging another in song with the verse that his companion remained with his bones exposed after war had withdrawn from him only to be hidden and buried. The Prophet ﷺ then supplicated that they be overturned in the Fire in tribulation and thrust toward Hell. > **""O Allah, overturn them both in the Fire in tribulation with an overturning, and thrust them to the Fire of Hell with a thrusting.""**"
"13313","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ heard the voices of two men singing the verse that his companion remained with his bones departing after war had withdrawn from him only to be hidden and buried. When he asked about them he was told they were Muawiyah and Amr bin al-As, so he supplicated that they be overturned in tribulation and thrust into the Fire. > **""O Allah, overturn them both in tribulation with an overturning, and thrust them into the Fire with a thrusting.""**"
"13314","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that selling singing women, buying them, trading in them, and their prices are unlawful, and that listening to them is tribulation. > **""It is not lawful to sell singing women, nor to buy them, nor to trade in them, and their prices are unlawful, and listening to them is tribulation.""**"
"13315","Siba bin Thabit رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the people of the pre-Islamic age circumambulating while chanting that today the gazelle flees with wide eyes and strikes the two Marwas. > **""Today the gazelle flees with wide eyes and strikes the two Marwas.""**"
"13316","Shaddad, meaning Ibn Aws رضي الله عنه, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever composes a line of poetry after the last night prayer will have no prayer accepted from him that night. > **""Whoever composes a line of poetry after the last Isha prayer, no prayer will be accepted from him that night.""**"
"13317","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about poetry and replied that it is speech, so its good is good and its ugly is ugly. > **""It is speech, so its good is good and its ugly is ugly.""**"
"13318","Abdullah bin Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said poetry holds the place of speech, so its good is like the good of speech and its ugly is like the ugly of speech. > **""Poetry holds the place of speech, so its good is like the good of speech and its ugly is like the ugly of speech.""**"
"13319","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted them all pre-Islamic poetry except two qasidas of al-Asha, in which, as the narrator claims, he committed polytheism. > **""I permit you all pre-Islamic poetry except the two qasidas of al-Asha.""**"
"13320","In another narration, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ permitted the poetry of the pre-Islamic age except for two qasidas of al-Asha, one concerning the people of Badr and the other concerning Amir and Alqamah. > **""I permit the poetry of the pre-Islamic age except for the two qasidas of al-Asha, one concerning the people of Badr and the other concerning Amir and Alqamah.""**"
"13321","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever introduces satire into Islam, his tongue should be cut off. Abdullah bin Ahmad bin Hanbal explained the meaning as whoever satirizes Islam. > **""Whoever introduces satire into Islam, cut off his tongue.""**"
"13322","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever utters obscene poetry concerning Islam, his tongue is wasted. > **""Whoever utters obscene poetry in Islam, his tongue is wasted.""**"
"13323","Ghudayf bin Abi Ghudayf, a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever introduces satire into Islam, his tongue should be cut off. > **""Whoever introduces satire into Islam, cut off his tongue.""**"
"13324","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said some poetry contains wisdom. > **""Indeed, part of poetry is wisdom.""**"
"13325","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ said some poetry contains wisdom. > **""Indeed, part of poetry is wisdom.""**"
"13326","Abu Bakrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said some poetry contains wisdom. > **""Indeed, part of poetry is wisdom.""**"
"13327","Amr bin Awf رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say some poetry contains wisdom. > **""Indeed, part of poetry is wisdom.""**"
"13328","Anas رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that some eloquent speech is magic and some poetry is wisdom. > **""Indeed, part of eloquent speech is surely magic, and indeed, part of poetry is wisdom.""**"
"13329","Kab bin Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to lampoon the polytheists in verse, for the believer strives with his self and his wealth, and by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, it strikes them as arrows do. > **""Lampoon them in verse, for the believer strives with his self and his wealth, and by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, it is as if you pierce them with arrows.""**"
"13330","In another narration, Kab رضي الله عنه also asked the Prophet ﷺ about what Allah had revealed concerning poetry. He replied that the believer strives with his self and his tongue, then the narrator mentioned the like of the previous report. > **""Indeed, the believer strives with his self and his tongue.""**"
"13331","Ammar bin Yasir رضي الله عنه reports that when the polytheists lampooned them they complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who told them to answer them as they spoke to them. The narrator adds that he saw them teaching it to the slave girls of Madinah. In an addition, a man was reciting satire against Muawiyah and Amr bin al-As while Ammar listened, and Ammar told him to attach it to the two old women. A man objected in astonishment that Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say such a thing, so Ammar told him to sit and listen or leave, then repeated the report of answering the polytheists as they spoke. > **""Say to them as they say to you.""**"
"13332","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Hassan رضي الله عنه to lampoon them, or to answer their lampoon, and supplicated that Allah aid him with the Holy Spirit. > **""Lampoon them, O Allah aid him with the Holy Spirit.""**"
"13333","Kab bin Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed by him while he was reciting his verses announcing to Ghassan across parched lands encircled by pursuit that every dark multitude contends with them for their sanctuary, with helmet crests gleaming like followers. The Prophet ﷺ objected to the wording, so Kab changed the line to contend with them for their religion, and the Prophet ﷺ approved him by name. > **""No, O Kab bin Malik. Yes, O Kab.""**"
"13334","Abdullah bin Rawahah رضي الله عنه reports that while he was passing through the mosque with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among some of his Companions, the people called him, and thinking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was calling him, he came forward. The Prophet ﷺ told him to sit and asked how he composed poetry when he wished to compose. He replied that he reflected then spoke, and the Prophet ﷺ directed him against the polytheists. Having prepared nothing, he first improvised a verse disparaging the tribe as the price of cloaks and as mallets subdued by Mudar. Seeing displeasure on the face of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for making his people the price of cloaks, he reflected and composed verses praising Hashim the bringer of good for the favor Allah bestowed upon them over creation, affirming his insight into their good contrary to what others saw, and that if they sought aid from others in grave matters they would find neither shelter nor help, asking Allah to make firm what good He had given them as Musa was made firm and with a victory like that granted to the victorious. The Prophet ﷺ then supplicated for his steadfastness. > **""You must take on the polytheists. And you too, may Allah make you steadfast, O Ibn Rawahah.""**"
"13335","Asma bint Abi Bakr رضي الله عنها reports that Zubayr bin al-Awwam رضي الله عنه passed by a council of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ while Hassan bin Thabit رضي الله عنه was reciting his poetry to them and they were not eager for what they heard. Zubayr sat with them and asked why they were not listening attentively to the poetry of Ibn al-Furayiah, when it used to be presented to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who would listen well, reward him generously for it, and not be distracted from it. Hassan then recited verses affirming the Disciple's adherence to the covenant and guidance of the Prophet, his following his path, his alliance with the party of truth, his fame as horseman and hero charging on the marked day, plunging with a white swift horse toward death when war bares its leg, and his noble birth from Safiyyah and Asad in a house of hope. > **""The Disciple remained upon the covenant of the Prophet and his guidance, and speech is measured by deed. He remained upon his path and way, allying with the ally of truth, and truth is most just.""**"
"13336","Humayd bin Thawr al-Hilali رضي الله عنه reports that when he embraced Islam he came to the Prophet ﷺ and recited to him his long qasida describing his heart as aimed at by Sulayma whether by mistake or intent, his bearing of weighty anxiety, his camels and mounts enduring the mirage and thirst like a chief seeking an ambush, no physician availing him, and their striving through hardship until they came to the Chosen Muhammad ﷺ reciting a guiding Book from Allah. > **""My heart has become a target from Sulayma, whether by mistake or by intent, until we came to the Chosen Muhammad, reciting a guiding Book from Allah.""**"
"13337","Amr bin Muslim al-Khuzai reports from his father that he was with the Prophet ﷺ and recited to him the verses of Suwayd bin Amir bin al-Mustaliq enjoining not to feel secure even in the sanctuary since deaths attend every man, to walk one's path unbowed until meeting what destiny apportions, for every companion must part one day and every provision must perish, while good and evil are yoked together and the two new things, day and night, bring them. The Prophet ﷺ said that had that poet reached him he would have embraced Islam, whereupon the narrator's father wept. His son asked why he wept for a polytheist who died in ignorance, and he swore he had never seen a better polytheist than Suwayd. > **""Do not feel secure even if you spend the evening in the sanctuary, for deaths attend the sides of every man. Had he reached me, he would surely have embraced Islam.""**"
"13338","Nabighah رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and recited his verse that they had surpassed mankind in chastity and nobility while hoping for a further triumph above that. The Prophet ﷺ asked where the triumph was, and he answered Paradise. The Prophet ﷺ affirmed that hope if Allah wills, then asked for more, so he recited that there is no good in forbearance without outbursts that guard its clarity from being muddied, nor any good in anger without forbearance to turn the matter rightly when it arises. The Prophet ﷺ praised him and supplicated that Allah never break his mouth. > **""Where is the triumph, O Abu Layla? You have done well, may Allah never break your mouth.""**"
"13339","Dirar bin al-Azwar رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him to extend his hands that he might pledge allegiance to him upon Islam. He then recited his verses that he had abandoned divining arrows, singing girls, and wine for forgiveness and devotion, as well as his wheeling of his branded horse in battle and his urging to fight the polytheists, asking his Lord not to let his bargain be lost since he had sold his family and wealth in exchange. The Prophet ﷺ assured him his bargain had not lost. > **""Your bargain has not lost, O Dirar.""**"
"13340","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ confirmed Umayyah as truthful in part of his poetry. When he recited that a man and a bull are beneath His right foot while an eagle is for the other and a watchful lion, the Prophet ﷺ said he spoke the truth. When he recited that the sun rises at the end of every night, red with its color turning rosy, refusing to rise for them gently except under torment and flogging, the Prophet ﷺ again said he spoke the truth. > **""He spoke the truth.""**"
"13341","Asha al-Mazini رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and recited his verses addressing the Master of men and Judge of the Arabs about meeting injury among injuries, going out seeking food in Rajab only to be betrayed in absence and deserted, with breach of covenant and concealment, and that such women are the worst conqueror for one conquered. The Prophet ﷺ began repeating the last line with him. > **""And they are the worst conqueror for one conquered.""**"
"13342","Tayhan رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ on his march to Khaybar say to Amir bin al-Akwa, whose patronymic al-Akwa belonged to Sinan, to give them some of his chants. He then dismounted and began chanting rajaz for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Take for us from your chants.""**"
"13343","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite verses to one another and laugh while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat smiling with them. > **""The Companions of the Messenger of Allah used to recite verses to one another and laugh while he sat smiling with them.""**"
"13344","Ajjaj reports that he asked Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه what he said about the verses describing two apparitions visiting and stirring sickness, the apparition of Salma and a hidden apparition, standing to show fear of severance with full shank and slender ankle. Abu Hurayrah replied that they used to recite this in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he did not find fault with it. > **""We used to recite this in the time of the Messenger of Allah, and he did not find fault with it.""**"
"13345","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him to visit intermittently so that love would increase. > **""O Abu Hurayrah, visit intermittently and love will increase.""**"
"13346","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to cite verses, including that news will come to you from one you did not provision. > **""And news will come to you from one you did not provision.""**"
"13347","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ awaited news with delay, he would cite the verse of Tarafah that news will come to you from one you did not provision. > **""And news will come to you from one you did not provision.""**"
"13348","Sad رضي الله عنه reports that he mentioned Banu Najiyah in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon either the Messenger of Allah ﷺ or the man said, weep, O eye, for Samah bin Luayy. The Prophet ﷺ, or the man addressing the Prophet ﷺ, completed it with the line that attachment clung to Samah. > **""O eye, weep for Samah bin Luayy. Attachment clung to Samah.""**"
"13349","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ heard their women saying at a wedding that he presented her a ram grazing in the enclosure and that her husband in the gathering knows what tomorrow holds. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ corrected them that none knows what tomorrow holds except Allah, and directed them instead to say that they came to them and they greeted them. > **""None knows what tomorrow holds except Allah. Why did you not say: We came to you, we came to you, so greet us and we greet you.""**"
"13350","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ was on a journey when he heard the voice of a camel-driver chanting, so he said to turn toward him. He asked from which people they were, and they said from Mudar. He said he too was from Mudar. They claimed they were the first to chant huda, and when asked how, they explained that a slave boy of theirs pastured camels, slept, and the camels scattered from him, so his master came and struck his hand, and he began crying out for his hands, whereupon the camels gathered to him. > **""Turn with us toward him. From which people are you? And how did huda begin?""**"
"13351","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Amir bin al-Akwa رضي الله عنه to give them some of his chants, so he recited that by Allah, had it not been for Allah they would not have been guided, nor given charity, nor prayed. > **""By Allah, had it not been for Allah we would not have been guided, nor given charity, nor prayed.""**"
"13352","Nasr bin Dahr al-Aslami reports from his father that the Prophet ﷺ told Amir bin al-Akwa رضي الله عنه to dismount and let them hear some of his chants. He began reciting that O Allah, had it not been for You they would not have been guided, nor given charity, nor prayed, so send down tranquillity upon them and make feet firm when they meet the enemy, for the foe has transgressed against them and when they desire tribulation they refuse. The Prophet ﷺ supplicated for mercy upon him, whereupon a man asked that they be allowed to enjoy Amir or his poetry longer. > **""Dismount and let us hear from your chants. O Allah, have mercy upon him.""**"
"13353","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered for the Makeup Pilgrimage while Abdullah bin Rawahah رضي الله عنه held his stirrup chanting rajaz that the sons of disbelievers should clear his way, for the Most Merciful had revealed in His revelation that the best killing is in His cause. > **""Clear the way, O sons of the disbelievers, for his way. The Most Merciful has revealed in His revelation that the best killing is in His cause.""**"
"13354","Qatadah reports that Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه would sometimes cite a line of poetry concerning the battles of the Arabs."
"13355","Mutarrif reports that he accompanied Imran from Kufah to Basrah, and no day passed but that he recited poetry to them, telling them that they had ample scope in allusive speech to avoid lying. > **""Indeed, for you in allusive speech there is ample scope away from lying.""**"
"13356","Ibn Harimah recited from his uncle Ibrahim bin Ali bin Harimah the verses that whoever does not seek his praise will find his qasidas as musk bladders circulating among the most noble, circulating with one who purchases praise through generosity like the circulation of the daughters of Harith bin Hisham. > **""Whoever does not seek my praise, my qasidas are as musk bladders circulating among the most noble.""**"
"13357","Abu al-Kawsaj, freedman of the family of Abu Farwah, recited the verse asking whether one thought his father on the day he insulted him in the market was Harith bin Hisham. > **""Did you think that your father, on the day you insulted me in the market, was Harith bin Hisham?""**"
"13358","Saib bin Yazid رضي الله عنه reports that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked Aishah رضي الله عنها whether she knew her. She said she did not, O Prophet of Allah. He said she was the singing girl of such-and-such tribe and asked whether she would like her to sing for her. She said yes, so she gave her a dish and the woman sang for her. The Prophet ﷺ then remarked that Satan had blown into her nostrils. > **""O Aishah, do you know this woman? This is the singing girl of such-and-such tribe. Would you like her to sing for you? Satan has blown into her nostrils.""**"
"13359","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that a slave girl was with them singing when the Prophet ﷺ entered while she remained in that state. Umar رضي الله عنه then sought permission and she sprang up silent. The Prophet ﷺ laughed, and Umar asked what made him laugh. When he informed him, Umar said he would not leave until he heard what the Prophet ﷺ heard, so he commanded her and she let him hear it. > **""I will not leave until I hear what you hear.""**"
"13360","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described what he saw on the night he was taken by night to the heavens. When they reached the seventh heaven he looked above himself and beheld thunder, lightning, and thunderbolts. He then came upon people whose bellies were swollen like large houses, with serpents inside them visible from outside their bellies, and he asked Jibril, peace be upon him, who they were. After descending to the lowest heaven he looked beneath himself and perceived wind, voices, and smoke, and again asked Jibril about what he witnessed, receiving explanation concerning devils diverting the eyes of mankind from reflection upon the kingdom of the heavens and the earth. > **""These are the devourers of riba. These are devils who are burned before the eyes of the children of Adam so that they do not reflect upon the kingdom of the heavens and the earth, and were it not for that, they would behold wonders.""**"
"13361","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at the sun at the time of its setting and spoke of its intense heat and of the restraint placed upon it by the command of Allah, without which it would have destroyed what is upon the earth. > **""It is in the intensely hot Fire of Allah. Were it not restrained by the command of Allah, it would have destroyed all that is upon the earth.""**"
"13362","Abu Umamah al-Bahili, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of angels appointed over the sun who cast snow upon it each day, for otherwise it would burn everything it came upon. > **""Nine angels have been entrusted with the sun, casting snow upon it every day. Were it not for that, it would burn everything it came upon.""**"
"13363","Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Prophet ﷺ was asked what the earth rests upon, then what the water rests upon, then what the rock rests upon, and then what the great fish rests upon, and he answered each inquiry in succession, describing the earth upon water, the water upon rock, the rock upon the back of a great fish whose extremities meet the Throne, and the fish upon the shoulder of an angel whose feet are in the air. > **""The earth is upon water. It is upon a rock. It is upon the back of a whale whose two ends meet the Throne. It is upon the shoulder of an angel whose feet are in the air.""**"
"13364","Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the thickness of the earth as a journey of five hundred years, and likewise the distance between the upper earth and the lowest heaven, between each earth, the thickness of each heaven, and the distance from the seventh heaven to the Throne as encompassing all that preceded. > **""The thickness of the earth is a journey of five hundred years, and between the upper earth and the lowest heaven is five hundred years, and the thickness of the second is like that, and between every earth is like that, and between the upper earth and the heaven is five hundred years, and the thickness of the heaven is five hundred years, and between the seventh heaven and the Throne is a journey equal to all of that combined.""**"
"13365","Al-Rabi ibn Anas, may Allah have mercy upon him, states that the lowest heaven is a withheld tablet, the second is rock, the third is iron, the fourth is copper, the fifth is silver, the sixth is gold, and the seventh is ruby."
"13366","Ubaydullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Anas reports that he asked Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from what the sun, the moon, and the stars were created, and Anas informed him of what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had told him concerning their origin from the light of the Throne. > **""They were created from the light of the Throne.""**"
"13367","Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, states that the strongest of the creation of his Lord are ten in succession: the mountains, then iron which cuts the mountains, then fire which consumes iron, then water which extinguishes fire, then the subservient cloud between heaven and earth which carries water, then wind which moves the cloud, then man who shields himself from wind with his hand and goes about his need therein, then intoxication which overcomes man, then sleep which overcomes intoxication, and then anxiety which prevents sleep, so the strongest of the creation of his Lord is anxiety."
"13368","Jabir ibn Abdullah ibn Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, who is not the Ansari, reports that he was once in a caravan belonging to Khadijah, may Allah be pleased with her, with the Prophet ﷺ among them prior to the proclamation of prophethood, and that he declared to him his perception of distinguishing qualities in him, his witness that he was the Prophet who would emerge from Tihamah, his present belief in him, and his promise to come upon hearing of his emergence. He was delayed until the day of the Conquest of Makkah, when he came and was greeted by the Prophet ﷺ. He then excused his delay, affirming that he had remained a believer in him, without denying his pledge or breaking his covenant, believing in the Quran and rejecting idols, but that successive years of severe drought had afflicted them after him, wasting marrow and emaciating mounts, drying up milk, spreading distress, breaking fortune, sweeping cultivated lands and offspring, hardening affairs, drying verdant land, and entrenching utter destitution, yet he had come neither breaking his covenant nor denying his pledge. He then asked about the light of day and the darkness of night, the heat of water in winter and its cold in summer, the Secure City, the origin of clouds, the emergence of locusts, thunder and lightning, and the shares of the man and the woman in the child, to which the Prophet ﷺ gave detailed answers. > **""Welcome to the first emigrant. Leave that aside. Allah, blessed and exalted is He, extends His Hand by night for the wrongdoer of the day to repent, and if he repents, Allah turns to him. And He extends His Hand by day for the wrongdoer of the night to repent, and if he repents, Allah turns to him. Truth shall be heavy on account of its weight on the Day of Resurrection, and falsehood shall be light on account of its lightness on the Day of Resurrection. Paradise is enclosed by hardships, and the world is enclosed by desires. As for the darkness of the night and the light of the day: when the sun sets it falls beneath the earth, and the night darkens for that reason, and when morning shines seventy thousand angels hasten toward it while it holds back, disliking to be worshipped besides Allah, until it rises and gives light; the day lengthens with the length of its stay and water grows warm for that reason, and when its stay is short water grows cold for that reason. As for locusts: they are the sneeze of a whale in the sea called al-Abwat, and therein it perishes. As for the origin of clouds: they arise from the direction of the two horizons, and from between the two horizons the easterly and southerly winds bridle them, while the northerly and westerly winds drive them from behind. As for thunder: it is an angel with a striker in his hand, bringing the distant near and sending the near far; when he raises it, lightning flashes, when he rebukes, thunder sounds, and when he strikes, a thunderbolt falls. As for what belongs to the man and what belongs to the woman in the child: bones, veins, and nerves are from the man, and flesh, blood, and hair are from the woman. As for the Secure City: it is Makkah.""**"
"13369","Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, raises the narration to the Prophet ﷺ, reporting that Allah created a rooster whose talons rest upon the seventh earth while its comb lies folded beneath the Throne, with its two wings spanning the horizons. When the last third of the night remains, it beats its wings and proclaims glorification, which all between the east and west hear except the two weighty beings, and domestic roosters beat their wings and crow when they hear it. > **""Glory be to the Sovereign, the Most Holy. Glory be to our Lord, the Sovereign, the Most Holy. There is no god but He. Glorify the Sovereign, the Most Holy.""**"
"13370","In another version of the narration concerning the celestial rooster, the proclamation is conveyed in abbreviated wording commanding glorification of the Sovereign, the Most Holy. > **""Glorify the Sovereign, the Most Holy.""**"
"13371","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that permission was granted to him to inform of a rooster whose feet have pierced the earth while its neck is bent beneath the Throne, declaring the perfection and greatness of its Lord, to which its Lord responds concerning those who swear falsely by Him. > **""Glory be to You, how great You are, our Lord. He who swears falsely by Me has not known that.""**"
"13372","Safwan ibn Assal, may Allah be pleased with him, states that Allah has a rooster beneath the Throne whose wing is in the air and whose talons are in the earth. When the time of predawn and the calls to prayer arrive, it flaps its wings and joins in glorification, whereupon the roosters of the world glorify and answer it with glorification."
"13373","Sabah ibn Ashras reports that Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, was asked about the rising and receding of the tide and explained that an angel is entrusted with the depths of the sea, so that when he places his foot the water surges, and when he lifts it the water recedes."
"13374","Musa ibn Abi Isa reports that Maryam, peace be upon her, once missed Isa, peace be upon them both, and went searching for him. She met a weaver who did not guide her, so she supplicated against him, and such a person remains ever bewildered. She then met a tailor who guided her, and therefore companionship is found with such people and sitting with them is pleasant."
"13375","Yusuf ibn Abi Maryam al-Hanafi reports that while sitting with Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, a man came, greeted him, and asked whether he recognized him as the one who had informed the Prophet ﷺ that he had seen the barrier. Abu Bakrah confirmed his identity, invited him to sit, and asked him to narrate. The man described traveling to a land whose people worked only in iron, resting in a house with his foot upon its wall, hearing at sunset an unprecedented sound that frightened him, being reassured by the master of the house that it was the people returning from the barrier and would not harm him, being asked whether he wished to see it, going forth in the morning to behold bricks of iron each like a rock, resembling patterned cloaks, with nails like tree trunks, then informing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked for its description and identified him as one who had reached the barrier, which Abu Bakrah affirmed as truthful. > **""Describe it to me. Whoever wishes to look at a man who has reached the barrier, let him look at this man.""**"
"13376","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, states that the angels were created from light."
"13377","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, states that no creation of Allah is more numerous than the angels, whom He creates like flies in multitude and then commands to become thousands upon thousands."
"13378","Muslim al-Hajari reports that he asked Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, O Abu Muhammad, from what creation was created, and he replied from water, wind, light, and darkness. He then went to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, asked the same matter, and received the same answer as that given by Abdullah ibn Amr."
"13379","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, states that among those who preceded, a man could reach eighty years of age before attaining puberty."
"13380","Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described how Allah created wind, lodged it in a chamber, and closed a door upon it, such that were the door opened it would sweep away all between heaven and earth, and what reaches people comes only through the crevice of that door, called by people the south wind and with Allah known as al-Adhyab. > **""Allah, blessed and exalted is He, created a wind, lodged it in a chamber, and closed a door upon it. Were that door opened, it would carry away all between the heaven and the earth. What comes to you comes only through the opening of that door. You call it the south wind, and with Allah it is al-Adhyab.""**"
"13381","Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that permission was granted to him to inform of an angel whose feet have pierced the seventh earth while the Throne rests upon his shoulder, declaring the perfection of Allah and questioning where He was and where He shall be. > **""Glory be to You, where were You and where shall You be.""**"
"13382","Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the luminous band in the sky is the vein of a serpent beneath the Throne. > **""The Milky Way in the heaven is the vein of a serpent beneath the Throne.""**"
"13383","Jabir ibn Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed Muadh, informing him that he was sending him to a people of the Scripture, and instructing him how to answer when asked about the luminous band in the heaven, identifying it as the saliva of a serpent beneath the Throne. > **""O Muadh, I am sending you to a people of the Scripture. When you are asked about the Milky Way in the heaven, say: it is the saliva of a serpent beneath the Throne.""**"
"13384","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, states that the Throne is encircled by a serpent and that revelation descends in chains."
"13385","Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, states that a quarter of those among the dark peoples who do not wear garments outnumber all other people."
"13386","Abu Thalabah al-Khushani, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the jinn as being of three types: a type with wings flying in the air, a type in the form of serpents, and a type that alights and departs. > **""The jinn are of three kinds: a kind with wings flying in the air, a kind as serpents, and a kind that settle and travel.""**"
"13387","Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the lifespan of the fly as forty nights and of all flies being in the Fire except the bee. > **""The lifespan of the fly is forty nights, and all flies shall be in the Fire except the bee.""**"
"13388","Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, states that man was called insan only because a covenant was entrusted to him and he forgot."
